/* 統測克漏字短文（綜合測驗）：B1/B2 40 篇（C01–C40），加上 A2 入門 5 篇（C41–C45）、C1 挑戰 15 篇（C46–C50、C56–C65）和 C2 頂尖 15 篇（C51–C55、C66–C75）。每篇 5 格，附中文解析與完整中文翻譯。由第二位老師盲解審核。 */
window.CLOZE_SETS = [{"id": "C01", "title": "Cleaning Time at School", "title_zh": "學校的打掃時間", "level": "B1", "topic": "school life", "passage": "Every morning at 7:40, the students in Class 203 put down their breakfast and pick up brooms. Like most schools in Taiwan, their school does not __1__ on cleaning staff to keep classrooms tidy. Instead, students take turns sweeping floors, wiping windows, and taking out the trash.\n\nAt first, Wei-ting thought the job was a waste of time. She was __2__ to the hallway outside the science lab, which was always covered in mud on rainy days. \"Why should we do this when we could be studying?\" she complained. __3__, after a few weeks, she began to notice a change. Her classmates stopped dropping paper on the floor, because they knew that one of their friends would have to pick it up.\n\nNow Wei-ting sees cleaning time __4__ a lesson in responsibility. \"When you clean a place yourself, you respect it more,\" she says. Her teacher agrees. She believes the habits students build at school will stay with them long after they __5__.", "passage_zh": "每天早上七點四十分，203班的學生放下早餐，拿起掃把。和台灣大多數學校一樣，他們學校並不依賴清潔人員來保持教室整潔，而是由學生輪流掃地、擦窗戶和倒垃圾。\n\n起初，薇婷覺得這份工作是在浪費時間。她被分配到科學實驗室外的走廊，那裡每逢雨天總是滿地泥巴。「我們明明可以拿這些時間來讀書，為什麼要做這種事？」她抱怨道。然而，幾個星期後，她開始注意到一個改變：同學們不再把紙屑丟在地上，因為他們知道，得把它撿起來的會是自己的某個朋友。\n\n現在，薇婷把打掃時間視為一堂培養責任感的課。「當你親手打掃一個地方，你就會更珍惜它。」她說。她的老師也同意，並認為學生在學校養成的習慣，在他們畢業之後很久仍會伴隨著他們。", "blanks": [{"options": ["insist", "rely", "comment", "result"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由後句 Instead, students take turns sweeping floors… 可知，學校「不依賴」清潔人員打掃教室，而是由學生自己來。rely on「依賴」。insist on 堅持；comment on 評論；result in/from 導致／起因於，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["admitted", "addicted", "devoted", "assigned"], "answer": 3, "explain": "由後文薇婷抱怨要打掃實驗室外滿是泥巴的走廊可知，她是「被分配到」這個區域。be assigned to + 地點／工作「被分派到…」。be admitted to 獲准進入；be addicted to 沉迷於；be devoted to 致力於，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["However", "Therefore", "Besides", "For example"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前文她抱怨打掃浪費時間，後句卻說幾週後她開始注意到正面的改變，前後語意轉折，應用 However「然而」。Therefore 因此；Besides 此外；For example 例如，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["for", "like", "as", "to"], "answer": 2, "explain": "固定句型 see A as B「把 A 視為 B」，本句意為「把打掃時間視為一堂責任感的課」。其他介系詞皆無法與 see 構成此用法。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["graduate", "will graduate", "graduated", "were graduating"], "answer": 0, "explain": "after 引導時間副詞子句，主要子句為未來式 will stay 時，副詞子句須以現在式代替未來式，故用 graduate。will graduate 不可用於時間子句；graduated 與 were graduating 為過去時態，與文意不合。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C02", "title": "Studying with a Tomato", "title_zh": "番茄鐘讀書法", "level": "B1", "topic": "study habits", "passage": "Do you find it hard to concentrate when you study? Many students __1__ themselves checking their phones every few minutes. One simple method that may help is the Pomodoro Technique, __2__ was developed by Francesco Cirillo in the late 1980s. He named it after the tomato-shaped kitchen timer he used as a university student, since \"pomodoro\" means \"tomato\" in Italian.\n\nThe idea is easy to follow. You choose one task, set a timer for 25 minutes, and work on nothing else until it rings. __3__, you take a five-minute break to stretch or drink some water. After four rounds, you can rest for a longer period, such as 20 minutes.\n\nThe method works because short sessions feel less __4__ than long hours at a desk. Knowing that a break is coming soon makes it easier to keep going. If you have trouble __5__ focused, why not give your own \"tomato\" a try tonight?", "passage_zh": "你讀書時是不是很難專心？許多學生發現自己每隔幾分鐘就會查看手機。有一個或許有幫助的簡單方法，叫做「番茄鐘工作法」，是由法蘭切斯科‧西里洛在1980年代後期發展出來的。他以自己大學時期使用的番茄造型廚房計時器為它命名，因為 pomodoro 在義大利文中就是「番茄」的意思。\n\n這個方法很容易執行。你選定一項任務，把計時器設定為25分鐘，在鈴響之前只專心做這件事。之後，休息五分鐘，伸展一下或喝點水。完成四輪之後，你可以休息比較長的時間，例如20分鐘。\n\n這個方法之所以有效，是因為比起在書桌前坐上好幾個小時，短時段的學習比較不會讓人感到吃不消。知道休息時間就快到了，會讓人更容易堅持下去。如果你很難保持專注，今晚何不試試你自己的「番茄」呢？", "blanks": [{"options": ["spend", "allow", "find", "prevent"], "answer": 2, "explain": "find oneself + V-ing「發現自己（不自覺地）在做…」，依題意為許多學生「發現自己」每隔幾分鐘就查看手機。spend 須接時間或金錢作受詞；allow oneself 後接 to V；prevent oneself 後接 from V-ing，且語意相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["that", "which", "what", "it"], "answer": 1, "explain": "逗號後為補充說明 the Pomodoro Technique 的非限定關係子句，且子句缺主詞，須用關係代名詞 which。that 不可用於非限定用法；what 本身已含先行詞；it 為代名詞，無法連接兩個子句。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Instead", "Otherwise", "In contrast", "Afterward"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說專心工作25分鐘直到鈴響，本句說接著休息五分鐘，為時間先後順序，應用 Afterward「之後」。Instead 反而；Otherwise 否則；In contrast 相較之下，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["overwhelming", "rewarding", "relaxing", "convenient"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由下句「知道休息快到了，會更容易堅持下去」可知，短時段比長時間坐在書桌前較不「令人吃不消」。overwhelming「令人難以承受的」。rewarding 有收穫的；relaxing 令人放鬆的；convenient 方便的，代入後皆與「這方法有效」的說法矛盾。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["to stay", "staying", "stay", "stayed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "固定句型 have trouble (in) + V-ing「做…有困難」，stay focused 意為「保持專注」，故用動名詞 staying。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C03", "title": "Friends Across the Ocean", "title_zh": "跨越海洋的友誼", "level": "B1", "topic": "friendship", "passage": "When Yu-hsuan's best friend, Amy, moved to Vancouver last summer, both girls cried at the airport. They had been classmates __1__ first grade and had shared almost everything. Yu-hsuan worried that the distance would slowly __2__ them apart.\n\nKeeping in touch was not easy at first. When it is morning in Taipei, it is still the evening before in Vancouver, so finding a good time to talk was a real challenge. __3__ giving up, the two friends came up with a plan. Every Saturday morning in Taiwan, which is Friday evening in Vancouver, they make a video call and talk about their week.\n\nThey also mail each other small gifts. Last month, Amy sent some maple candy, and Yu-hsuan __4__ back a box of pineapple cakes. \"Real friendship is not about how often you see each other,\" Yu-hsuan says. \"It's about __5__ you still care when you're apart.\"", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，語萱最好的朋友艾咪搬到溫哥華時，兩個女孩在機場哭成一團。她們從一年級起就是同班同學，幾乎什麼都和對方分享。語萱擔心距離會慢慢讓她們漸行漸遠。\n\n一開始，保持聯絡並不容易。台北是早上的時候，溫哥華還是前一天的晚上，所以要找到適合聊天的時間是一大挑戰。兩個好朋友沒有放棄，而是想出了一個計畫：每個台灣時間的星期六早上，也就是溫哥華的星期五晚上，她們會視訊通話，聊聊這一週發生的事。\n\n她們也會互相寄小禮物。上個月，艾咪寄來一些楓糖糖果，語萱則回寄了一盒鳳梨酥。「真正的友誼不在於你們多常見面，」語萱說，「而在於分隔兩地時，你是否仍然在乎對方。」", "blanks": [{"options": ["for", "while", "since", "ago"], "answer": 2, "explain": "主要子句為過去完成式 had been classmates，後接時間起點 first grade，應用 since「自從」。for 後接一段時間（如 for ten years）；while 為連接詞，後須接子句，不可直接接名詞 first grade；ago 須置於一段時間之後（如 ten years ago）。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["tell", "drive", "set", "take"], "answer": 1, "explain": "drive… apart「使…疏遠、分開」，句意為語萱擔心距離會慢慢讓兩人疏遠。tell… apart 分辨；set… apart 使與眾不同；take… apart 拆開，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Instead of", "Because of", "In spite of", "In addition to"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由前句「找時間聊天很困難」及後句「兩人想出一個計畫」可知，她們「沒有放棄，而是」想辦法解決。Instead of + V-ing「不…而是…」。Because of 因為；In spite of 儘管；In addition to 除了…之外還，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["mails", "has mailed", "had been mailing", "mailed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "句首 Last month 表過去時間，且與前一子句 Amy sent 以 and 對等連接，應用過去簡單式 mailed。mails 為現在式；has mailed 現在完成式不可與 last month 連用；had been mailing 過去完成進行式不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["that", "what", "whether", "which"], "answer": 2, "explain": "介系詞 about 之後須接名詞（子句）。whether 引導名詞子句，表「是否」，且子句 you still care 結構完整。介系詞後不可直接接 that 子句；what 與 which 須在子句中擔任主詞或受詞，但本子句並不缺。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C04", "title": "Teaching Grandma to Text", "title_zh": "教奶奶傳訊息", "level": "B1", "topic": "family", "passage": "Last winter, my grandmother got her first smartphone. She was excited, but she was also afraid of pressing the wrong button and __1__ something. My parents were busy with work, so I __2__ to teach her.\n\nI thought it would take one afternoon. I was wrong. Grandma kept forgetting how to unlock the screen, and she typed so slowly that one message could take ten minutes. More than once, I felt like giving up. __3__, I remembered how patient she had been when she taught me to ride a bike as a child.\n\nSo I changed my approach. I wrote the steps on a card in big letters and let her practice by __4__ while I watched. Little by little, she improved. Now she sends our family LINE group a \"good morning\" picture every day, usually with flowers and a cheerful greeting. Teaching her taught me something too: patience is a gift that can be __5__ from one generation to the next.", "passage_zh": "去年冬天，我的奶奶有了她的第一支智慧型手機。她很興奮，但也害怕按錯按鈕、把東西弄壞。我爸媽忙於工作，所以我自告奮勇要教她。\n\n我原本以為一個下午就能搞定，結果我錯了。奶奶老是忘記怎麼解鎖螢幕，打字又慢到一則訊息可能要花十分鐘。不只一次，我很想放棄。然而，我想起小時候她教我騎腳踏車時是多麼有耐心。\n\n於是我改變了方法。我把步驟用大字寫在卡片上，讓她自己練習，我在一旁看著。她一點一點地進步了。現在她每天都會在我們家的 LINE 群組傳一張「早安圖」，通常配上花朵和愉快的問候語。教她的過程也讓我學到一件事：耐心是一份可以一代傳一代的禮物。", "blanks": [{"options": ["break", "breaking", "broke", "to break"], "answer": 1, "explain": "介系詞 of 後須接動名詞，且以 and 與前面的 pressing 對等連接，故用 breaking。be afraid of + V-ing「害怕…」。break 為原形；broke 為過去式；to break 為不定詞，皆無法與 pressing 對等。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["refused", "pretended", "volunteered", "forgot"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由「爸媽忙於工作，所以（so）…」及下一段作者實際教奶奶的過程可知，作者「自願」教她。volunteer to V「自願做…」。refused 拒絕；pretended 假裝；forgot 忘記，皆與後文矛盾。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["As a result", "Besides", "Similarly", "However"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說作者好幾次想放棄，後句卻說作者想起奶奶當年教自己騎車時的耐心（因而堅持下去），前後語意轉折，應用 However「然而」。As a result 結果；Besides 此外；Similarly 同樣地，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["herself", "her", "hers", "she"], "answer": 0, "explain": "by oneself「獨自、靠自己」，此處指奶奶自己練習，故用反身代名詞 herself。by her 意為「在她旁邊」或表行為者，不合語意；hers 為所有格代名詞；she 為主格，不可置於介系詞後。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["turned down", "passed down", "put off", "taken over"], "answer": 1, "explain": "pass down「傳承、傳給後代」，與 from one generation to the next「一代傳一代」搭配。turn down 拒絕；put off 延後；take over 接管，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C05", "title": "The Last Leg of the Relay", "title_zh": "大隊接力的最後一棒", "level": "B1", "topic": "school life", "passage": "Every October, our school holds a sports day, and the most exciting event is the class relay race. This year, my class had a problem: our fastest runner, Kai, __1__ his ankle during basketball practice a week before the race. Everyone thought we had no chance.\n\nOur teacher asked for a volunteer to take his place, and to everyone's surprise, quiet Chen-yu raised her hand. She was not known for being fast, __2__ she promised to do her best. Every day after school, she practiced passing the baton with the team. Kai, __3__ could only sit and watch, gave her tips on starting quickly.\n\nOn the day of the race, we were in fourth place when the baton reached Chen-yu, our last runner. She ran harder than we had ever seen anyone run and passed two runners before the finish line. We finished second, our best result ever. The experience taught us never to __4__ people by first impressions. Sometimes the people we least expect are the ones who make the biggest __5__.", "passage_zh": "每年十月，我們學校都會舉辦運動會，其中最刺激的項目就是班際大隊接力。今年我們班遇到了一個難題：跑得最快的阿凱在比賽前一週打籃球時扭傷了腳踝。大家都認為我們沒希望了。\n\n老師徵求自願者代替他上場，出乎大家意料的是，文靜的晨語舉起了手。她並不是以跑得快出名，但她承諾會盡全力。每天放學後，她都和隊友一起練習交接棒。只能坐在一旁看的阿凱，則教她如何快速起跑。\n\n比賽當天，棒子交到最後一棒晨語手上時，我們是第四名。她跑得比我們見過的任何人都還要拚命，在終點線前超越了兩名跑者。我們最後拿下第二名，是有史以來最好的成績。這次經驗教會我們，絕不要憑第一印象評斷別人。有時候，那些我們最意想不到的人，正是最能帶來改變的人。", "blanks": [{"options": ["spread", "splashed", "sprained", "sprayed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由後句「大家都認為我們沒希望了」及老師要找人代替他可知，阿凱受傷了。sprain one's ankle「扭傷腳踝」為常見搭配。spread 散布；splash 濺起；spray 噴灑，字形相近但語意不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["but", "for", "or", "unless"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句「她並不以跑得快出名」與後句「她承諾會盡全力」語意相反，應用對等連接詞 but「但是」。for 表「因為」，前後並無因果關係；or 或者；unless 除非，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["that", "whom", "which", "who"], "answer": 3, "explain": "逗號後為補充說明人物 Kai 的非限定關係子句，且子句缺主詞，應用主格關係代名詞 who。that 不可用於非限定子句；whom 為受格，不可當主詞；which 用於事物。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["blame", "judge", "trust", "follow"], "answer": 1, "explain": "judge people by first impressions「憑第一印象評斷人」，呼應文靜、不以跑得快出名而不被看好的晨語，最後卻表現亮眼。blame 責怪；trust 信任；follow 跟隨，皆無法與 by first impressions 搭配表達此意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["mistake", "noise", "decision", "difference"], "answer": 3, "explain": "make a difference「產生影響、帶來改變」為固定搭配，make the biggest difference 指帶來最大的改變，呼應晨語幫助全班拿到最佳成績。make a mistake 犯錯；make noise 製造噪音；make a decision 做決定，皆不合文意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C06", "title": "Pen or Keyboard?", "title_zh": "手寫還是打字？", "level": "B1", "topic": "study habits", "passage": "Many students today take notes on laptops __1__ typing is faster than writing by hand. However, being faster does not always mean learning more. In a well-known 2014 study, researchers Pam Mueller and Daniel Oppenheimer compared students who typed their notes with those who wrote them by hand. Students using laptops tended to copy down the speaker's words exactly, while those using pens had to __2__ what was important.\n\nBecause handwriting is slower, it forces you to listen carefully, think, and put ideas into your own words. This extra effort helps the information stay in your memory. In the study, students who had written notes by hand did better on questions __3__ asked them to understand ideas, not just remember facts.\n\nThis does not mean you should throw away your laptop. Digital notes are easy to search and share. The key is to stay __4__ with what you hear instead of acting like a recording machine. Whether you use a pen or a keyboard, try to __5__ each main point in a sentence of your own.", "passage_zh": "現今許多學生用筆電做筆記，因為打字比手寫快。然而，速度快並不一定代表學得多。在2014年一項知名的研究中，研究人員帕姆‧穆勒和丹尼爾‧奧本海默比較了用電腦打筆記和用手寫筆記的學生。使用筆電的學生往往會一字不漏地抄下講者的話，而使用紙筆的學生則必須判斷哪些內容是重要的。\n\n由於手寫速度較慢，它迫使你仔細聆聽、思考，並用自己的話來表達概念。這份額外的努力有助於讓資訊留在記憶中。在這項研究裡，用手寫筆記的學生在要求理解概念、而不只是記住事實的題目上表現得較好。\n\n這並不表示你應該把筆電丟掉。數位筆記很容易搜尋和分享。關鍵在於要專注投入於你所聽到的內容，而不是像一台錄音機一樣。無論你用的是筆還是鍵盤，都試著用你自己的一句話來總結每個重點。", "blanks": [{"options": ["unless", "because", "although", "until"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前後兩部分為因果關係：學生用筆電做筆記，「因為」打字比手寫快，應用 because。unless 除非；although 雖然；until 直到，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["ignore", "forget", "decide", "pretend"], "answer": 2, "explain": "while 表對比：筆電使用者逐字照抄，手寫者則因寫得慢無法全抄，必須「判斷」哪些內容重要。decide + 名詞子句「決定、判斷…」。ignore 忽略、forget 忘記，與文意相反；pretend 後不接 what 子句。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["that", "who", "where", "what"], "answer": 0, "explain": "先行詞 questions 為事物，後面的子句缺主詞，應用關係代名詞 that（或 which）。who 用於人；where 為關係副詞，後須接完整子句；what 本身含先行詞，不可接在名詞之後。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["bored", "distracted", "engaged", "confused"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由後半句 instead of acting like a recording machine「而不是像錄音機一樣只會照錄」可知，關鍵在於保持「投入」。stay engaged with「專注投入於…」。bored 無聊的；distracted 分心的；confused 困惑的，皆與文意不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["look up", "give up", "make up", "sum up"], "answer": 3, "explain": "sum up「總結、概括」，in a sentence of your own 指用自己的一句話總結每個重點，呼應前文 put ideas into your own words。look up 查閱；give up 放棄；make up 編造，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C07", "title": "The Hardest Word", "title_zh": "最難說出口的話", "level": "B1", "topic": "friendship", "passage": "Ming and Jason had been close friends for years, but last month they had a big argument. Jason had promised to help Ming prepare for a speech contest, but he forgot and went to a movie __1__. Ming was so angry that he stopped __2__ to Jason, even at lunch.\n\nFor two weeks, they ignored each other. Jason kept telling himself that it was just one small mistake. Deep down, __3__, he knew that he had broken a promise. Finally, he wrote Ming a short note. He did not make any excuses; he simply admitted that he was wrong. The next day, Ming walked over to Jason's desk and smiled. \"Apology __4__,\" he said.\n\nSaying \"I'm sorry\" can be hard because it means admitting our faults. Yet a sincere apology can repair a friendship, __5__ silence only makes the hurt grow deeper.", "passage_zh": "阿明和傑森多年來一直是好朋友，但上個月他們大吵了一架。傑森原本答應要幫阿明準備演講比賽，但他忘了，反而跑去看電影。阿明氣到不再跟傑森說話，連午餐時間也一樣。\n\n整整兩個星期，他們對彼此視而不見。傑森一直告訴自己，那只不過是一個小錯誤。不過在內心深處，他知道自己違背了承諾。最後，他寫了一張簡短的紙條給阿明。他沒有找任何藉口，只是坦承自己錯了。隔天，阿明走到傑森的座位旁，笑著說：「我接受你的道歉。」\n\n說「對不起」可能很難，因為那代表要承認自己的過錯。然而，真誠的道歉能夠修補友誼，而沉默只會讓傷痛越來越深。", "blanks": [{"options": ["either", "yet", "neither", "instead"], "answer": 3, "explain": "由前文「傑森答應幫阿明準備比賽，但忘了」可知，他「反而」跑去看電影。instead 作副詞置於句尾，表「反而、取而代之」。either 用於否定句句尾表「也（不）」；yet 用於否定句或疑問句表「尚未」；neither 為否定詞，皆不合語法或語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["to talk", "talk", "talking", "talked"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由 so angry that「氣到…」可知，阿明「不再」跟傑森說話。stop + V-ing 表「停止做某事」；stop + to V 表「停下來（手邊的事）去做另一件事」，與文意相反；talk、talked 不可接在 stopped 之後。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["though", "besides", "otherwise", "instead"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句傑森告訴自己那只是小錯，本句卻說他內心深處知道自己違背了承諾，語意轉折，應用 though「不過、然而」，作副詞時常置於句中並以逗號隔開。besides 此外；otherwise 否則；instead 反而，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["accept", "acceptance", "accepted", "accepting"], "answer": 2, "explain": "Apology accepted. 為慣用的省略句，完整句為 Your apology is accepted.「你的道歉被接受了」，道歉是「被」接受，故用過去分詞 accepted 表被動。accept 為原形動詞；acceptance 為名詞；accepting 為現在分詞，表主動。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["because", "while", "unless", "so that"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前句「真誠的道歉能修補友誼」與後句「沉默只會讓傷痛加深」形成對比，應用 while「而；然而」。because 因為；unless 除非；so that 以便，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C08", "title": "A Mid-Autumn Barbecue", "title_zh": "中秋烤肉夜", "level": "B1", "topic": "family", "passage": "In Taiwan, the Mid-Autumn Festival falls on the 15th day of the eighth month of the lunar calendar. Besides eating mooncakes and pomelos, many families __1__ together to have a barbecue outdoors. For my family, it is the only night of the year __2__ all of my relatives are in the same place.\n\nThis year, my uncle brought a huge bag of charcoal, and my aunt prepared corn, sausages, and squid. My cousins and I were __3__ for grilling the food. It sounded easy, but the fire was hard to control, and we burned the first batch of sausages within ten minutes. Grandpa laughed and told us to wait until the flames had died down and the charcoal was __4__ red.\n\nLater, we sat on the balcony, __5__ up at the full moon and listening to Grandpa's stories about his childhood. The food was a little burned, but nobody seemed to mind. What mattered was that we were together.", "passage_zh": "在台灣，中秋節是在農曆八月十五日。除了吃月餅和柚子之外，許多家庭會聚在一起到戶外烤肉。對我們家來說，這是一年之中唯一一個所有親戚都聚在同一個地方的夜晚。\n\n今年，叔叔帶來一大袋木炭，阿姨則準備了玉米、香腸和花枝。我和堂表兄弟姊妹負責烤食物。聽起來很簡單，但火很難控制，不到十分鐘，我們就把第一批香腸烤焦了。爺爺笑著告訴我們，要等到火焰變小、木炭燒得通紅才行。\n\n後來，我們坐在陽台上，抬頭望著滿月，聽爺爺講他小時候的故事。食物雖然有點焦，但似乎沒有人在意。重要的是我們聚在一起。", "blanks": [{"options": ["put", "get", "take", "bring"], "answer": 1, "explain": "get together「聚在一起」為不及物片語動詞，符合句中 together 後無受詞的結構。put together 組合、拼湊與 bring together 使聚集，皆為及物用法，須接受詞；take together 沒有「聚在一起」的意思。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["which", "where", "what", "when"], "answer": 3, "explain": "先行詞 the only night of the year 表時間，後面子句 all of my relatives are in the same place 結構完整，應用關係副詞 when。which 須在子句中當主詞或受詞；where 用於地點；what 本身含先行詞。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["responsible", "famous", "anxious", "thankful"], "answer": 0, "explain": "be responsible for + V-ing「負責做…」，依題意，我和堂表兄弟姊妹負責烤食物。famous for 以…出名，與後文「把香腸烤焦」矛盾；anxious for 渴望得到；thankful for 感謝，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["melting", "glowing", "freezing", "shaking"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由前文「等火焰變小」可知，此時木炭應「燒得通紅」，才適合烤東西。glow red「發出紅光、燒得通紅」。melting 融化；freezing 結冰；shaking 搖晃，皆不合常理。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["looked", "to look", "look", "looking"], "answer": 3, "explain": "本句主要動詞為 sat，逗號後以分詞構句表伴隨動作，並用 and 與 listening 對等連接，故用現在分詞 looking。looked 與 listening 形式不對等；to look 表目的，也無法與 listening 對稱；look 為原形動詞，不可置於此處。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C09", "title": "A Class Trip to the Palace Museum", "title_zh": "故宮校外教學", "level": "B1", "topic": "school life", "passage": "Last Friday, our history class took a field trip to the National Palace Museum in Taipei. I had expected the trip to be boring, but it turned out to be one of the best days of the semester.\n\nOur guide, a retired teacher, led us __1__ rooms full of ancient paintings, bronze vessels, and porcelain. The most popular piece was the Jadeite Cabbage, a small carving that looks __2__ real that you might want to cook it. If you look closely, you can see two tiny insects __3__ on its leaves. The artist cleverly used the natural colors of the stone: white for the stem and green for the leaves.\n\nBefore we left, our teacher gave us a task. Each of us had to choose one object and explain __4__ we found it interesting. I chose a curio box, a small wooden case with secret drawers full of tiny treasures. __5__ that trip, I have become much more interested in history, and I even plan to visit again with my family.", "passage_zh": "上週五，我們的歷史課到台北的國立故宮博物院校外教學。我原本以為這趟行程會很無聊，結果卻成了這學期最棒的日子之一。\n\n我們的導覽員是一位退休老師，帶領我們穿過一間間陳列著古代畫作、青銅器和瓷器的展間。最受歡迎的展品是翠玉白菜，這件小巧的雕刻看起來逼真到讓人想把它拿來煮。如果仔細看，還能看到兩隻小昆蟲停在葉子上。藝術家巧妙地運用了玉石的天然顏色：白色的部分做菜梗，綠色的部分做菜葉。\n\n離開前，老師給了我們一項任務：每個人都要選一件文物，並說明自己為什麼覺得它有趣。我選了一件多寶格，那是一個小巧的木盒，裡面的暗格裝滿了迷你珍寶。自從那次參訪之後，我對歷史變得更有興趣，甚至打算和家人再去一次。", "blanks": [{"options": ["through", "above", "beyond", "without"], "answer": 0, "explain": "lead sb through + 地點「帶領某人穿過…」，表示導覽員帶學生走過一間間展間。above 在…之上；beyond 超出、在…之外；without 沒有，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["such", "too", "so", "very"], "answer": 2, "explain": "句型 so + 形容詞 + that…「如此…以致於…」，real 為形容詞，故用 so。such 後須接名詞（如 such a real-looking cabbage that…）；too + 形容詞常接 to V，不接表結果的 that 子句；very 不可與 that 子句連用表結果。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["rests", "resting", "to rest", "rested"], "answer": 1, "explain": "感官動詞 see + 受詞 + V-ing，表示看見受詞「正在」做某動作，此處指看到兩隻小昆蟲停在葉子上，故用現在分詞 resting。rests 為限定動詞，不可接在受詞後；主動語態的感官動詞後不接 to V；rested 表被動，不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["what", "which", "whom", "why"], "answer": 3, "explain": "explain 後接名詞子句當受詞，子句 we found it interesting 結構完整，且依文意是說明「為什麼」覺得它有趣，應用 why。what、which、whom 都須在子句中擔任主詞或受詞，但 found 已有受詞 it。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Since", "During", "Until", "Before"], "answer": 0, "explain": "主要子句為現在完成式 I have become…，表示從過去某時間點持續到現在的變化，應用 since「自從」。During 在…期間；Until 直到；Before 在…之前，皆無法與現在完成式搭配表達此意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C10", "title": "Sleep: The Secret Study Tool", "title_zh": "睡眠：讀書的祕密武器", "level": "B1", "topic": "study habits", "passage": "During exam season, many students stay up late, believing that every extra hour of studying will __1__ to a higher score. However, scientists have found that sleep plays an important role in learning. While we sleep, the brain sorts through the information we took in during the day and stores the important parts in long-term memory. __2__, cutting back on sleep can make it harder to remember what you studied.\n\nExperts recommend that teenagers __3__ eight to ten hours of sleep each night, but many get far less. A lack of sleep can also leave you tired, moody, and unable to focus in class the next day.\n\nSo, what can you do? First, try to go to bed at the same time every night, even on weekends. Second, put your phone away at least half an hour before bed, __4__ the light from screens can keep you awake. Finally, review your notes in the evening before a test and then get a good night's rest. You may be surprised at __5__ much more you remember in the morning.", "passage_zh": "考試期間，許多學生熬夜，認為每多讀一個小時的書，就能換來更高的分數。然而，科學家發現睡眠在學習中扮演著重要的角色。我們睡覺時，大腦會整理白天吸收的資訊，並把重要的部分存入長期記憶。因此，減少睡眠會讓你更難記住讀過的內容。\n\n專家建議青少年每晚睡八到十個小時，但許多人的睡眠時間遠遠不足。睡眠不足也會讓你隔天感到疲倦、情緒不穩，上課時無法專心。\n\n那麼，你可以怎麼做呢？首先，試著每晚在同一時間上床睡覺，即使週末也一樣。其次，睡前至少半小時把手機收起來，因為螢幕的光線會讓你難以入睡。最後，考試前一晚複習筆記，然後好好睡一覺。你可能會驚訝地發現，早上醒來時自己多記住了好多東西。", "blanks": [{"options": ["belong", "lead", "object", "react"], "answer": 1, "explain": "lead to「導致、帶來」，句意為學生認為每多讀一小時就能「帶來」更高的分數。belong to 屬於；object to 反對；react to 對…做出反應，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["In contrast", "Otherwise", "As a result", "For example"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前句說明睡覺時大腦會把重要資訊存入長期記憶，本句說減少睡眠會讓人更難記住讀過的內容，為前因後果，應用 As a result「因此、結果」。In contrast 相較之下；Otherwise 否則；For example 例如，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["get", "getting", "got", "gets"], "answer": 0, "explain": "recommend 表「建議」時，後接 that 子句，子句動詞用（should +）原形動詞，故用 get。getting 為分詞，無法當子句的主要動詞；got 為過去式；gets 為第三人稱單數，與複數主詞 teenagers 不一致。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["unless", "although", "since", "until"], "answer": 2, "explain": "後半句「螢幕的光線會讓你保持清醒、難以入睡」是前半句「睡前半小時收起手機」的原因，應用 since「因為、既然」。unless 除非；although 雖然；until 直到，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["that", "so", "what", "how"], "answer": 3, "explain": "be surprised at + 名詞子句，how much more you remember 表「你多記住了多少」，how 用來修飾 much more。介系詞 at 後不可接 that 子句；so 無法引導名詞子句；what 不能修飾 much more。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C11", "title": "The Power of a Good Night's Sleep", "title_zh": "好眠的力量", "level": "B1", "topic": "health", "passage": "Many high school students stay up late to study or chat online, and they often feel tired the next morning. Doctors say that teenagers need about eight to ten hours of sleep each night. __1__, most of them get much less than that.\n\nSleep is not just a time for the body to rest. While we are sleeping, the brain __2__ the information we learned during the day, which helps us remember it better. Students who do not get enough sleep may find it hard to __3__ in class, and they are also more likely to catch colds.\n\nTo sleep better, experts suggest putting phones away at least an hour before bed. The blue light __4__ by screens can trick the brain into thinking it is still daytime. It is also wise to go to bed and wake up at the same time every day __5__ your body clock can stay steady. Small changes like these can make a big difference to both your health and your grades.", "passage_zh": "許多高中生熬夜念書或上網聊天，隔天早上常常覺得很累。醫生表示，青少年每晚需要大約八到十小時的睡眠。然而，他們大多數人的睡眠時間遠少於此。\n\n睡眠不只是讓身體休息的時間。我們睡覺時，大腦會整理白天學到的資訊，幫助我們記得更牢。睡眠不足的學生上課時可能難以專心，也比較容易感冒。\n\n為了睡得更好，專家建議至少在睡前一小時把手機收起來。螢幕發出的藍光會讓大腦誤以為現在還是白天。每天在同一時間上床和起床也是明智的做法，好讓你的生理時鐘保持穩定。像這樣的小改變，對你的健康和成績都會有很大的影響。", "blanks": [{"options": ["Therefore", "However", "For example", "Besides"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前句說青少年每晚需要八到十小時的睡眠，後句說大多數人睡得遠少於此，前後語意相反，須用表示轉折的 However（然而）。Therefore 因此、For example 例如、Besides 此外，皆不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["ignores", "borrows", "organizes", "replaces"], "answer": 2, "explain": "我們睡覺時，大腦會「整理」白天學到的資訊，後面又說這能幫助我們記得更牢，應選 organizes（整理、組織）。ignores 忽略、borrows 借用、replaces 取代，語意皆不通。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["consist", "complain", "conclude", "concentrate"], "answer": 3, "explain": "find it hard to + 原形動詞 表「覺得難以……」。睡眠不足的學生上課時難以「專心」，concentrate 最合語意。consist 須接 of（由……組成）、complain 抱怨、conclude 下結論，皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["gives off", "giving off", "given off", "to give off"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格修飾名詞 The blue light，藍光是「被」螢幕發出的，且後面有 by screens，應用過去分詞表被動：the blue light (which is) given off by screens。give off 意為「發出（光、熱、氣味）」。gives off 為動詞，但句中已有主要動詞 can trick；giving off 表主動，不合「被螢幕發出」之意；to give off 表目的，語意不通。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["so that", "even though", "as if", "unless"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格引導表目的的副詞子句：每天固定時間睡覺和起床，「以便」生理時鐘保持穩定，so that + 主詞 + can + 原形動詞 表目的。even though 雖然、as if 彷彿、unless 除非，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C12", "title": "Clean Hands, Healthy Life", "title_zh": "乾淨雙手，健康生活", "level": "B1", "topic": "health", "passage": "Washing your hands may seem like a small thing, but it is one of the best ways to stop germs from __1__. Every day, our hands touch doors, phones, and desks, and they pick up germs that we cannot see. If we then touch our eyes, nose, or mouth, those germs can easily get into our bodies.\n\nMany people think a quick rinse with water is enough. __2__, health experts say we should use soap and scrub for at least 20 seconds. That is about the time it takes to hum the \"Happy Birthday\" song twice. Soap works well because it breaks down the oily layer __3__ protects many kinds of germs.\n\nIt is especially important to wash your hands before you eat and after you use the restroom. When soap and water are not __4__, hand sanitizer with at least 60 percent alcohol is a good choice. Teachers at Hsin-Ming Junior High, for example, put sanitizer in every classroom last year, and fewer students __5__ sick with the flu that winter.", "passage_zh": "洗手看似小事，卻是阻止病菌傳播的最佳方法之一。我們的手每天都會碰觸門、手機和桌子，沾上肉眼看不見的病菌。如果接著再摸眼睛、鼻子或嘴巴，這些病菌就很容易進入我們的身體。\n\n許多人認為用水快速沖一下就夠了。然而，衛生專家表示，我們應該使用肥皂並搓洗至少二十秒。這大約是哼兩遍〈生日快樂歌〉的時間。肥皂之所以有效，是因為它能破壞保護許多種病菌的油脂層。\n\n在吃東西前和上完廁所後洗手尤其重要。沒有肥皂和水可用時，酒精濃度至少百分之六十的乾洗手是不錯的選擇。例如，新明國中的老師們去年在每間教室都放了乾洗手，結果那年冬天因流感生病的學生變少了。", "blanks": [{"options": ["spread", "to spread", "spreads", "spreading"], "answer": 3, "explain": "stop + 受詞 + from + V-ing 為固定用法，表「阻止……做某事」。介系詞 from 後須接動名詞，故用 spreading。spread、to spread、spreads 皆不能放在介系詞 from 之後。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["However", "Similarly", "As a result", "For instance"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說許多人認為用水沖一下就夠了，後句說專家建議要用肥皂搓洗至少二十秒，前後語意對立，應用 However（然而）。Similarly 同樣地、As a result 結果、For instance 例如，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["who", "that", "where", "what"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格引導形容詞子句，修飾先行詞 the oily layer（油脂層，指物），且在子句中當主詞，應用關係代名詞 that（或 which）。who 只用於人；where 為關係副詞，後須接完整子句；what 前面不可有先行詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["valuable", "comfortable", "available", "responsible"], "answer": 2, "explain": "「沒有肥皂和水『可用』時」，乾洗手是好選擇，available（可取得的、可用的）最合語意。valuable 有價值的、comfortable 舒適的、responsible 負責的，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["get", "got", "getting", "have gotten"], "answer": 1, "explain": "句中 last year 與 that winter 都是明確的過去時間，應用過去式 got（get sick 生病）。have gotten 為現在完成式，不可與明確的過去時間連用；get 為現在式、getting 不能單獨當動詞。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C13", "title": "Bubble Tea: Sweet but Smart", "title_zh": "珍珠奶茶：甜也要喝得聰明", "level": "B1", "topic": "food", "passage": "Bubble tea was first created in Taiwan in the 1980s, and today it is __1__ all over the world, from Tokyo to New York. The chewy \"pearls\" in the drink are made from tapioca, a kind of starch that comes from the root of the cassava plant.\n\nAlthough bubble tea is delicious, it is not always a healthy choice. A large cup with full sugar can __2__ more than 50 grams of sugar, which is more than many people should have in a whole day. Drinking too much sugar can __3__ to weight gain and tooth problems.\n\nThis does not mean you have to give up your favorite drink. __4__, you can simply make smarter choices. Many shops now let customers choose how sweet their drink is, so ordering \"half sugar\" or \"no sugar\" is an easy first step. You can also pick a smaller cup or ask for fresh milk __5__ non-dairy creamer. That way, you can still enjoy a cup now and then without feeling guilty.", "passage_zh": "珍珠奶茶在1980年代首次於台灣誕生，如今從東京到紐約，在世界各地都很受歡迎。飲料裡有嚼勁的「珍珠」是用樹薯粉做的，這是一種取自樹薯根部的澱粉。\n\n珍珠奶茶雖然好喝，卻不一定是健康的選擇。一大杯全糖的珍奶可能含有超過五十克的糖，比許多人一整天該攝取的量還多。攝取過多的糖可能導致體重增加和牙齒問題。\n\n這不代表你必須放棄最愛的飲料。相反地，你只要做出更聰明的選擇就好。現在許多店家讓顧客選擇飲料的甜度，所以點「半糖」或「無糖」是簡單的第一步。你也可以選小杯的，或是要求加鮮奶而不是奶精。如此一來，你仍然可以偶爾享受一杯，而不會有罪惡感。", "blanks": [{"options": ["similar", "regular", "particular", "popular"], "answer": 3, "explain": "珍珠奶茶從台灣紅到東京、紐約等世界各地，表示它「受歡迎的」，popular 最合語意。similar 相似的、regular 規律的、particular 特別的，皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["consider", "continue", "contain", "contact"], "answer": 2, "explain": "主詞為 A large cup（一大杯），後接 more than 50 grams of sugar，表示一杯飲料「含有」超過五十克的糖，應選 contain。consider 考慮、continue 繼續、contact 聯絡，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["cause", "lead", "result", "bring"], "answer": 1, "explain": "lead to + 名詞 為固定搭配，表「導致……」。cause 後直接接受詞，不加 to；result 須搭配 in 才表「導致」；bring 須用 bring about。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Instead", "Otherwise", "Moreover", "Similarly"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說「這不代表你必須放棄最愛的飲料」，後句提出替代做法「做出更聰明的選擇」，應用 Instead（反而、改為）。Otherwise 否則、Moreover 此外、Similarly 同樣地，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["because of", "such as", "according to", "rather than"], "answer": 3, "explain": "本段在建議較健康的點法：「要鮮奶，而不是奶精」，rather than（而不是）表示取捨。because of 因為、such as 例如、according to 根據，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C14", "title": "Ugly Fruit, Beautiful Idea", "title_zh": "醜蔬果，好點子", "level": "B1", "topic": "food", "passage": "Every year, a huge amount of food around the world is thrown away. According to the United Nations, about one third of all food produced is lost or wasted. Some of this waste happens before the food even __1__ the store. Farmers often throw away fruit and vegetables simply because they look strange, even though they taste just as good.\n\nLin Yu-ting, a 17-year-old student in Tainan, decided to do something about this problem. She and her classmates started a small market __2__ \"Second Look.\" Every Saturday, they buy oddly shaped carrots, spotted bananas, and tiny apples from local farmers and sell them at low prices. At first, many shoppers were not __3__ in the strange-looking food. __4__, after tasting it, most of them came back for more.\n\nToday, \"Second Look\" sells about 200 kilograms of produce each week. Lin hopes her market will encourage people to judge food by its taste, not by __5__ it looks.", "passage_zh": "每年，全世界都有大量的食物被丟棄。根據聯合國的資料，所有生產出來的食物中約有三分之一遭到損耗或浪費。有些浪費甚至在食物送到商店之前就發生了。農民常常只因為蔬果長得奇怪就把它們丟掉，儘管它們嚐起來一樣美味。\n\n林語婷是台南一名十七歲的學生，她決定為這個問題做點什麼。她和同學們開了一個名叫「再看一眼」的小市集。每週六，他們向當地農民收購形狀奇特的紅蘿蔔、有斑點的香蕉和小顆的蘋果，再以低價售出。起初，許多顧客對這些外表奇怪的食物不感興趣。然而，嚐過之後，大部分的人都回來買更多。\n\n如今，「再看一眼」每週大約賣出兩百公斤的農產品。林語婷希望她的市集能鼓勵人們以味道來評斷食物，而不是看它的外表。", "blanks": [{"options": ["arrives", "reaches", "gets", "comes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "reach 為及物動詞，後面可直接接受詞 the store，表「抵達」。arrive 須接 at/in，get 與 come 須接 to；空格後沒有介系詞，故皆不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["calls", "calling", "called", "to call"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格修飾名詞 a small market，市集是「被命名為」Second Look，應用過去分詞 called 表被動，相當於 which was called。calls 為動詞，但句中已有主要動詞 started；calling 表主動，不合「被命名」之意；to call 表目的，語意不通。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["excited", "worried", "interesting", "interested"], "answer": 3, "explain": "be interested in 表「對……感興趣」，主詞為人（shoppers）時用 -ed 形容詞。interesting 用來形容事物；excited 與 worried 通常搭配 about，不與 in 連用，worried 的語意也與後文 However 的轉折不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["However", "Therefore", "In addition", "In other words"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說起初顧客不感興趣，後句說嚐過之後大多數人都回來買更多，前後語意轉折，應用 However（然而）。Therefore 因此、In addition 此外、In other words 換句話說，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["what", "how", "which", "that"], "answer": 1, "explain": "介系詞 by 後接名詞子句，「以它的外表」即 how it looks（它看起來如何）。若用 what，須寫成 what it looks like；which 與 that 不能引導此名詞子句。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C15", "title": "Racing Together: Dragon Boats", "title_zh": "齊心划龍舟", "level": "B1", "topic": "sports", "passage": "Dragon boat racing is one of the oldest team sports in the world, with a history of more than 2,000 years. In Taiwan, races are held __1__ the Dragon Boat Festival, which falls on the fifth day of the fifth month of the lunar calendar. According to legend, people first raced boats to search for Qu Yuan, a poet who __2__ himself in a river.\n\nA standard dragon boat carries 20 paddlers, a drummer, and a steerer. The drummer sits at the front and beats a rhythm so that the paddlers can move their paddles at the same time. If even one person is out of sync, the boat will slow down. __3__, teamwork is more important than individual strength in this sport.\n\nToday, dragon boat racing has spread far beyond Asia. Teams in Canada, Germany, and Australia __4__ part in international competitions every year. For many paddlers, the best part is not winning but the feeling of working together toward a common __5__.", "passage_zh": "龍舟競賽是世界上最古老的團隊運動之一，已有超過兩千年的歷史。在台灣，比賽在端午節期間舉行，端午節是農曆五月初五。根據傳說，人們最初划船競渡是為了尋找投江自盡的詩人屈原。\n\n一艘標準龍舟載有二十名划手、一名鼓手和一名舵手。鼓手坐在船頭擊鼓打節奏，讓划手們能同時划槳。只要有一個人動作不同步，船速就會變慢。因此，在這項運動中，團隊合作比個人力量更重要。\n\n如今，龍舟競賽已傳播到亞洲以外的地方。加拿大、德國和澳洲的隊伍每年都會參加國際比賽。對許多划手而言，最棒的部分不是獲勝，而是朝著共同目標一起努力的感覺。", "blanks": [{"options": ["while", "during", "since", "until"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格後為名詞片語 the Dragon Boat Festival，須用介系詞 during（在……期間）。while 為連接詞，後須接子句；since 自從、until 直到，語意不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["drowned", "drowning", "was drowned", "has drowned"], "answer": 0, "explain": "屈原投江是過去發生的事，應用過去式；drown oneself 表「投水自盡」，為主動用法，故用 drowned。was drowned 為被動，後面不可再接 himself；has drowned 為現在完成式，不適用於兩千多年前的事；drowning 不能單獨當動詞。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["In contrast", "For example", "Therefore", "Otherwise"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前句說只要有一人動作不同步船速就會變慢，後句得出結論「團隊合作比個人力量更重要」，前因後果，應用 Therefore（因此）。In contrast 相較之下、For example 例如、Otherwise 否則，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["make", "have", "get", "take"], "answer": 3, "explain": "take part in 為固定片語，表「參加」。make、have、get 皆不與 part in 搭配成此意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["habit", "price", "goal", "rule"], "answer": 2, "explain": "work together toward a common goal 表「朝著共同目標一起努力」，goal（目標）最合語意。habit 習慣、price 價格、rule 規則，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C16", "title": "Climbing to New Heights", "title_zh": "攀向新高峰", "level": "B1", "topic": "sports", "passage": "Rock climbing used to be a sport mainly for adventurers who climbed real mountains. Today, however, more and more teenagers are trying it at indoor climbing gyms. These gyms have colorful plastic holds attached to tall walls, and many climbers wear a harness tied to a rope __1__ they fall.\n\nSport climbing became even more popular after it __2__ its first appearance at the Tokyo 2020 Olympic Games. Many young people watched the athletes move quickly up the walls and wanted to try it __3__.\n\nClimbing is a great workout for the whole body. It builds strength in your arms, legs, and back, and it also trains your mind. __4__ you can reach the top, you need to plan each move carefully, almost like solving a puzzle. Chen Wei, a high school student who climbs three times a week, says the sport has made him more patient. \"When I get stuck, I don't give up,\" he explains. \"I just stop, take a deep breath, and look for another way. I've learned that being calm is just as important as __5__ strong.\"", "passage_zh": "攀岩過去主要是攀登真正山岳的冒險家所從事的運動。然而，如今越來越多青少年到室內攀岩館嘗試這項運動。這些攀岩館在高牆上裝了色彩繽紛的塑膠岩點，許多攀岩者會穿戴繫著繩索的吊帶，以防墜落。\n\n運動攀登在2020年東京奧運首度亮相後變得更加熱門。許多年輕人看到選手們迅速攀上岩牆，便想親自試試看。\n\n攀岩是很棒的全身運動。它能鍛鍊手臂、腿部和背部的力量，也能訓練你的頭腦。在你能抵達頂端之前，你需要仔細規劃每一步，幾乎就像在解謎一樣。陳威是一名每週攀岩三次的高中生，他說這項運動讓他變得更有耐心。他解釋：「卡住的時候，我不會放棄。我只是停下來，深呼吸，然後尋找另一條路線。我學到了保持冷靜和強壯一樣重要。」", "blanks": [{"options": ["in case", "so that", "even if", "as long as"], "answer": 0, "explain": "穿戴繫著繩索的吊帶是為了「以防」墜落，in case + 子句 表「以防萬一」。so that 以便、even if 即使、as long as 只要，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["took", "made", "did", "gave"], "answer": 1, "explain": "make one's first appearance 為固定搭配，表「首度亮相、首次登場」。take、do、give 皆不與 appearance 構成此意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["itself", "ourselves", "them", "themselves"], "answer": 3, "explain": "主詞為 Many young people，表示「親自」嘗試，應用反身代名詞 themselves 加強語氣。itself 指單數事物、ourselves 指「我們」，them 為受格，不能放在句尾表「親自」。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Unless", "Although", "Before", "Because"], "answer": 2, "explain": "「在你能抵達頂端『之前』，你需要仔細規劃每一步」，Before 最合語意。Unless 除非、Although 雖然、Because 因為，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["be", "being", "been", "to be"], "answer": 1, "explain": "as...as 比較的前後須詞性對等（平行結構）：前面為動名詞 being calm，後面也應用動名詞 being strong。be 為原形、been 為過去分詞、to be 為不定詞，皆與前面的 being calm 不對等。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C17", "title": "A Day Trip on the High Speed Rail", "title_zh": "搭高鐵一日遊", "level": "B1", "topic": "travel", "passage": "Last summer, my cousin and I took a day trip from Taipei to Kaohsiung on the Taiwan High Speed Rail. Before the high-speed rail __1__ in 2007, the same journey by regular train usually took four to five hours. Now, the fastest trains can travel at 300 kilometers per hour and reach Kaohsiung in about an hour and a half.\n\nWe booked our tickets online __2__, so we got seats next to the window. The ride was so smooth that I hardly noticed how fast we were going. After we arrived at Zuoying Station, we took the MRT to the Pier-2 Art Center, __3__ old warehouses have been turned into galleries and cafés. We spent the afternoon __4__ photos of the colorful art along the harbor. In the evening, we enjoyed some local snacks at a night market before catching the train home.\n\nThe trip was short. __5__, it turned out to be one of the best days of our summer. Thanks to the high-speed rail, we explored a whole new city and were back in our own beds that night.", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，我和表哥搭台灣高鐵從台北到高雄一日遊。在高鐵於2007年通車之前，搭一般火車走同樣的路程通常要四到五個小時。現在，最快的列車時速可達三百公里，大約一個半小時就能抵達高雄。\n\n我們事先在網路上訂了票，所以拿到了靠窗的座位。列車行駛得非常平穩，我幾乎沒察覺我們跑得有多快。抵達左營站後，我們搭捷運到駁二藝術特區，那裡的舊倉庫已被改造成藝廊和咖啡館。我們整個下午都在港邊拍攝色彩繽紛的藝術作品。傍晚，我們在夜市享用了一些在地小吃，然後搭高鐵回家。\n\n這趟旅程很短。儘管如此，它卻成了我們這個夏天最棒的日子之一。多虧了高鐵，我們探索了一座全新的城市，當晚就回到自己的床上了。", "blanks": [{"options": ["opens", "has opened", "opened", "will open"], "answer": 2, "explain": "高鐵通車是過去發生的事，句中有明確的過去時間 in 2007，應用過去式 opened。has opened 為現在完成式，不可與明確的過去時間連用；opens、will open 時態不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["in advance", "in person", "in vain", "in turn"], "answer": 0, "explain": "「事先」在網路上訂票，所以才拿到靠窗座位，in advance（事先、預先）最合語意。in person 親自（與 online 矛盾）、in vain 徒勞、in turn 依序，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["which", "where", "when", "what"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格引導非限定形容詞子句，修飾表地點的先行詞 the Pier-2 Art Center，且子句 old warehouses have been turned into... 結構完整、不缺主詞或受詞，應用關係副詞 where。which 在子句中須當主詞或受詞；when 指時間；what 前面不可有先行詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["take", "to take", "taking", "took"], "answer": 2, "explain": "spend + 時間 + V-ing 為固定句型，表「花時間做某事」，故用 taking（take photos 拍照）。take 為原形、to take 為不定詞、took 為過去式，皆不合此句型。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["Therefore", "Besides", "Instead", "Still"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說旅程很短，後句卻說它是夏天最棒的日子之一，前後語意轉折，應用 Still（儘管如此、然而）。Therefore 因此、Besides 此外、Instead 反而（表取代），皆不合。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C18", "title": "Around Taiwan on Two Wheels", "title_zh": "單車環島", "level": "B1", "topic": "travel", "passage": "For many young people in Taiwan, cycling around the island is a dream they hope to make __1__ true before they graduate. The most popular path, Cycling Route No. 1, is nearly 1,000 kilometers long and passes through mountains, fields, and small fishing villages.\n\nLast winter, Huang Kai and two friends from his senior high school decided to take on the challenge. They prepared for the trip __2__ riding 30 kilometers every weekend for three months. They also learned how to fix a flat tire, __3__ they knew they might have trouble far from any bike shop.\n\nThe journey took them eleven days. Some parts were harder than they had expected. On the east coast, strong winds blew against them all day, and they could barely move forward. __4__, the views of the Pacific Ocean made every hard moment worth it. \"The best part was the people,\" Huang says. \"Shop owners gave us free water, and convenience store clerks __5__ us where to find cheap places to stay.\"", "passage_zh": "對台灣許多年輕人來說，騎單車環島是他們希望在畢業前實現的夢想。最熱門的路線「環島1號線」全長將近一千公里，沿途經過山區、田野和小漁村。\n\n去年冬天，黃凱和兩位高中朋友決定接受這項挑戰。他們藉由連續三個月每週末騎三十公里，為這趟旅程做準備。他們也學會了修補爆胎，因為他們知道可能會在離任何單車店都很遠的地方遇上麻煩。\n\n這趟旅程花了他們十一天。有些路段比他們預期的還要艱難。在東海岸，強風整天迎面吹來，他們幾乎無法前進。即便如此，太平洋的美景讓每個辛苦的時刻都值得了。黃凱說：「最棒的是遇到的人。商店老闆請我們喝免費的水，便利商店店員告訴我們哪裡可以找到便宜的住處。」", "blanks": [{"options": ["to come", "come", "coming", "came"], "answer": 1, "explain": "make + 受詞 + 原形動詞 表「使……」，make a dream come true 意為「實現夢想」。本句受詞 a dream 移到前面當先行詞，make 後面仍接原形動詞 come。to come、coming、came 皆不能接在 make 的受詞之後當受詞補語。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["with", "for", "by", "in"], "answer": 2, "explain": "by + V-ing 表「藉由（某種方法）」：他們「藉由」每週末騎三十公里來為旅程做準備。with、for、in 皆無此用法。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["although", "unless", "until", "because"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說他們學會修補爆胎，後句說明原因「因為知道可能在遠離單車店的地方遇上麻煩」，應用 because（因為）。although 雖然、unless 除非、until 直到，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Even so", "As a result", "For instance", "In other words"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說強風讓他們幾乎無法前進，後句說美景讓每個辛苦時刻都值得，前後語意轉折，Even so（即使如此）最恰當。As a result 結果、For instance 例如、In other words 換句話說，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["said", "told", "spoke", "talked"], "answer": 1, "explain": "tell + 人 + wh- 片語 表「告訴某人……」，故用 told（told us where to find...）。say 不直接接「人」當受詞（須用 say to sb），speak、talk 也須接 to，且不接 where to... 的句型。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C19", "title": "Sharing the Ride with YouBike", "title_zh": "共享單車 YouBike", "level": "B1", "topic": "transportation", "passage": "In many cities in Taiwan, you can see rows of yellow bikes parked at stations near MRT exits, schools, and parks. These are YouBikes, part of a public bike-sharing system. Users can rent a bike at one of the many stations in the city and return it at __1__ station, which makes the bikes very convenient for short trips. Renting a YouBike is simple. Most people just tap an EasyCard or use a phone app to __2__ a bike. The first part of each ride is cheap, so many students use the bikes to get from the MRT station to school.\n\nBike-sharing has several benefits. It reduces traffic jams and air pollution, and it gives people a chance to exercise during their daily trips. __3__, it saves riders from worrying about bike theft or repairs.\n\nHowever, riders should follow some basic rules. They must obey traffic lights, and they should never use their phones __4__ riding. At night, it is important to turn on the bike's lights so that drivers can see you. If everyone rides safely, public bikes can make our cities cleaner, healthier, and __5__ places to live.", "passage_zh": "在台灣許多城市，你可以看到一排排黃色的腳踏車停放在捷運出口、學校和公園附近的站點。這些是 YouBike，屬於公共自行車共享系統的一部分。使用者可以在市區眾多站點的其中一個借車，再到另一個站點還車，讓這些腳踏車用於短程移動非常方便。租借 YouBike 很簡單。大部分的人只要用悠遊卡感應或使用手機應用程式，就能解鎖腳踏車。每趟騎乘前段的費用很便宜，所以許多學生騎它從捷運站到學校。\n\n共享單車有好幾項好處。它能減少交通壅塞和空氣汙染，也讓人們在日常通勤時有機會運動。此外，它讓騎乘者不必擔心腳踏車遭竊或需要維修。\n\n然而，騎乘者應該遵守一些基本規則。他們必須遵守紅綠燈，騎車時也絕對不該使用手機。晚上騎車時，打開車燈很重要，這樣駕駛人才看得到你。如果每個人都安全騎車，公共自行車就能讓我們的城市成為更乾淨、更健康、更宜人的居住地。", "blanks": [{"options": ["other", "others", "another", "the other"], "answer": 2, "explain": "another + 單數可數名詞 表「另一個（不特定的）」。前文說市區有眾多站點（one of the many stations），還車的站點是其中「另一個」不特定的站，故用 another station。other 修飾可數名詞時須接複數；others 為代名詞，後面不可接名詞；the other 表「兩者中的另一個」，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["unlock", "unload", "uncover", "undo"], "answer": 0, "explain": "用悠遊卡感應或手機應用程式是為了「解鎖」腳踏車，unlock 最合語意。unload 卸貨、uncover 揭開、undo 復原，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["Otherwise", "As a result", "In contrast", "In addition"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句列出共享單車的好處（減少塞車與汙染、能順便運動），本句再補充另一項好處，應用 In addition（此外）。Otherwise 否則、As a result 結果、In contrast 相較之下，皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["during", "while", "because", "unless"], "answer": 1, "explain": "while + V-ing 表「在……的時候」，為連接詞後省略主詞與 be 動詞的分詞構句：while (they are) riding。during 為介系詞，須接名詞（如 during the ride），不接 V-ing；because 因為、unless 除非，語意不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["more pleasant", "most pleasant", "pleasantly", "pleasure"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格與 cleaner、healthier 對等並列，三者皆為比較級形容詞，共同修飾 places，故應用比較級 more pleasant。most pleasant 為最高級、pleasantly 為副詞、pleasure 為名詞，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C20", "title": "Riding the Taipei MRT", "title_zh": "搭乘台北捷運", "level": "B1", "topic": "transportation", "passage": "The Taipei MRT is famous for being clean, safe, and on time. Since it first opened in 1996, it __1__ one of the most important ways to get around the city. Every day, about two million trips are made on its trains.\n\nOne reason the MRT stays so clean is its strict rules. Passengers are not allowed to eat, drink, or chew gum once they pass through the ticket gates. People who break this rule may be __2__. Visitors from other countries are often surprised by this, but most of them soon understand why it is necessary.\n\nAnother thing visitors notice is the dark blue priority seats. These seats are reserved for people __3__ need them more, such as the elderly, pregnant women, and people with injuries. __4__ there are no such passengers nearby, other people can sit there, but they should give up the seat when someone in need gets on. Small acts of politeness like these make the ride __5__ for everyone. That is why many people say that taking the MRT shows the best side of Taiwan.", "passage_zh": "台北捷運以乾淨、安全、準時聞名。自1996年首度通車以來，它已成為在市區移動最重要的方式之一。每天約有兩百萬人次搭乘捷運列車。\n\n捷運能保持如此乾淨的原因之一，是它嚴格的規定。乘客一旦通過驗票閘門，就不准吃東西、喝飲料或嚼口香糖。違反這項規定的人可能會被罰款。來自其他國家的遊客常對此感到驚訝，但大多數人很快就明白為什麼這是必要的。\n\n遊客注意到的另一件事是深藍色的博愛座。這些座位是保留給較需要的人，例如年長者、孕婦和受傷的人。如果附近沒有這類乘客，其他人可以坐，但有需要的人上車時，他們應該讓座。像這樣的小小禮貌舉動，讓每個人的乘車經驗都很愉快。這就是為什麼許多人說，搭捷運展現了台灣最美好的一面。", "blanks": [{"options": ["became", "has become", "is becoming", "had become"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Since + 過去時間（自1996年首度通車以來），主要子句應用現在完成式，表示從過去持續到現在的狀態，故用 has become。became 為過去式，無法表示持續到現在的狀態；is becoming 為現在進行式、had become 為過去完成式，皆不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["found", "fired", "fined", "fed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "違反規定的人可能「被罰款」，be fined 為被動語態，fine 當動詞意為「處以罰金」。found 被發現、fired 被解雇、fed 被餵食，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["which", "whose", "whom", "who"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格引導形容詞子句修飾先行詞 people（人），且在子句中當主詞（___ need them more），應用 who。which 用於物、whose 後須接名詞、whom 為受格，皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["If", "Unless", "Because of", "Although"], "answer": 0, "explain": "「『如果』附近沒有這類乘客，其他人可以坐」，If 引導條件子句最合語意。Unless 除非（語意相反）、Although 雖然（語意不合），Because of 為介系詞片語，不可接子句。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["enjoy", "enjoyably", "enjoyable", "enjoyment"], "answer": 2, "explain": "make + 受詞 + 形容詞 表「使……變得……」，空格為受詞 the ride 的補語，應用形容詞 enjoyable（愉快的）。enjoy 為動詞、enjoyably 為副詞、enjoyment 為名詞，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C21", "title": "Seeing Is No Longer Believing", "title_zh": "眼見不再為憑", "level": "B2", "topic": "technology", "passage": "A few years ago, a video could usually be trusted as proof that something had really happened. Today, __1__, artificial intelligence can produce \"deepfakes,\" fake videos in which a person appears to say or do things they never did. Some are made just for fun, but others are used to spread false news or __2__ people out of their money.\n\nSo how can we protect ourselves? Experts say the first step is to slow down. Before sharing a shocking clip, ask who posted it and whether any trusted news outlet has reported the same story. It also helps to look closely at the details. Faces in deepfakes may blink in strange ways, and the movements of the lips may not __3__ the words being spoken.\n\nAs the technology improves, such mistakes are becoming harder to notice. __4__, the most powerful tool we have is not a piece of software but our own critical thinking. A video that makes you extremely angry or excited is exactly the kind you should question. After all, a lie that __5__ a thousand times does not become true.", "passage_zh": "幾年前，影片通常可以被當作某件事確實發生過的證據。然而如今，人工智慧可以製作出「深偽」影片，也就是讓某人看起來說了或做了他從未說過、做過的事的假影片。有些只是做來好玩，但有些則被用來散播假新聞，或騙取人們的錢財。\n\n那麼，我們要如何保護自己呢？專家表示，第一步是放慢腳步。在分享一段令人震驚的影片之前，先問問是誰發布的，以及是否有任何可信的新聞媒體報導過同樣的事件。仔細觀察細節也有幫助。深偽影片中的人臉眨眼的方式可能很奇怪，嘴唇的動作也可能和說出的話對不上。\n\n隨著科技進步，這類破綻越來越難察覺。因此，我們所擁有最強大的工具並不是某個軟體，而是我們自己的批判性思考。一段讓你極度憤怒或興奮的影片，正是你最該質疑的那種。畢竟，一個謊言就算被重複一千次，也不會變成真的。", "blanks": [{"options": ["therefore", "however", "for instance", "in addition"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前句說幾年前影片通常可被當作事情真實發生的證據，本句說如今 AI 能製作以假亂真的「深偽」影片，前後語意形成對比，應選 however（然而）。therefore 因此、for instance 例如、in addition 此外，皆無法表達轉折。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["trick", "warn", "remind", "prevent"], "answer": 0, "explain": "trick sb out of sth 為固定用法，表「騙走某人的某物」，trick people out of their money 即「騙取人們的錢財」，與前面的 spread false news 同為深偽影片的不良用途。warn 警告、remind 提醒、prevent 防止，皆不能與 out of their money 搭配，語意也不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["attract", "replace", "match", "repeat"], "answer": 2, "explain": "本段在說明如何看出深偽影片的破綻：嘴唇的動作可能和說出的話「對不上」，空格需要及物動詞 match（與……相符），may not match the words 即「可能與話語不吻合」。attract 吸引、replace 取代、repeat 重複，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["Otherwise", "On the contrary", "For example", "Therefore"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句指出隨著科技進步，深偽的破綻越來越難察覺；後句說明「因此」我們最強大的工具是自己的批判性思考，前因後果，應選 Therefore（因此）。Otherwise 否則、On the contrary 正好相反、For example 例如，皆不合上下文邏輯。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["repeats", "is repeated", "repeating", "has repeated"], "answer": 1, "explain": "關係子句 that ___ a thousand times 修飾先行詞 a lie，謊言是「被」重複的，須用被動語態 is repeated。repeats 與 has repeated 為主動語態，repeating 則使關係子句缺少動詞，皆不正確。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C22", "title": "Gold in Your Drawer", "title_zh": "抽屜裡的黃金", "level": "B2", "topic": "environment", "passage": "Do you have an old phone lying forgotten in a drawer? You are not alone. Millions of used devices are never recycled, and many end up in landfills, __1__ harmful chemicals such as lead and mercury can leak into the soil and water.\n\nWhat many people do not realize is that these devices are also full of valuable metals. In fact, a tonne of old phones contains far more gold than a tonne of rock dug from a gold mine. Collecting such metals from electronic waste, a process known as \"urban mining,\" __2__ the need for new mines, which often destroy forests and pollute rivers. Japan offered a famous example of this idea. Before the Tokyo 2020 Olympics, organizers asked the public to __3__ their unwanted electronics. More than six million used phones were collected, and every medal was made from metal __4__ from them and other small devices.\n\nSo the next time you get a new phone, do not just throw the old one away. Take it to a recycling point __5__. Who knows? It might end up around an athlete's neck one day.", "passage_zh": "你是否有一支舊手機被遺忘在抽屜裡？你並不孤單。數以百萬計的舊裝置從未被回收，許多最後被丟進垃圾掩埋場，在那裡，鉛和汞等有害化學物質可能滲入土壤和水中。\n\n許多人沒有意識到的是，這些裝置裡也充滿了貴重金屬。事實上，一公噸舊手機所含的黃金，遠比從金礦挖出的一公噸岩石還要多。從電子廢棄物中收集這些金屬，也就是所謂的「城市採礦」，能減少開採新礦場的需要，而開採礦場往往會破壞森林、污染河川。日本就為這個概念提供了一個著名的例子。在 2020 年東京奧運之前，主辦單位請民眾捐出不要的電子產品。結果收集到超過六百萬支舊手機，而每一面獎牌都是用從這些手機及其他小型裝置中提煉出的金屬製成的。\n\n所以下次你換新手機時，別只是把舊的丟掉，改把它送到回收點吧。誰知道呢？說不定有一天它會掛在某位運動員的脖子上。", "blanks": [{"options": ["what", "which", "when", "where"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格引導形容詞子句修飾表地點的先行詞 landfills，且子句 harmful chemicals ... can leak into the soil and water 結構完整，不缺主詞或受詞，應用關係副詞 where（= in which）。which 須在子句中當主詞或受詞，what 不可接先行詞，when 用來修飾時間，皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["reduce", "reduces", "reducing", "to reduce"], "answer": 1, "explain": "本句主詞為動名詞片語 Collecting such metals from electronic waste（中間的 a process known as \"urban mining\" 是同位語），動名詞當主詞視為單數，且敘述一般事實用現在式，故動詞用 reduces。reduce 與單數主詞不一致，reducing 與 to reduce 都不能當句子的主要動詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["donate", "repair", "purchase", "design"], "answer": 0, "explain": "後句提到「收集到超過六百萬支舊手機」，可知主辦單位是請民眾「捐出」不要的電子產品，應選 donate（捐贈）。repair 修理、purchase 購買、design 設計，皆與下文不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["exported", "expected", "extracted", "excluded"], "answer": 2, "explain": "獎牌是用從舊手機和其他小型裝置中「提煉出」的金屬製成的，metal (which was) extracted from them，extract 意為「提取、提煉」，此處為過去分詞表被動。exported 出口、expected 預期、excluded 排除，語意皆不通。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["instead", "otherwise", "as well", "anyway"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說不要把舊手機丟掉，本句說「改為」把它送到回收點，表示以後者取代前者，應選副詞 instead（反而、改為）。otherwise 否則、as well 也、anyway 無論如何，皆無法表達「取而代之」的意思。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C23", "title": "When Coral Turns White", "title_zh": "當珊瑚變白", "level": "B2", "topic": "environment", "passage": "Coral reefs cover less than one percent of the ocean floor, yet they are home to about a quarter of all marine species. Although corals may look like colorful rocks, they are actually animals. Much of their color comes from tiny algae that live inside their bodies. The algae produce food through photosynthesis, and in return, the corals provide them __1__ a safe place to live.\n\nThis partnership, however, is easily __2__ by heat. When the water stays too warm for several weeks, stressed corals push the algae out and turn pale white. This is called coral bleaching. Bleached corals are not dead yet, but they are slowly starving. If the temperature returns to normal soon, they can __3__; if not, many of them will die.\n\nIn recent years, rising ocean temperatures __4__ mass bleaching on reefs around the world, including those in Kenting, Taiwan. Scientists are now trying to grow heat-tolerant corals in labs. __5__, they warn that such efforts will mean little unless we also cut the greenhouse gases that are warming our seas.", "passage_zh": "珊瑚礁覆蓋的面積不到海床的百分之一，卻是約四分之一海洋物種的家。雖然珊瑚看起來像彩色的石頭，但牠們其實是動物。牠們的顏色大多來自住在體內的微小藻類。這些藻類透過光合作用製造養分，而珊瑚則提供牠們一個安全的居所作為回報。\n\n然而，這種合作關係很容易被高溫破壞。當海水連續數週過熱時，受到壓力的珊瑚會把藻類排出體外，變成蒼白色，這就是所謂的珊瑚白化。白化的珊瑚還沒有死，但正在慢慢挨餓。如果水溫很快恢復正常，牠們就能復原；如果沒有，許多珊瑚將會死亡。\n\n近年來，海水溫度上升已在全世界的珊瑚礁造成大規模白化，台灣墾丁的珊瑚礁也不例外。科學家目前正嘗試在實驗室培育耐熱的珊瑚。即便如此，他們警告，除非我們同時減少使海洋暖化的溫室氣體，否則這類努力將沒有多大意義。", "blanks": [{"options": ["for", "with", "to", "of"], "answer": 1, "explain": "provide sb with sth 為固定用法，表「提供某人某物」；provide them with a safe place to live 即「提供牠們一個安全的居所」。若要用 for，須寫成 provide a safe place for them，與本句語序不符；to 與 of 皆無此用法。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["damaged", "strengthened", "improved", "supported"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前段說珊瑚與藻類互利共生，本句以 however 轉折，接著描述高溫使珊瑚排出藻類而白化，可知這種合作關係容易「被破壞」，be damaged by 表被動。strengthened 加強、improved 改善、supported 支持，皆與下文描述相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["remove", "refuse", "reflect", "recover"], "answer": 3, "explain": "分號前後形成對比：若水溫很快恢復正常，珊瑚就能「復原」；若沒有，許多珊瑚就會死亡，應選 recover（恢復、復原）。remove 移除、refuse 拒絕、reflect 反射、反映，語意皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["will cause", "had caused", "have caused", "are caused"], "answer": 2, "explain": "In recent years（近年來）常與現在完成式連用，表示從過去持續到現在的情況；主詞 rising ocean temperatures 為複數，後面又有受詞 mass bleaching，應用主動的 have caused。will cause 為未來式、had caused 為過去完成式、are caused 為被動語態，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["Even so", "As a result", "In other words", "For example"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說科學家正在實驗室培育耐熱珊瑚，後句卻說他們警告這類努力意義不大，語意有讓步轉折，應選 Even so（即便如此）。As a result 因此、In other words 換句話說、For example 例如，皆無法表達轉折。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C24", "title": "The Toughest Animal on Earth", "title_zh": "地球上最頑強的動物", "level": "B2", "topic": "science", "passage": "If you were asked to name the toughest animal on Earth, you might think of a lion or a shark. Scientists, __1__, might choose a creature that is usually less than one millimeter long: the tardigrade, also known as the \"water bear.\"\n\nTardigrades live almost everywhere, from mountaintops to the deep sea, and even in the moss growing on city walls. What makes them so special is their ability to survive conditions that would kill nearly any other animal. When their surroundings dry out, they pull in their legs, lose almost all the water in their bodies, and enter a state __2__ a \"tun.\" In this form, they can stay alive for years without food or water. Once water returns, they __3__ to life within a few hours.\n\nIn 2007, scientists even sent tardigrades into space and __4__ them directly to its cold vacuum. Amazingly, many of them survived the trip. Researchers are now studying how these tiny animals protect their cells, hoping the knowledge might one day help us __5__ vaccines without refrigeration.", "passage_zh": "如果要你說出地球上最頑強的動物，你可能會想到獅子或鯊魚。另一方面，科學家可能會選擇一種通常不到一公釐長的生物：緩步動物，也就是俗稱的「水熊蟲」。\n\n水熊蟲幾乎無所不在，從山頂到深海，甚至在城市牆面上生長的苔蘚裡都找得到。牠們之所以如此特別，在於牠們能在幾乎會殺死其他任何動物的環境中存活。當周遭環境變乾時，牠們會縮起腳，失去體內幾乎所有的水分，進入一種稱為「桶狀體」（tun）的狀態。在這種型態下，牠們可以在沒有食物和水的情況下存活好幾年。一旦水分回來，牠們會在幾個小時內復活。\n\n2007 年，科學家甚至把水熊蟲送上太空，讓牠們直接暴露在寒冷的真空中。令人驚訝的是，其中許多都活著回來了。研究人員目前正在研究這些微小的動物如何保護自己的細胞，希望這些知識有一天能幫助我們在不冷藏的情況下保存疫苗。", "blanks": [{"options": ["in addition", "as a result", "in short", "on the other hand"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說一般人可能會想到獅子或鯊魚，本句說科學家則可能選擇微小的水熊蟲，兩者形成對照，應選 on the other hand（另一方面）。in addition 此外、as a result 因此、in short 簡言之，皆沒有對照的意思。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["known as", "knowing as", "is known as", "which knows as"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格修飾名詞 a state，指「被稱為」桶狀體的狀態，應用過去分詞片語 known as（= which is known as）表被動。knowing as 為主動，不合文法；is known as 前面缺少關係代名詞；which knows as 為主動語態，語意錯誤。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["turn down", "come back", "give up", "carry on"], "answer": 1, "explain": "come back to life 為固定片語，表「復活、甦醒」。前句說水熊蟲在缺水時進入休眠狀態，本句說水分回來後牠們便「復活」。turn down 拒絕、give up 放棄、carry on 繼續，皆不能與 to life 搭配。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["explored", "expanded", "exposed", "explained"], "answer": 2, "explain": "expose A to B 表「使 A 暴露於 B」，句中 them directly to its cold vacuum 正符合此用法，指科學家讓水熊蟲直接暴露在太空的真空中。explored 探索、expanded 擴大、explained 解釋，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["storing", "stored", "being stored", "store"], "answer": 3, "explain": "help + 受詞 + 原形動詞（或 to V）表「幫助某人做某事」，選項中只有原形動詞 store 符合，help us store vaccines 即「幫助我們保存疫苗」。storing、stored、being stored 皆不能接在 help us 之後。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C25", "title": "Saying Goodbye to Plastic Straws", "title_zh": "向塑膠吸管說再見", "level": "B2", "topic": "society", "passage": "In 2015, a video of a sea turtle with a plastic straw stuck in its nose shocked people around the world. For many viewers, it was the first time they __1__ how much damage a small piece of plastic could do.\n\nTaiwan has been one of the leaders in reducing plastic waste. Since July 2019, fast-food restaurants, department stores, schools and government offices have not been allowed to give out single-use plastic straws to customers __2__ eat in. At first, some people complained, but many soon got used to drinking straight from the cup or using straws made of paper, glass or stainless steel. Some drink shops even offer discounts to customers who bring their own cups, __3__ encourages people to form greener habits.\n\nOf course, straws make up only a tiny __4__ of the plastic in the ocean, and some critics say that banning them is not enough. __5__, supporters believe that such small changes matter because they make people think twice about what they throw away.", "passage_zh": "2015 年，一段海龜鼻孔裡卡著塑膠吸管的影片震驚了全世界。對許多觀眾來說，那是他們第一次意識到，一小段塑膠竟能造成如此大的傷害。\n\n台灣一直是減少塑膠垃圾的領先者之一。自 2019 年 7 月起，速食店、百貨公司、學校和政府機關都不得提供一次性塑膠吸管給內用的顧客。起初有些人抱怨，但許多人很快就習慣直接就口喝，或改用紙、玻璃或不鏽鋼製成的吸管。有些飲料店甚至會給自備杯子的顧客折扣，這鼓勵了人們養成更環保的習慣。\n\n當然，吸管只占海洋塑膠的一小部分，也有批評者認為光是禁用吸管還不夠。儘管如此，支持者相信這樣的小改變很重要，因為它們讓人們在丟東西之前會三思。", "blanks": [{"options": ["realize", "have realized", "had realized", "will realize"], "answer": 2, "explain": "「It was the first time + (that) 子句」表「那是（某人）第一次……」，主要子句用過去式 was 時，子句須用過去完成式 had + p.p.，故用 had realized。realize 為現在式、have realized 為現在完成式、will realize 為未來式，時態皆與 was 不一致。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["who", "whose", "what", "whom"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格引導形容詞子句修飾先行詞 customers（人），且在子句中當 eat in 的主詞，應用主格關係代名詞 who。whose 為所有格、what 不可有先行詞、whom 為受格，皆不合。eat in 意為「內用」。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["that", "which", "what", "it"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格前有逗號，引導非限定關係子句，指前面整件事（店家給自備杯的顧客折扣），並當 encourages 的主詞，應用 which。that 不可用在逗號後的非限定子句；what 不可有先行詞；用 it 會使兩個子句之間缺少連接詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["function", "fortune", "factor", "fraction"], "answer": 3, "explain": "a (tiny) fraction of 表「（一小）部分的……」，後句說批評者認為禁用吸管還不夠，可知吸管只占海洋塑膠的一小部分。function 功能、fortune 財富、運氣、factor 因素，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Similarly", "As a result", "Nevertheless", "In fact"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前句說吸管只占海洋塑膠的一小部分，批評者認為禁用還不夠；後句說支持者仍相信小改變很重要，前後語意相反，應選 Nevertheless（儘管如此）。Similarly 同樣地、As a result 因此、In fact 事實上，皆無法表達轉折。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C26", "title": "Homework Helper or Shortcut?", "title_zh": "作業幫手還是捷徑？", "level": "B2", "topic": "technology", "passage": "AI chatbots have quickly become a part of many students' lives. With just a few clicks, they can explain a math problem, summarize a long article, or suggest ideas for an essay. It is no wonder that so many teenagers __1__ them when they get stuck on their homework.\n\nHowever, these tools are not as reliable as they may seem. Chatbots work by predicting which words are likely to come next, not by checking facts. As a result, they sometimes make up information and present it __2__ confidently that users believe it. A student who copies such an answer without checking it might end up __3__ a report full of errors.\n\nThere is also a deeper concern. Learning often happens when we struggle with a difficult question. If a machine always does the thinking for us, our own skills may never fully __4__. Many teachers therefore suggest using AI like a tutor rather than an answer machine: ask it to explain the steps, and then try to solve similar problems on your own. Used __5__, AI can make us smarter instead of lazier.", "passage_zh": "AI 聊天機器人很快就成為許多學生生活的一部分。只要點幾下，它們就能解釋數學題、摘要一篇長文章，或為作文提供點子。難怪有這麼多青少年在寫作業卡關時會求助於它們。\n\n然而，這些工具並不像看起來那麼可靠。聊天機器人的運作方式是預測接下來可能出現哪些字詞，而不是查證事實。因此，它們有時會捏造資訊，而且呈現得如此有自信，讓使用者信以為真。一個沒有查證就照抄這種答案的學生，最後可能會交出一份錯誤百出的報告。\n\n此外還有一個更深層的隱憂。學習往往發生在我們努力思考難題的時候。如果總是由機器替我們思考，我們自己的能力可能永遠無法完全發展。因此，許多老師建議把 AI 當作家教，而不是解答機器：請它解釋步驟，然後試著自己解類似的題目。只要使用得當，AI 能讓我們更聰明，而不是更懶惰。", "blanks": [{"options": ["take after", "turn to", "look down on", "put off"], "answer": 1, "explain": "turn to sb 表「向某人求助」，句意為青少年寫作業卡關時會求助於聊天機器人，呼應前句聊天機器人能解釋題目、提供點子。take after 長得像（長輩）、look down on 瞧不起、put off 延後，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["so", "such", "too", "very"], "answer": 0, "explain": "so + 形容詞／副詞 + that 子句 表「如此……以致於……」，空格後為副詞 confidently，並接 that users believe it，應用 so。such 後須接名詞；too 常與 to V 連用（too ... to）；very 不能與 that 子句構成此句型。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["hand in", "to hand in", "handing in", "handed in"], "answer": 2, "explain": "end up + V-ing 表「最後落得……、結果……」，end up handing in a report 即「最後交出一份報告」。hand in 為原形、to hand in 為不定詞、handed in 為過去分詞，皆不能接在 end up 之後。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["decline", "deliver", "disappoint", "develop"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說學習往往發生在努力思考難題的時候，若總是由機器替我們思考，我們自己的能力可能永遠無法完全「發展」，應選 develop。decline 衰退、deliver 遞送、disappoint 使失望，語意皆不通。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["wise", "wisely", "wisdom", "wiser"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Used ___ 為分詞構句（= If it is used ___），修飾動詞 used 須用副詞，wisely 意為「明智地」，句意為「若使用得當，AI 能讓我們更聰明」。wise 為形容詞、wisdom 為名詞、wiser 為比較級形容詞，皆不能修飾動詞。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C27", "title": "Why Ice Cream Hurts Your Head", "title_zh": "為什麼吃冰會頭痛", "level": "B2", "topic": "science", "passage": "Have you ever eaten ice cream so fast that a sharp pain suddenly __1__ across your forehead? This feeling, commonly called \"brain freeze,\" usually lasts less than a minute, but it can be surprisingly painful.\n\nScientists believe brain freeze is caused by a quick change in blood flow. When something very cold touches the roof of your mouth, the blood vessels there quickly narrow. Then, as the area warms up again, they suddenly widen, __2__ more blood to rush in. Nerves in the mouth sense this sudden change and send pain signals to the brain. __3__ these nerves also carry signals from the face, the brain mistakenly thinks the pain is coming from your forehead.\n\nThe good news is that brain freeze is harmless, and it is easy to __4__. Simply press your tongue against the roof of your mouth or sip some warm water to heat the area up. Better still, eat cold treats slowly. That way, you can avoid the pain __5__.", "passage_zh": "你是否曾經吃冰淇淋吃得太快，結果一陣劇痛突然蔓延到整個額頭？這種感覺通常被稱為「腦凍結」，一般持續不到一分鐘，但可能痛得令人驚訝。\n\n科學家認為，腦凍結是由血流的快速變化所引起的。當非常冰冷的東西碰到你的上顎時，那裡的血管會迅速收縮。接著，當該部位再度變暖時，血管又突然擴張，使更多血液湧入。口腔裡的神經察覺到這種突然的變化，便將疼痛訊號傳送到大腦。由於這些神經同時也傳遞來自臉部的訊號，大腦會誤以為疼痛來自你的額頭。\n\n好消息是，腦凍結無害，而且很容易處理。只要用舌頭抵住上顎，或啜飲一些溫水讓該部位變暖即可。更好的做法是，慢慢享用冰涼的點心。如此一來，你從一開始就能避免疼痛。", "blanks": [{"options": ["shrank", "slipped", "spread", "stayed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "spread across 表「蔓延、擴散到整個……」，句意為一陣劇痛突然「擴散」到整個額頭；spread 的過去式與原形同形，此處為過去式。shrank 縮小、slipped 滑落、stayed 停留，皆無法表達疼痛擴散的意思。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["causing", "caused", "cause", "to be caused"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格前為完整句子 they suddenly widen，逗號後接分詞片語表示「結果」，主動語意用現在分詞 causing（= which causes），cause + O + to V 表「使……」。caused 為過去分詞，表被動；cause 會使兩個動詞之間缺少連接詞；to be caused 為被動不定詞，皆不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["Although", "Unless", "Until", "Because"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前半句「這些神經也傳遞來自臉部的訊號」是後半句「大腦誤以為額頭在痛」的原因，應選表原因的連接詞 Because。Although 雖然、Unless 除非、Until 直到，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["trust", "treat", "trace", "trade"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前句說腦凍結無害，後句提出用舌頭抵住上顎或喝溫水的方法，可知它很容易「處理、治療」，應選 treat。trust 信任、trace 追蹤、trade 交易，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["in the first place", "at the last minute", "by chance", "for nothing"], "answer": 0, "explain": "in the first place 表「從一開始、根本」，句意為慢慢吃冰就能「從一開始」避免疼痛發生，呼應前句 Better still（更好的是）的預防做法。at the last minute 在最後一刻、by chance 偶然地、for nothing 白白地，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C28", "title": "Bringing Back the Dark", "title_zh": "找回黑夜", "level": "B2", "topic": "environment", "passage": "For most of human history, people could look up at night and see thousands of stars. Today, however, more than a third of the world's population can no longer see the Milky Way from where they live. The reason is light pollution, the __1__ use of artificial light at night.\n\nLight pollution does more than hide the stars. It also confuses wildlife. Baby sea turtles, for example, find their way to the sea by heading toward the brightest horizon, which is usually the moonlit ocean. Near beaches lit by streetlights and hotels, __2__, many hatchlings crawl toward land instead and die. Bright lights in cities also attract birds and insects, __3__ their natural behavior.\n\nFortunately, this problem is easier to solve than many others. Unlike plastic waste, light pollution disappears the moment the lights __4__. Some places have already taken action to protect their night skies. In 2019, Hehuanshan, a mountain area in central Taiwan, __5__ the island's first International Dark Sky Park after efforts to reduce unnecessary lighting there. Since then, it has attracted many stargazers.", "passage_zh": "在人類歷史的大部分時間裡，人們晚上抬頭就能看見成千上萬顆星星。然而如今，全世界超過三分之一的人口在自己住的地方已經看不到銀河了。原因就是光害，也就是夜間過度使用人造光。\n\n光害不只會遮蔽星星，也會讓野生動物迷失方向。舉例來說，剛孵化的小海龜是朝著最明亮的地平線前進來找到大海，而那通常是映著月光的海面。然而，在被路燈和飯店照亮的海灘附近，許多小海龜反而爬向陸地而死亡。城市裡明亮的燈光也會吸引鳥類和昆蟲，擾亂牠們的自然行為。\n\n幸好，這個問題比其他許多問題更容易解決。和塑膠垃圾不同，燈一熄滅，光害就會立刻消失。有些地方已經採取行動來保護夜空。2019 年，位於台灣中部山區的合歡山在努力減少當地不必要的照明後，成為全台第一座國際暗空公園。從那時起，它就吸引了許多觀星者。", "blanks": [{"options": ["essential", "excessive", "efficient", "exclusive"], "answer": 1, "explain": "下文說明光害會遮蔽星星、干擾野生動物，可知光害是夜間「過度」使用人造光，應選 excessive（過度的）。essential 必要的、efficient 有效率的、exclusive 獨有的、排外的，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["however", "similarly", "moreover", "otherwise"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說小海龜通常朝著映著月光的海面前進；本句說在有路燈和飯店照明的海灘附近，許多小海龜「反而」爬向陸地而死亡，前後語意相反，應選 however（然而）。similarly 同樣地、moreover 此外、otherwise 否則，皆無法表達前後相反的語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["disrupted", "to disrupt", "disrupts", "disrupting"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格前為完整句子，逗號後接分詞片語表示附帶的結果，燈光「擾亂」動物的行為為主動，應用現在分詞 disrupting（= which disrupts）。disrupted 表被動；to disrupt 表目的，語意不合；disrupts 會使兩個動詞之間缺少連接詞。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["take off", "go out", "give in", "show up"], "answer": 1, "explain": "go out 表「（燈、火）熄滅」；the moment + 子句 表「一……就……」，句意為燈一熄滅，光害就立刻消失。take off 起飛、脫下，give in 讓步、屈服，show up 出現，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["has become", "becomes", "became", "had become"], "answer": 2, "explain": "本句有明確的過去時間 In 2019，動詞須用過去簡單式 became。has become 為現在完成式，不能與明確的過去時間連用；becomes 為現在式；had become 為過去完成式，皆不合。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C29", "title": "Traffic Jam in Space", "title_zh": "太空大塞車", "level": "B2", "topic": "technology", "passage": "Since the first satellite was launched in 1957, humans have sent thousands of objects into orbit. Many of these satellites stopped working long ago, __1__ they are still circling Earth. Together with broken rocket parts and tiny pieces from past collisions, they form what is known as space junk.\n\nEven a small piece of junk can be dangerous. Objects in low orbit travel at about 28,000 kilometers per hour, so a fragment no bigger than a coin can hit harder than a bullet. In fact, the International Space Station has had to change its path many times to __2__ being hit. Some scientists also worry about a chain reaction: one crash creates more pieces, __3__ in turn cause more crashes, until some orbits become too crowded to use.\n\nTo prevent this, engineers are testing ways to __4__ the mess, such as using nets, magnets and even harpoons to catch old satellites. Many companies are also designing satellites that will fall back into the atmosphere and burn up at the end of their lives. __5__ the problem is solved, space may one day become too dangerous for us to explore.", "passage_zh": "自從 1957 年第一顆人造衛星發射以來，人類已將數千個物體送入軌道。其中許多衛星早已停止運作，卻仍在繞著地球運行。這些衛星連同損壞的火箭零件，以及過去碰撞所產生的微小碎片，形成了所謂的太空垃圾。\n\n即使是一小塊太空垃圾也可能很危險。低軌道上的物體以每小時約兩萬八千公里的速度移動，因此一片不比硬幣大的碎片，撞擊力道就可能比子彈還強。事實上，國際太空站已經多次必須改變航道，以避免被撞擊。有些科學家也擔心會發生連鎖反應：一次撞擊會產生更多碎片，這些碎片又會引發更多撞擊，直到某些軌道變得太擁擠而無法使用。\n\n為了防止這種情況，工程師正在測試清理這團混亂的方法，例如用網子、磁鐵，甚至魚叉來捕捉舊衛星。許多公司現在也在設計會在壽命結束時落回大氣層燒毀的衛星。除非這個問題得到解決，否則太空有一天可能會變得太危險，讓我們無法探索。", "blanks": [{"options": ["yet", "so", "because", "unless"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前半句說許多衛星早已停止運作，後半句說它們仍在繞地球運行，語意有轉折，應選對等連接詞 yet（但是、然而）。so 所以、because 因為、unless 除非，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["admit", "avoid", "enjoy", "deny"], "answer": 1, "explain": "國際太空站改變航道是為了「避免」被撞擊，avoid + V-ing 表「避免……」，avoid being hit 即「避免被撞」。admit 承認、enjoy 享受、deny 否認，後面雖然也接 V-ing，但語意不通。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["what", "it", "which", "who"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格引導非限定關係子句，修飾先行詞 more pieces（物），並當子句中 cause 的主詞，應用 which；複數動詞 cause 也與 pieces 一致。what 不可有先行詞；it 會使兩個子句缺少連接詞，且單複數不符；who 用於人。in turn 意為「轉而、接著」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["make up", "set up", "look up", "clean up"], "answer": 3, "explain": "clean up 表「清理、清除」，clean up the mess 即「清理這團混亂」，後面提到用網子、磁鐵和魚叉捕捉舊衛星，正是清理的方法。make up 編造、組成，set up 設立，look up 查詢，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Once", "Unless", "Because", "Although"], "answer": 1, "explain": "句意為「除非這個問題得到解決，否則太空有一天可能會變得太危險而無法探索」，應選 Unless（除非）。Once 一旦、Because 因為、Although 雖然，代入後語意皆不合邏輯。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C30", "title": "Fighting Mosquitoes with Mosquitoes", "title_zh": "以蚊制蚊", "level": "B2", "topic": "science", "passage": "Every summer, dengue fever becomes a serious concern in southern Taiwan. The disease is __1__ by mosquitoes, and there is still no medicine that can cure it. For years, the main ways to fight it have been spraying chemicals and removing standing water where mosquitoes lay their eggs.\n\nScientists, however, have found a surprising new weapon: more mosquitoes. These insects have been given a natural bacterium called Wolbachia, which __2__ it much harder for the dengue virus to grow inside them. When they are released into the wild, they mate with local mosquitoes and __3__ the bacterium on to the next generation. Over time, most of the mosquitoes in the area carry Wolbachia, and fewer people get sick.\n\nThe results have been impressive. In a large trial in Yogyakarta, Indonesia, dengue cases dropped by 77 percent in areas where the mosquitoes __4__. Because the method uses no harmful chemicals and keeps working for years, many countries are now trying it. It may sound strange, but releasing mosquitoes could one day help us __5__ one of the world's most common tropical diseases.", "passage_zh": "每年夏天，登革熱都會成為台灣南部嚴重的隱憂。這種疾病是由蚊子傳播的，而且目前仍沒有能治癒它的藥物。多年來，對抗登革熱的主要方法一直是噴灑化學藥劑，以及清除蚊子產卵的積水。\n\n然而，科學家發現了一種出人意料的新武器：更多的蚊子。這些蚊子被植入了一種名為沃爾巴克氏菌的天然細菌，它會讓登革病毒很難在蚊子體內增生。當這些蚊子被野放後，牠們會與當地的蚊子交配，並把這種細菌傳給下一代。隨著時間過去，該地區大部分的蚊子都帶有沃爾巴克氏菌，生病的人也就變少了。\n\n成果相當驚人。在印尼日惹的一項大型試驗中，有野放這種蚊子的地區，登革熱病例減少了百分之七十七。由於這種方法不使用有害的化學藥劑，而且能持續發揮作用好幾年，許多國家現在都在嘗試採用。這聽起來或許很奇怪，但野放蚊子有一天可能幫助我們戰勝世界上最常見的熱帶疾病之一。", "blanks": [{"options": ["cured", "prevented", "spread", "discovered"], "answer": 2, "explain": "登革熱是「由蚊子傳播」的疾病，be spread by 表被動「被……傳播」，下文也提到以清除蚊子產卵的積水來防治。cured 治癒（與後面「沒有藥物能治癒」矛盾）、prevented 預防、discovered 發現，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["makes", "lets", "allows", "keeps"], "answer": 0, "explain": "make + it + 形容詞 + for sb + to V 為虛受詞句型，表「使某人做某事變得……」；which makes it much harder for the dengue virus to grow 即「使登革病毒更難增生」。lets 與 allows 後面須接原形動詞或 to V，不能接形容詞；keeps 意為「保持」，但病毒原本並不難增生，語意不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["take", "turn", "hold", "pass"], "answer": 3, "explain": "pass sth on to sb 表「把某物傳給某人」，pass the bacterium on to the next generation 即「把細菌傳給下一代」。take on 承擔、turn on 打開、hold on 堅持、稍等，皆不能與 to the next generation 搭配。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["released", "were released", "have released", "were releasing"], "answer": 1, "explain": "蚊子是「被」野放的，關係子句 where the mosquitoes ___ 須用被動語態，且敘述的是過去進行的試驗，應用過去式被動 were released。released 與 have released 為主動，變成蚊子去野放別的東西；were releasing 為過去進行式主動，皆不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["spread", "describe", "defeat", "diagnose"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前文說明野放帶菌蚊子能大幅減少登革熱病例，可知此法有一天可能幫助我們「戰勝」這種熱帶疾病，應選 defeat（擊敗、戰勝）。spread 散播、describe 描述、diagnose 診斷，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C31", "title": "My First Part-Time Job", "title_zh": "我的第一份打工", "level": "B1", "topic": "work", "passage": "Last summer, seventeen-year-old Kai-wen got his first part-time job at a convenience store near his home. He thought the work would be easy, but he soon __1__ that a clerk does much more than sell snacks and drinks. In a single shift, he had to help customers pay their phone bills, print documents, and pick up packages they had ordered online.\n\nThe hardest part was the night shift on weekends. The store was __2__ busy after midnight that he barely had time to drink water. Once, a tired customer shouted at him because the coffee machine was broken. Kai-wen wanted to argue, but he took a deep breath and offered the man a free cup of tea __3__.\n\nBy the end of the summer, Kai-wen had saved enough money to buy a new laptop. More importantly, he had learned how to stay calm under __4__. \"I used to think working was only about money,\" he says. \"Now I know it's also a way to grow up.\" He is already looking forward to __5__ at the store again next summer.", "passage_zh": "去年暑假，十七歲的凱文在家附近的一家便利商店找到了人生第一份打工。他原本以為這份工作很輕鬆，但很快就發現店員要做的事遠不只是賣零食和飲料。在一個班次裡，他得幫顧客繳電話費、列印文件，還要幫他們領取網購的包裹。\n\n最辛苦的是週末的大夜班。午夜過後店裡忙得不得了，他幾乎連喝水的時間都沒有。有一次，一位疲憊的顧客因為咖啡機故障而對他大吼。凱文很想跟對方爭辯，但他深吸一口氣，改為請那位男士喝一杯免費的茶。\n\n暑假結束時，凱文已經存夠錢買了一台新筆電。更重要的是，他學會了如何在壓力下保持冷靜。「我以前以為工作只是為了賺錢，」他說，「現在我知道它也是一種成長的方式。」他已經很期待明年暑假再回到這家店工作了。", "blanks": [{"options": ["pretended", "realized", "doubted", "forgot"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由前句 He thought the work would be easy, but… 及後文 a clerk does much more than sell snacks and drinks 可知，他原本以為工作輕鬆，後來才「發現」店員的工作遠不只如此。realize that…「了解到、發現」。pretend 假裝；doubt 懷疑；forget 忘記，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["such", "too", "very", "so"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格後為形容詞 busy，再接 that 子句表結果，應用 so + adj. + that…「如此…以致於…」。such 後須接名詞片語；too 須搭配 to V（太…而不能…）；very 不可接 that 子句表結果。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["instead", "either", "already", "otherwise"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由 Kai-wen wanted to argue, but… 可知，他沒有和顧客爭辯，而是「改為」請對方喝免費的茶，故用副詞 instead「作為替代、反而」。either 用於否定句表「也（不）」；already 已經；otherwise 否則，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["control", "construction", "pressure", "arrest"], "answer": 2, "explain": "under pressure 為固定搭配，意為「在壓力下」；stay calm under pressure 即「在壓力下保持冷靜」，呼應前文被顧客大吼仍冷靜應對。under control 在控制之中；under construction 施工中；under arrest 被逮捕，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["work", "worked", "working", "to work"], "answer": 2, "explain": "look forward to「期待」中的 to 為介系詞，後面須接名詞或動名詞，故用 working。work、worked 與 to work 皆不符合此用法。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C32", "title": "Why Free Samples Work", "title_zh": "免費試吃為何有效", "level": "B2", "topic": "business", "passage": "If you have ever walked through a big supermarket on a weekend, you have probably been offered a free sample of cheese, sausage, or juice. It may seem strange that stores give away food for nothing, but this simple practice is __1__ one of the most effective marketing tools in retail.\n\nPart of the reason is psychological. When someone gives us a gift, even a small one, we tend to feel that we owe them something in return. Psychologists call this feeling \"reciprocity.\" A shopper who has just enjoyed a free bite of dumpling may feel a little guilty walking away __2__ buying anything. In addition, samples remove the risk of trying something new. Customers who are __3__ about an unfamiliar flavor can taste it first, without spending a single dollar.\n\nSamples also help brands __4__ out in crowded stores, where dozens of similar products compete for attention on the same shelf. Some companies have reported that sales of a product __5__ sharply on days when samples are offered. For businesses, a few plates of free food may be one of the cheapest forms of advertising available.", "passage_zh": "如果你曾在週末逛過大型超市，你很可能被請過試吃一小塊起司、香腸，或試喝果汁。商店免費送出食物似乎很奇怪，但這個簡單的做法其實是零售業中最有效的行銷手法之一。\n\n部分原因與心理有關。當有人送我們禮物，即使只是小禮物，我們往往會覺得自己欠對方一份人情。心理學家把這種感覺稱為「互惠」。一位剛享用完一口免費水餃的顧客，如果什麼都沒買就走開，可能會覺得有點內疚。此外，試吃也消除了嘗試新事物的風險。對陌生口味沒有把握的顧客可以先嚐嚐看，不必花一毛錢。\n\n試吃也能幫助品牌在擁擠的商店中脫穎而出，因為在同一個貨架上，有數十種類似的產品在爭取顧客的注意。有些公司表示，在提供試吃的日子裡，產品的銷售量會大幅上升。對企業來說，幾盤免費食物也許是現有最便宜的廣告形式之一。", "blanks": [{"options": ["actually", "hardly", "rarely", "barely"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由 It may seem strange…, but… 可知，雖然免費送食物看似奇怪，但這個做法「其實」很有效，故用 actually「事實上、其實」。hardly、rarely、barely 皆帶否定意味（幾乎不、很少），與後文說明試吃效果很好的內容矛盾。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["despite", "besides", "without", "unless"], "answer": 2, "explain": "依前文「互惠」心理，顧客吃了免費試吃後，若「沒有」買任何東西就離開，會感到內疚。without + V-ing「沒有…」。despite 儘管；besides 除了…之外還；unless 除非（為連接詞，後接子句），皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["confident", "uncertain", "enthusiastic", "generous"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由前句 samples remove the risk of trying something new 可知，試吃能幫助對陌生口味「沒有把握」的顧客降低風險，be uncertain about「對…不確定」。confident 有信心的；enthusiastic 熱衷的；generous 慷慨的，皆無「風險」可言，不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["give", "drop", "run", "stand"], "answer": 3, "explain": "由後文 where dozens of similar products compete for attention 可知，試吃能幫助品牌在眾多類似商品中「脫穎而出」，stand out「突出、引人注目」。give out 分發；drop out 退出；run out 用完，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["raise", "rise", "rises", "raising"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格為 that 子句的動詞，主詞 sales 為複數，且後面沒有受詞，應用不及物動詞 rise「上升」。raise 為及物動詞，須接受詞；rises 用於單數主詞；raising 為動名詞或分詞，不能當子句的主要動詞。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C33", "title": "The Wrong Drink", "title_zh": "送錯的飲料", "level": "B1", "topic": "customer service", "passage": "Mei-ling works at a busy bubble tea shop called Sunny Cup. One afternoon, a customer came back to the counter looking upset. She had ordered a green tea with no sugar, but the drink she __1__ was very sweet. \"I'm trying to stay healthy,\" she said. \"I can't drink this.\"\n\nMei-ling felt nervous, but she remembered what her manager had taught her: listen first, and never blame the customer. __2__ explaining that the shop had been crowded, she simply said, \"I'm so sorry about that. Let me make you a new one right away.\" She also gave the customer a coupon __3__ she could use on her next visit.\n\nThe customer left with a smile, and a week later she returned with three friends. That evening, Mei-ling's manager praised her for handling the problem so well. \"Everyone makes mistakes,\" he said. \"What matters is how we __4__ them.\" Mei-ling realized that good customer service is not about being perfect. It is about making people feel __5__ and respected.", "passage_zh": "美玲在一家名叫「晴天杯」、生意興隆的手搖飲料店工作。某天下午，一位顧客一臉不悅地回到櫃檯。她點的是無糖綠茶，但拿到的飲料卻非常甜。「我正在努力保持健康，」她說，「這杯我不能喝。」\n\n美玲很緊張，但她記得店長教過她的話：先傾聽，而且絕不責怪顧客。她沒有解釋說店裡剛才很擁擠，只是說：「真的很抱歉，我馬上幫您重做一杯。」她還送給這位顧客一張下次光臨時可以使用的折價券。\n\n這位顧客笑著離開了，一週後還帶著三個朋友再度光臨。那天晚上，店長稱讚美玲把問題處理得很好。「每個人都會犯錯，」他說，「重要的是我們如何處理錯誤。」美玲明白了，好的顧客服務並不在於完美無缺，而在於讓人覺得受到重視與尊重。", "blanks": [{"options": ["received", "has received", "receives", "was received"], "answer": 0, "explain": "本段描述過去發生的事（One afternoon… came back），且主詞 she「收到」飲料為主動，應用過去式 received。has received 為現在完成式、receives 為現在式，時態不符；was received 為被動語態，與主詞 she 不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["Because of", "Instead of", "In spite of", "In addition to"], "answer": 1, "explain": "店長教導要「先傾聽、絕不責怪顧客」，因此美玲「沒有」解釋店裡很忙，而是直接道歉，instead of + V-ing「而不是…」。because of 因為；in spite of 儘管；in addition to 除了…之外還（表示她也解釋了，與 simply said 矛盾），皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["who", "whose", "that", "what"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格引導形容詞子句修飾先行詞 a coupon（物），且在子句中作 use 的受詞，應用關係代名詞 that（或 which）。who 用於人；whose 後須接名詞；what 本身已含先行詞，前面不可再有先行詞。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["put up with", "come up with", "keep up with", "deal with"], "answer": 3, "explain": "店長說「每個人都會犯錯，重要的是我們如何『處理』錯誤」，deal with「處理」。put up with 忍受；come up with 想出；keep up with 跟上，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["ignored", "confused", "bored", "valued"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格與 respected 以 and 連接，語意須一致且為正面，make people feel valued and respected「讓人覺得受到重視與尊重」。ignored 被忽視的；confused 困惑的；bored 感到無聊的，皆為負面，與好的顧客服務不符。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C34", "title": "Wishes in the Night Sky", "title_zh": "夜空中的心願", "level": "B2", "topic": "culture and festivals", "passage": "Every year around the Lantern Festival, thousands of visitors crowd into Pingxi, a small mountain town in New Taipei, to release sky lanterns. Before letting them go, people write their wishes on the paper sides with brushes: hopes for good grades, good health, or a successful year. Then the lanterns, __1__ by a small fire inside, rise slowly into the dark sky.\n\nThe tradition has a practical origin. According to local legend, villagers in the area once fled into the mountains to hide from bandits. When the danger had passed, those who had stayed behind released lanterns to let the others know it was safe to return home. __2__, the lanterns became a symbol of peace and good news.\n\nIn recent years, __3__, the custom has raised environmental concerns. Many lanterns come down in forests and rivers, where their wire frames can harm wildlife, and some even start fires. To __4__ the problem, local authorities have encouraged residents to collect fallen lanterns in exchange for small rewards, and some makers now use materials that break down more easily. These efforts show that a festival can be preserved __5__ it also respects nature.", "passage_zh": "每年元宵節前後，成千上萬的遊客湧入新北市的山城小鎮平溪施放天燈。在放手之前，人們會用毛筆在燈的紙面上寫下心願：希望成績進步、身體健康，或是一整年順順利利。接著，被燈內小火加熱的天燈便緩緩升上漆黑的夜空。\n\n這項傳統其實源自實際的需要。根據地方傳說，當地村民過去曾逃進山裡躲避土匪。危險解除後，留守的村民便施放天燈，通知其他人可以安心返家了。久而久之，天燈成了平安與好消息的象徵。\n\n然而，近年來這項習俗也引發了環保方面的疑慮。許多天燈掉落在森林和河川中，其鐵絲骨架可能傷害野生動物，有些天燈甚至會引發火災。為了解決這個問題，地方政府鼓勵居民撿拾掉落的天燈以換取小獎勵，有些業者也開始改用較容易分解的材料。這些努力顯示，只要節慶也同時尊重自然，就能保存下來。", "blanks": [{"options": ["heating", "heated", "to heat", "are heated"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格與前後逗號構成插入的分詞片語，修飾 the lanterns；天燈是「被」燈內的小火加熱，且後有 by，應用過去分詞 heated 表被動。heating 表主動；to heat 表目的；are heated 為動詞，會使句中出現兩個主要動詞（rise）。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Over time", "In contrast", "For instance", "On the other hand"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前文說明天燈原本用來通知村民平安返家，後句說天燈「漸漸地」成為平安與好消息的象徵，表示隨時間演變，應用 Over time「久而久之」。In contrast 相較之下；For instance 例如；On the other hand 另一方面，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["therefore", "besides", "however", "for example"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前兩段介紹天燈美好的意義與由來，本段轉而談環保疑慮，語意轉折，應用 however「然而」。therefore 因此；besides 此外；for example 例如，皆無轉折意味。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["worsen", "overlook", "witness", "tackle"], "answer": 3, "explain": "由後文鼓勵居民撿拾天燈、業者改用易分解材料可知，這些做法是為了「處理、解決」問題，tackle「處理、對付」。worsen 使惡化；overlook 忽略；witness 目擊，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["unless", "as long as", "even though", "so that"], "answer": 1, "explain": "本句意為「只要節慶也尊重自然，就能被保存下來」，表條件，應用 as long as「只要」。unless 除非（＝if not），語意相反；even though 即使；so that 以便，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C35", "title": "Working Four Days a Week", "title_zh": "一週工作四天", "level": "B2", "topic": "work", "passage": "For about a hundred years, the five-day working week has been the standard in many countries. Recently, however, a growing number of companies have begun to question __1__ people really need to work so many hours to get their jobs done.\n\nIn 2022, about sixty British companies took part in a six-month experiment. Their employees worked four days a week __2__ receiving the same salary as before. In return, the workers promised to keep their productivity at the same level. To make this possible, many teams cut down on long meetings and found smarter ways to __3__ their tasks.\n\nThe results surprised many observers. Many employees reported feeling less stressed, and fewer of them took sick days. __4__, the companies did not lose money: their income stayed roughly the same, and most of them decided to keep the shorter week after the trial ended. Of course, a four-day week may not suit every industry. Still, the experiment suggests that working longer does not __5__ mean working better.", "passage_zh": "大約一百年來，一週工作五天一直是許多國家的標準。然而最近，有越來越多公司開始質疑，人們是否真的需要工作這麼多個小時才能完成工作。\n\n2022年，大約六十家英國公司參與了一項為期六個月的實驗。這些公司的員工每週只工作四天，同時領取和以前一樣的薪水。作為交換，員工承諾將生產力維持在同樣的水準。為了做到這一點，許多團隊減少了冗長的會議，並找出更聰明的方法來處理工作。\n\n實驗結果令許多觀察者感到意外。許多員工表示壓力減輕了，請病假的人也變少了。此外，公司並沒有虧損：營收大致維持不變，而且大多數公司在實驗結束後決定繼續實施較短的工作週。當然，一週工作四天未必適合每個產業。儘管如此，這項實驗顯示，工作時間更長未必代表工作成效更好。", "blanks": [{"options": ["that", "whether", "what", "which"], "answer": 1, "explain": "question 作動詞時意為「質疑」，後接 whether 子句表「質疑是否…」，本句意為「質疑人們是否真的需要工作這麼多小時」。that 子句表肯定的事實，沒有「是否」之意，不合質疑是否的句意；what 與 which 引導的名詞子句中須缺主詞或受詞，但此處子句結構完整。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["while", "unless", "until", "since"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由後句 In return, the workers promised to keep their productivity… 可知，員工一週工作四天的「同時」仍領同樣的薪水，while + V-ing「在…的同時」。unless 除非；until 直到；since 自從，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["abandon", "postpone", "exaggerate", "manage"], "answer": 3, "explain": "為了在較短時間內維持相同的生產力，團隊減少冗長會議，並找出更聰明的方法來「處理、管理」工作，manage「處理、管理」。abandon 放棄；postpone 延後；exaggerate 誇大，皆與維持生產力的目標不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Otherwise", "For instance", "Moreover", "Instead"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前句提到員工壓力減輕、請病假者減少，後句再補充公司也沒有虧損，兩者皆為實驗的正面結果，屬於附加說明，應用 Moreover「此外」。Otherwise 否則；For instance 例如；Instead 反而，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["necessarily", "hardly", "rarely", "never"], "answer": 0, "explain": "not necessarily 意為「未必、不一定」，本句意為「工作時間更長未必代表工作成效更好」。hardly、rarely、never 本身已具否定意味，與 does not 連用會形成雙重否定，文法錯誤。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C36", "title": "Grandma's Pineapple Cakes Go Online", "title_zh": "阿嬤的鳳梨酥上網了", "level": "B1", "topic": "business", "passage": "For forty years, Mrs. Lin has sold handmade pineapple cakes in a tiny shop in Changhua. Her cakes are famous among local people, but the shop is on a quiet street, and __1__ tourists ever find it. When the pandemic hit in 2020, business became even worse, and she almost decided to close the shop.\n\nThat was when her granddaughter, Yu-han, came up with an idea. She opened an online store for the shop and posted short videos __2__ how her grandmother makes each cake by hand. In one video, Mrs. Lin explained that she uses fresh pineapples __3__ cheaper winter melon jam, which many factories use. The video was shared thousands of times.\n\nWithin a few months, orders began to arrive from all over Taiwan. Now the family ships hundreds of boxes every week, and Yu-han has hired two part-time workers so that they can __4__ up with the demand. \"I never thought my old recipe could reach so many people,\" Mrs. Lin says. Her story shows that even a traditional business can __5__ in the digital age.", "passage_zh": "四十年來，林奶奶一直在彰化的一間小店裡販賣手工鳳梨酥。她的鳳梨酥在當地人之間很有名，但店開在一條安靜的街上，很少有觀光客找得到。2020年疫情爆發時，生意變得更差，她幾乎決定要把店收掉。\n\n就在那時，她的孫女雨涵想到了一個點子。她幫這家店開了一間網路商店，並上傳短片，展示奶奶如何一個一個親手製作鳳梨酥。在其中一支影片裡，林奶奶說明她使用的是新鮮鳳梨，而不是許多工廠所用、較便宜的冬瓜醬。這支影片被分享了數千次。\n\n短短幾個月內，訂單開始從台灣各地湧入。如今這家人每週要寄出數百盒鳳梨酥，雨涵還請了兩名兼職人員，好讓他們能跟上訂單的需求。「我從沒想過我的老配方能讓這麼多人吃到，」林奶奶說。她的故事顯示，即使是傳統的生意，也能在數位時代蓬勃發展。", "blanks": [{"options": ["a few", "little", "few", "many"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由前句 the shop is on a quiet street 可知，「很少」觀光客找得到這家店；tourists 為可數名詞，應用帶否定意味的 few「很少」。a few 表肯定「有一些」；little 修飾不可數名詞；many 與文意矛盾。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["show", "showing", "shown", "to be shown"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格引導分詞片語修飾 short videos，影片「展示」奶奶如何製作鳳梨酥，為主動，且後有受詞 how 子句，應用現在分詞 showing（＝which show）。show 為原形動詞，不可直接修飾名詞；shown 與 to be shown 表被動，但影片是主動展示做法，how 子句是 show 的受詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["instead of", "because of", "in case of", "according to"], "answer": 0, "explain": "林奶奶強調她使用新鮮鳳梨，「而不是」許多工廠所用、較便宜的冬瓜醬，以突顯產品品質，instead of「而不是」。because of 因為；in case of 萬一；according to 根據，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["make", "keep", "give", "turn"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由前句每週要寄出數百盒可知訂單量大，需雇人幫忙才能「跟上」需求，keep up with「跟上」。make up with 與…和好；give up 放棄；turn up 出現，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["disappear", "struggle", "retire", "thrive"], "answer": 3, "explain": "林奶奶的店從幾乎收掉到訂單來自全台各地，可知傳統生意也能在數位時代「蓬勃發展」，thrive「興盛、繁榮」。disappear 消失；struggle 掙扎；retire 退休，皆與文中正面的結果不符。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C37", "title": "To Tip or Not to Tip?", "title_zh": "給不給小費？", "level": "B2", "topic": "customer service", "passage": "Few customs confuse international travelers more than tipping. In the United States, diners are generally expected to leave a tip of 15 to 20 percent of the bill at restaurants. This is because many servers there are paid a low hourly wage, so tips __1__ a large part of their income. A customer who leaves nothing may be seen as rude, __2__ the service was excellent or just average.\n\nIn Japan, __3__, tipping is not part of the culture. Good service is considered a basic part of the job, not something extra that customers must pay for. Visitors who leave coins on the table are sometimes surprised when a waiter runs after them to return the money, __4__ that they have forgotten it.\n\nTaiwan falls somewhere in between. Most local restaurants do not expect tips, but many upscale ones add a 10 percent service charge to the bill. Before traveling abroad, therefore, it is wise to learn the local rules. A little research can __5__ you from an embarrassing moment and help you show respect for the people who serve you.", "passage_zh": "很少有習俗比小費更讓國際旅客困惑。在美國，在餐廳用餐的客人一般應該留下帳單金額百分之十五到二十的小費。這是因為當地許多服務生的時薪很低，所以小費占了他們收入的一大部分。不論服務是非常好還是普普通通，什麼都沒留下的客人都可能被認為沒禮貌。\n\n相較之下，在日本，給小費並不是當地文化的一部分。良好的服務被視為工作的基本要求，而不是客人必須額外付費的東西。在桌上留下零錢的遊客，有時會驚訝地看到服務生追出來把錢還給他們，因為服務生以為他們忘了把錢帶走。\n\n台灣則介於兩者之間。大多數本地餐廳並不期待客人給小費，但許多高級餐廳會在帳單上加收百分之十的服務費。因此，出國旅行前，最好先了解當地的規矩。做一點功課就能讓你免於尷尬的場面，也能幫助你對為你服務的人表達尊重。", "blanks": [{"options": ["make up", "take after", "turn down", "give off"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由前文 many servers there are paid a low hourly wage, so… 可知，小費「構成」服務生收入的一大部分，make up「構成、占（比例）」。take after 長得像（長輩）；turn down 拒絕；give off 散發（氣味、光），皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["unless", "whether", "because", "since"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格後有 …excellent or just average，構成 whether A or B「不論是 A 還是 B」的句型，表示不論服務好壞，不給小費都可能被視為無禮。unless 除非；because 與 since 表原因，皆無法與 or 搭配形成此句型，語意也不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["as a result", "in addition", "for this reason", "by contrast"], "answer": 3, "explain": "上段說明美國必須給小費，本段說日本沒有小費文化，前後形成對比，應用 by contrast「相較之下」。as a result 因此；in addition 此外；for this reason 由於這個原因，皆無對比意味。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["believe", "believed", "believing", "to be believed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格引導分詞構句，邏輯主詞為 a waiter，服務生「以為」客人忘了錢，為主動，應用現在分詞 believing（＝because he believes）。believe 為原形動詞，不能直接接在逗號後修飾；believed 與 to be believed 表被動，不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["remind", "inform", "save", "accuse"], "answer": 2, "explain": "save sb from + N/V-ing「使某人免於…」，本句意為「做一點功課能讓你免於尷尬的場面」。remind sb of 提醒；inform sb of 通知；accuse sb of 指控，皆不與 from 搭配，語意也不合。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C38", "title": "Splashing into the New Year", "title_zh": "潑水迎新年", "level": "B1", "topic": "culture and festivals", "passage": "If you visit Thailand in mid-April, be ready to get wet. From April 13 to 15, people across the country celebrate Songkran, the traditional Thai New Year. During the festival, streets __1__ into giant battlegrounds for water fights. Children and adults alike carry buckets and water guns, and no one who walks outside can stay dry.\n\nThe water fights may look like pure fun, but the festival has a deeper meaning. Traditionally, people gently pour scented water over the hands of their elders to show respect and ask for their blessings. Water is believed to wash away bad luck from the past year, __2__ everyone can start the new year fresh. Many families also visit temples, __3__ they clean statues of the Buddha and offer food to monks.\n\nSongkran also comes at the perfect time. April is usually the hottest month of the year in Thailand, so most people are happy to be __4__ with cold water. In 2023, UNESCO added the festival to its list of Intangible Cultural Heritage. If you ever join the celebration, remember one rule: smile, and don't get angry __5__ you get soaked!", "passage_zh": "如果你在四月中旬造訪泰國，就要有被淋濕的準備。從四月十三日到十五日，全國各地的人們都會慶祝潑水節，也就是泰國的傳統新年。節慶期間，街道會變成巨大的潑水戰場。不論大人小孩都提著水桶、拿著水槍，只要走到戶外，沒有人能保持乾爽。\n\n潑水大戰看起來也許純粹是為了好玩，但這個節日其實有更深層的意義。傳統上，人們會把帶有香氣的水輕輕澆在長輩的手上，以表達敬意並祈求祝福。人們相信水能洗去過去一年的霉運，讓每個人都能以全新的面貌迎接新年。許多家庭也會到寺廟去，在那裡清洗佛像，並供養食物給僧侶。\n\n潑水節的時機也恰到好處。四月通常是泰國一年中最熱的月份，所以大多數人都很樂意被潑冷水。2023年，聯合國教科文組織將這個節日列入非物質文化遺產名錄。如果你有機會參加這場慶典，記得一個規則：當你被淋得全身濕透時，要保持微笑，千萬別生氣！", "blanks": [{"options": ["turn", "look", "run", "break"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由後句大人小孩都提著水桶、拿著水槍可知，節慶期間街道「變成」大型潑水戰場，turn into「變成」。look into 調查；run into 偶遇；break into 闖入、突然開始（某動作），皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["even if", "so that", "as if", "unless"], "answer": 1, "explain": "人們相信水能洗去去年的霉運，「以便」大家能有全新的開始，表目的，應用 so that「以便、好讓」。even if 即使；as if 彷彿；unless 除非，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["which", "when", "where", "what"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格引導非限定形容詞子句修飾表地點的 temples，且子句 they clean statues… 結構完整、不缺主詞或受詞，應用關係副詞 where（＝at which）。which 在子句中須作主詞或受詞；when 修飾時間；what 前面不可有先行詞。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["splashing", "splash", "to splash", "splashed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "天氣炎熱，人們樂意「被」潑冷水，應用被動語態 be + p.p.，即 be splashed with cold water。splashing 表主動進行；splash 與 to splash 不能接在 be 之後。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["until", "unless", "before", "when"], "answer": 3, "explain": "本句意為「當你被淋濕時，要微笑、別生氣」，應用 when 引導時間副詞子句。until 直到；unless 除非；before 在…之前，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C39", "title": "Ready for the Interview", "title_zh": "準備好面試了", "level": "B1", "topic": "work", "passage": "Hsin-yi, a high school senior, had her first job interview last Saturday at a small café called Morning Leaf. She wanted to work there during the winter vacation, so she spent the whole week __1__ for it.\n\nFirst, she looked at the café's website to learn about its menu and history. Then she practiced answering common questions with her older brother, who __2__ as a hotel manager for five years. He reminded her to speak clearly and to look at the interviewer when answering. \"Don't just memorize your answers,\" he said. \"Be honest and let them see who you really are.\"\n\nOn the day of the interview, Hsin-yi arrived ten minutes early. __3__ she was nervous, she smiled and introduced herself confidently. When the owner asked why she wanted the job, she explained that she loved making people happy with good coffee and food. At the end, she asked a question of her own: \"What do your best employees have in __4__?\" The owner looked impressed. Two days later, Hsin-yi received a phone call. She had __5__ the job!", "passage_zh": "高三學生心怡上週六在一家名叫「晨葉」的小咖啡館參加了人生第一次工作面試。她想在寒假期間到那裡打工，所以花了一整個星期做準備。\n\n首先，她瀏覽了咖啡館的網站，了解店裡的菜單和歷史。接著，她和哥哥一起練習回答常見的面試問題；她哥哥已經當了五年的飯店經理。哥哥提醒她說話要清楚，回答問題時要看著面試官。「別只是死背答案，」他說，「要誠實，讓他們看見真正的你。」\n\n面試當天，心怡提早十分鐘抵達。雖然她很緊張，她還是面帶微笑，充滿自信地自我介紹。當老闆問她為什麼想要這份工作時，她解釋說她喜歡用好咖啡和美食讓人開心。面試最後，她也提出了一個自己的問題：「您最優秀的員工有什麼共同點？」老闆看起來印象深刻。兩天後，心怡接到了一通電話——她得到這份工作了！", "blanks": [{"options": ["prepare", "to prepare", "preparing", "prepared"], "answer": 2, "explain": "spend + 時間 + (in) V-ing「花時間做某事」，故用 preparing。prepare、to prepare 與 prepared 皆不符合此句型。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["works", "has worked", "is working", "will work"], "answer": 1, "explain": "句尾 for five years 表示從過去持續到現在的一段時間，應用現在完成式 has worked。works 為現在簡單式、is working 為現在進行式，皆不能與 for + 一段時間連用表示持續到現在；will work 為未來式，不合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Although", "Because", "Unless", "So"], "answer": 0, "explain": "「她很緊張」與「她仍面帶微笑、自信地自我介紹」語意相反，應用表讓步的 Although「雖然」。Because 因為；Unless 除非；So 所以，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["general", "public", "total", "common"], "answer": 3, "explain": "have… in common「有…共同點」，本句意為「您最優秀的員工有什麼共同點？」。in general 一般而言；in public 公開地；in total 總共，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["landed", "refused", "quit", "applied"], "answer": 0, "explain": "由前文老闆印象深刻、兩天後接到電話，以及句尾的驚嘆號可知，她「得到」了這份工作，land a job「得到工作」。refuse 拒絕；quit 辭職；apply 須用 apply for 才表「應徵」，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C40", "title": "Ghost Month in Taiwan", "title_zh": "台灣的鬼月", "level": "B2", "topic": "culture and festivals", "passage": "In Taiwan, the seventh month of the lunar calendar is known as Ghost Month. According to folk belief, the gates of the underworld open at the beginning of the month, __1__ wandering spirits to visit the world of the living. To keep these \"good brothers\" satisfied, families and businesses set out tables of food, drinks, and paper money as offerings.\n\nThe month also comes with many taboos. People are advised not to swim, move house, or whistle at night, since such actions might attract unwanted attention from the spirits. As a __2__, sales of houses and cars often slow down, and few couples choose to get married during this period.\n\nPerhaps the most famous celebration takes place in Keelung. Since the mid-1800s, local family clans have taken turns organizing the city's ceremonies, a tradition that began as a peaceful way to end violent __3__ between groups of immigrants. The highlight is the release of water lanterns into the sea. It is said that the farther a lantern floats, the more luck it brings to the clan __4__ name it carries. For many visitors, the festival is less about fear than __5__ honoring the dead and bringing the community together.", "passage_zh": "在台灣，農曆七月被稱為鬼月。根據民間信仰，鬼門會在這個月初打開，讓四處遊蕩的鬼魂來到陽間。為了讓這些「好兄弟」滿意，家家戶戶和商家都會擺上一桌桌的食物、飲料和紙錢作為供品。\n\n這個月也伴隨著許多禁忌。人們被勸告不要游泳、搬家或在夜裡吹口哨，因為這些行為可能會引來鬼魂不必要的注意。因此，房屋和汽車的銷售往往會趨緩，也很少有情侶選擇在這段期間結婚。\n\n或許最著名的慶典是在基隆舉行。自十九世紀中葉以來，當地各宗族便輪流主辦市內的祭典，這項傳統起初是為了以和平的方式終結移民族群之間的暴力衝突。祭典的重頭戲是將水燈放入海中。據說水燈漂得越遠，就能為燈上所寫姓氏的宗族帶來越多好運。對許多遊客來說，這個節慶與其說是關於恐懼，不如說是關於緬懷亡者、凝聚社區。", "blanks": [{"options": ["allow", "allowing", "allowed", "to be allowed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格前為完整句子，空格引導分詞構句表結果：鬼門打開，「讓」遊蕩的鬼魂來到陽間；此為主動且後有受詞 wandering spirits，應用現在分詞 allowing。allow 為原形動詞，不能直接連接兩個句子；allowed 與 to be allowed 表被動，後面不能再接受詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["result", "whole", "reminder", "favor"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說人們避免搬家等行為，後句說房屋與汽車的銷售因而趨緩，前後為因果關係，應用 as a result「因此、結果」。as a whole 整體而言；as a reminder 作為提醒；as a favor 作為幫忙，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["contracts", "concerts", "conflicts", "conversations"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由 violent（暴力的）與 a peaceful way to end 可知，輪流主辦祭典是為了以和平方式終結移民族群之間的暴力「衝突」，conflict「衝突」。contract 合約；concert 音樂會；conversation 對話，皆無法與 violent 搭配。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["who", "whom", "which", "whose"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格後接名詞 name，且 name 屬於先行詞 the clan（該宗族的姓氏），應用所有格關係代名詞 whose。who、whom、which 後面不能直接接名詞表示所有關係。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["for", "with", "about", "by"], "answer": 2, "explain": "less A than B「與其說是 A，不如說是 B」中，A 與 B 須對等；前面為 about fear，後面亦須用 about honoring…，意為「與其說是關於恐懼，不如說是關於緬懷亡者」。for、with、by 皆無法與 about fear 形成對等結構。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C41", "title": "Learning to Swim at Fifteen", "title_zh": "十五歲才學游泳", "level": "A2", "topic": "sports", "passage": "When I started senior high school, I had a secret: I could not swim. In P.E. class, my classmates jumped into the pool and __1__ from one end to the other. I stayed in the shallow water and held on to the wall.\n\nOne day, my teacher, Mr. Huang, said to me, \"Swimming is not about being strong. It is about being __2__ in the water.\" After that, I practiced at the pool every Tuesday and Thursday after school. Mr. Huang showed me how to breathe and float. At first, I swallowed a lot of water, __3__ I did not give up.\n\nThree months later, I swam 25 meters for the first time. My arms and legs were very tired, but I felt really __4__ of myself. Now I go to the pool every weekend. Next summer, I am going to __5__ in the sea at Kenting with my family.", "passage_zh": "我剛上高中的時候，有一個祕密：我不會游泳。上體育課時，同學們跳進泳池，從這一頭游到另一頭，我卻只敢待在淺水區，緊緊抓著池邊。\n\n有一天，黃老師對我說：「游泳靠的不是力氣大，而是在水裡保持放鬆。」從那以後，我每個星期二和星期四放學後都到游泳池練習。黃老師教我怎麼換氣和漂浮。一開始我喝了好多水，但是我沒有放棄。\n\n三個月後，我第一次游完了 25 公尺。我的手腳都非常痠，但我真的為自己感到驕傲。現在我每個週末都會去游泳池。明年夏天，我打算和家人一起去墾丁的海裡游泳。", "blanks": [{"options": ["cried", "swam", "slept", "cooked"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由 jumped into the pool（跳進泳池）和 from one end to the other（從這一頭到另一頭）可知，同學們是在泳池裡「游」來游去；swim 的過去式為 swam，與 jumped 時態一致。cried 哭；slept 睡覺；cooked 煮，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["angry", "hungry", "busy", "relaxed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "老師說游泳靠的不是力氣，而是在水中保持「放鬆」，relaxed 放鬆的。angry 生氣的；hungry 飢餓的；busy 忙碌的，都不是學游泳的訣竅。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["but", "so", "because", "or"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句「一開始喝了很多水」與後句「我沒有放棄」語意相反，應用表示轉折的 but「但是」。so 所以；because 因為；or 或者，皆無法表達前後相反的關係。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["afraid", "bored", "proud", "sorry"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一次游完 25 公尺，雖然手腳很痠，卻覺得很有成就感。be proud of oneself「為自己感到驕傲」。afraid 害怕的；bored 無聊的；sorry 抱歉的，都與 but 之後的正面感受不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["swimming", "swim", "swam", "swims"], "answer": 1, "explain": "be going to 後面接原形動詞，表示打算要做的事，故用 swim。swimming 是動名詞或現在分詞；swam 是過去式；swims 是第三人稱單數現在式，都不能放在 going to 後面。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C42", "title": "The Penguins of Boulders Beach", "title_zh": "巨石海灘的企鵝", "level": "A2", "topic": "animals", "passage": "Many people think that penguins only live in very cold places like Antarctica. But some penguins live in warm places, too. At Boulders Beach in South Africa, not far from the city of Cape Town, many African penguins live among the big rocks.\n\nAfrican penguins are small. They are only about 60 centimeters __1__. They have black and white feathers and a pink spot above each eye. They are also very noisy. Their loud call sounds __2__ a donkey.\n\nEvery year, many tourists come to see them. At one part of the beach, visitors watch the birds from wooden walkways. At another part, people can even swim near them! But there is one important rule: do not __3__ or feed the penguins.\n\nSadly, the number of African penguins is getting __4__ every year. One big reason is that they cannot find enough fish to eat. Scientists and volunteers are working hard to __5__ these birds, so people can still see them in the future.", "passage_zh": "很多人以為企鵝只住在像南極洲那樣非常寒冷的地方，但其實有些企鵝也住在溫暖的地方。在南非的巨石海灘，離開普敦市不遠處，就有許多非洲企鵝住在大石頭之間。\n\n非洲企鵝的體型很小，身高只有大約 60 公分。牠們有黑白相間的羽毛，兩隻眼睛上方各有一塊粉紅色的斑。牠們也非常吵，響亮的叫聲聽起來就像驢子一樣。\n\n每年都有許多遊客來看牠們。在海灘的某一區，遊客從木棧道上觀察這些鳥；在另一區，人們甚至可以在牠們附近游泳！不過有一條很重要的規定：不可以觸摸或餵食企鵝。\n\n令人難過的是，非洲企鵝的數量一年比一年少。一個重要的原因是牠們找不到足夠的魚來吃。科學家和志工們正努力保護這些鳥，讓人們將來仍然能看到牠們。", "blanks": [{"options": ["heavy", "old", "tall", "wide"], "answer": 2, "explain": "描述身高用「數字 + 單位 + tall」，60 centimeters tall 指「身高 60 公分」，也呼應前句 African penguins are small（體型很小）。heavy 重的（重量要用公斤表示）；old 老的（年齡不會用公分表示）；wide 寬的，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["like", "with", "for", "from"], "answer": 0, "explain": "sound like + 名詞「聽起來像…」，表示企鵝響亮的叫聲聽起來像驢子。sound with、sound for、sound from 都不能表達「聽起來像」的意思。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["watch", "visit", "find", "touch"], "answer": 3, "explain": "遊客可以從木棧道觀察企鵝，甚至在附近游泳，但重要的規定是「不可以觸摸或餵食企鵝」，touch 觸摸。watch 觀看；visit 拜訪；find 找到，前文已說遊客就是來看企鵝的，不可能禁止這些行為。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["bigger", "smaller", "happier", "louder"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由 Sadly（令人難過的是）以及後句「牠們找不到足夠的魚吃」可知，企鵝的數量越來越少；number 的多寡用 large/small 形容，故用 smaller。bigger 較大的，與 Sadly 不合；happier 較快樂的；louder 較大聲的，都不能形容數量。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["protect", "catch", "eat", "sell"], "answer": 0, "explain": "企鵝數量減少，科學家和志工努力「保護」牠們，讓人們將來仍能看到。protect 保護。catch 抓；eat 吃；sell 賣，皆與後半句的目的相反。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C43", "title": "The Lunch Box Mix-Up", "title_zh": "便當盒拿錯了", "level": "A2", "topic": "school life", "passage": "At my school, many students bring lunch from home. Every morning, we put our lunch boxes into a big steamer. At noon, the food is hot and __1__ to eat.\n\nLast Monday, I opened my lunch box and got a big surprise. There was no rice and no chicken. Instead, there were __2__ noodles and two boiled eggs. \"This isn't my lunch!\" I said. Then I understood. My lunch box and Ting-yu's lunch box looked exactly the same. They were both silver, and they were the same __3__.\n\nJust then, Ting-yu walked over to my desk with a smile. She was holding my chicken and rice. \"I think this is __4__,\" she said. We changed lunch boxes and ate together.\n\nThe next day, my mom tied a red ribbon on my lunch box. Now my lunch box is easy to __5__. And Ting-yu? She is my good friend now.", "passage_zh": "在我們學校，很多學生都從家裡帶午餐。每天早上，我們把便當盒放進一個大蒸飯箱裡，到了中午，飯菜就熱好可以吃了。\n\n上星期一，我打開便當盒，嚇了一大跳：裡面沒有白飯，也沒有雞肉，反而是一些麵和兩顆水煮蛋。「這不是我的午餐！」我說。後來我明白了：我的便當盒和婷妤的便當盒看起來一模一樣，兩個都是銀色的，大小也一樣。\n\n就在這時，婷妤笑著走到我的座位旁，手裡拿著我的雞肉和白飯。「我想這是你的。」她說。我們換回便當盒，然後一起吃午餐。\n\n隔天，媽媽在我的便當盒上綁了一條紅色緞帶。現在我的便當盒很容易就能找到。那婷妤呢？她現在已經是我的好朋友了。", "blanks": [{"options": ["afraid", "late", "sorry", "ready"], "answer": 3, "explain": "便當放進蒸飯箱，到了中午就熱好「可以吃了」。be ready to + 原形動詞「準備好可以…」。afraid 害怕的；late 遲到的；sorry 抱歉的，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["a", "some", "much", "an"], "answer": 1, "explain": "noodles 是可數名詞複數，前面可用 some 表示「一些」。a 和 an 只能接單數可數名詞；much 只能修飾不可數名詞。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["age", "taste", "size", "smell"], "answer": 2, "explain": "兩個便當盒看起來一模一樣：都是銀色，而且「大小」相同，the same size「同樣大小」。age 年紀；taste 味道；smell 氣味，都不是從外觀就能看出來的特徵。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["yours", "your", "you", "yourself"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格後面沒有名詞，要用所有格代名詞 yours（= your lunch）。your 後面必須接名詞；you 是主格或受格；yourself 是反身代名詞，皆不合文法。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["forget", "cook", "find", "break"], "answer": 2, "explain": "綁上紅色緞帶之後，便當盒變得很容易「找到、認出來」，就不會再拿錯了。find 找到。forget 忘記；cook 煮；break 打破，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C44", "title": "Why Do Leaves Change Color?", "title_zh": "葉子為什麼會變色？", "level": "A2", "topic": "science", "passage": "In many parts of the world, leaves turn red, yellow, and orange in autumn. Why does this happen?\n\nLeaves are green because they have a green material called chlorophyll. Plants __1__ chlorophyll to make food from sunlight. In autumn, the days get shorter and the weather gets colder. Trees slowly stop __2__ chlorophyll, so the green color goes away.\n\nThe yellow and orange colors were in the leaves all summer. We could not see them __3__ there was so much green. Some trees also make a new red color in autumn.\n\nYou do not have to go to Canada or Japan to see colorful leaves. In Taiwan, Aowanda in Nantou is famous for its red maple leaves. The best time to see them is usually __4__ late November to January. Aowanda is high in the mountains, and it gets cold there. If you plan to go, __5__ a warm jacket.", "passage_zh": "在世界上的許多地方，樹葉到了秋天會變成紅色、黃色和橘色。為什麼會這樣呢？\n\n葉子之所以是綠色的，是因為裡面有一種叫做葉綠素的綠色物質。植物利用葉綠素，靠陽光製造養分。到了秋天，白天變短，天氣也變冷，樹木會慢慢停止製造葉綠素，於是綠色就漸漸消失了。\n\n其實黃色和橘色整個夏天都在葉子裡，只是因為綠色太多，我們才看不到它們。有些樹在秋天還會製造出一種新的紅色。\n\n你不必到加拿大或日本，也能看到色彩繽紛的葉子。在台灣，南投的奧萬大就以紅色的楓葉聞名，最佳觀賞時間通常是從十一月下旬到一月。奧萬大位在高山上，那裡會變得很冷，如果你打算去，記得帶一件保暖的外套。", "blanks": [{"options": ["use", "sell", "wash", "lose"], "answer": 0, "explain": "植物「利用」葉綠素，靠陽光製造養分。use A to + 原形動詞「用 A 來做…」。sell 賣；wash 洗；lose 失去，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["make", "made", "making", "to making"], "answer": 2, "explain": "stop + V-ing 表示「停止做某事」：樹木停止製造葉綠素，所以綠色消失，故用 making。make 和 made 不能直接放在 stop 後面；to making 不是正確的形式（stop to + 原形動詞 則表示「停下來去做另一件事」）。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["but", "because", "so", "if"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前句「我們看不到黃色和橘色」是結果，後句「綠色太多」是原因，應用 because「因為」。but 但是；so 所以；if 如果，都不能引導表示原因的子句。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["at", "on", "since", "from"], "answer": 3, "explain": "from A to B「從 A 到 B」表示一段時間的起訖，與後面的 to January 搭配。at 用於時間點；on 用於日期或星期；since 表示「自從」，不能和 to 連用表示起訖。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["forget", "sell", "lose", "bring"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句提到奧萬大在高山上、天氣很冷，所以打算去的話要「帶」一件保暖外套。bring 帶來。forget 忘記；sell 賣；lose 遺失，皆與文意相反或不合。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C45", "title": "Venice: A City Without Cars", "title_zh": "威尼斯：沒有汽車的城市", "level": "A2", "topic": "travel", "passage": "Venice is a famous city in northern Italy. It is very different from other cities because it is built on more than 100 small islands. __1__ roads for cars, the city has more than 150 canals, and people cross them on over 400 bridges.\n\nThere are no cars in the old part of Venice. People walk __2__ travel by boat. The vaporetto is like a bus on the water. Tourists also love to ride in gondolas, the long black boats of Venice. A gondola ride is fun, but it is quite __3__.\n\nVenice has a big problem, too. In autumn and winter, very high water sometimes comes into the streets. In 2020, the city started to use a system of large gates to __4__ the sea out.\n\nEvery year, millions of tourists visit Venice. If you go there one day, wear comfortable shoes. You will walk a __5__!", "passage_zh": "威尼斯是義大利北部一座有名的城市。它和其他城市非常不同，因為它建在一百多座小島上。這座城市沒有供汽車行駛的道路，取而代之的是一百五十多條運河，人們則經由四百多座橋往來於運河兩岸。\n\n威尼斯的舊城區裡沒有汽車，人們不是走路，就是搭船。水上巴士（vaporetto）就像行駛在水上的公車。遊客也很喜歡搭乘貢多拉，也就是威尼斯那種細長的黑色小船。搭貢多拉很好玩，但是相當昂貴。\n\n威尼斯也有一個大問題。在秋天和冬天，異常高漲的海水有時會淹進街道。2020 年，這座城市開始使用一套由大型閘門組成的系統，把海水擋在外面。\n\n每年都有數百萬名遊客造訪威尼斯。如果你有一天去那裡，記得穿雙舒服的鞋子，因為你會走很多路！", "blanks": [{"options": ["Because of", "In front of", "Instead of", "Out of"], "answer": 2, "explain": "威尼斯沒有供汽車行駛的道路，而是以一百五十多條運河取而代之。instead of + 名詞「代替…；而不是…」。because of 因為；in front of 在…前面；out of 從…出來，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["or", "but", "so", "because"], "answer": 0, "explain": "舊城區沒有汽車，人們「走路或搭船」，兩者是選擇關係，應用 or「或」。but 但是；so 所以；because 因為，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["cheap", "free", "easy", "expensive"], "answer": 3, "explain": "but 表示前後語意轉折：搭貢多拉很好玩，「但是」相當昂貴，expensive 昂貴的。cheap 便宜的；free 免費的；easy 容易的，都是正面的描述，與 but 的轉折不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["let", "keep", "bring", "take"], "answer": 1, "explain": "keep A out「把 A 擋在外面」，閘門的作用是把海水擋在城市外面，不讓它淹進街道。let A out 讓 A 出去；bring 帶來；take 拿走，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["lot", "few", "many", "little"], "answer": 0, "explain": "a lot「很多」可修飾動詞 walk，表示會走很多路，所以才要穿舒服的鞋。a few 後面須接可數名詞；many 前面不加 a；a little 意為「一點點」，與「要穿舒服的鞋」的提醒不合。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C46", "title": "From Garbage Island to Recycling Model", "title_zh": "從「垃圾島」到回收模範生", "level": "C1", "topic": "waste management", "passage": "In the early 1990s, Taiwan faced a crisis that few visitors today would believe. Landfills were overflowing, piles of uncollected rubbish lined the streets, and angry residents sometimes blocked garbage trucks from entering their neighborhoods. The island even earned the unflattering nickname \"Garbage Island.\" Three decades later, however, it is __1__ cited as one of the world's most successful examples of waste reduction.\n\nThe turnaround came not from a single brilliant invention but from a combination of policies that reshaped everyday habits. One of the most effective was a \"pay-as-you-throw\" scheme. In Taipei, for example, residents have since 2000 been required to put their general waste in special bags __2__ price includes the cost of collection, while recyclables are taken away for free. The message could hardly be clearer: the less you throw away, the less you pay.\n\nEqually important is the way rubbish is collected. Rather than leaving bags on the street, where they would attract rats and stray dogs, people wait for garbage trucks that arrive at fixed times, their approach __3__ by a tinkling version of Beethoven's \"Für Elise.\" Residents hand their bags directly to the collectors and place their recyclables in a second truck that follows close behind. __4__ everyone can see how carefully, or how carelessly, their neighbors sort their waste, the system also relies on a gentle form of social pressure.\n\nThe results have been remarkable. Taiwan now recycles more than half of its household waste, and the amount of rubbish each person sends for disposal has fallen dramatically. Some experts, __5__, warn against complacency: recycling, they point out, is only part of the answer, and the real challenge is to create less waste in the first place.", "passage_zh": "1990 年代初期，台灣面臨一場如今的遊客很難相信的危機：垃圾掩埋場滿到溢出來，街道兩旁堆滿沒人清運的垃圾，憤怒的居民有時甚至會阻擋垃圾車進入他們的社區。這座島嶼甚至得到了「垃圾島」這個不光彩的綽號。然而三十年後，台灣卻被廣泛引用為全世界減少垃圾最成功的例子之一。\n\n這樣的轉變並非來自某一項了不起的發明，而是來自一系列重塑日常習慣的政策。其中最有效的措施之一是「隨袋徵收」制度。以台北為例，自 2000 年起，居民就必須把一般垃圾裝進專用垃圾袋，袋子的價格已包含清運費用，而資源回收物則免費收取。這個訊息再清楚不過了：丟得越少，付得越少。\n\n同樣重要的是垃圾的收集方式。人們不把垃圾袋放在街上（那樣會引來老鼠和流浪狗），而是等待在固定時間抵達的垃圾車；垃圾車快到時，會播放叮叮咚咚的貝多芬〈給愛麗絲〉來通知大家。居民直接把垃圾交給清潔隊員，再把回收物放進緊跟在後的第二輛車。由於每個人都看得到鄰居分類垃圾有多仔細或多馬虎，這套制度也仰賴一種溫和的社會壓力。\n\n成效相當驚人。台灣現在回收超過一半的家戶垃圾，每人送去處理的垃圾量也大幅下降。然而，有些專家警告不能因此自滿：他們指出，回收只是解決之道的一部分，真正的挑戰在於從一開始就少製造垃圾。", "blanks": [{"options": ["barely", "widely", "narrowly", "reluctantly"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由 however 可知，此句與前文「垃圾島」的負面形象形成對比：如今台灣「被廣泛引用」為成功範例。widely cited「被廣泛引用」為常見搭配。barely 幾乎沒有；narrowly 勉強地；以些微差距；reluctantly 不情願地，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["which", "that", "its", "whose"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格後 price includes the cost of collection 為完整子句，空格須引導形容詞子句修飾 bags，並表示「袋子的價格」，應用所有格關係代名詞 whose。which 與 that 作關係代名詞時，子句中須缺主詞或受詞；its 是所有格，不是連接詞或關係詞，無法連接兩個子句。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["announced", "interrupted", "replaced", "deafened"], "answer": 0, "explain": "their approach ___ by… 為獨立分詞構句（＝ and their approach is announced by…），垃圾車的到來是「由〈給愛麗絲〉的旋律來宣告」，故用 announced。interrupted 打斷；replaced 取代；deafened 使耳聾，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["Although", "Unless", "Since", "Whereas"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格引導副詞子句，說明「每個人都看得到鄰居如何分類垃圾」正是這套制度能運用社會壓力的原因，應用 Since「由於；既然」。Although 雖然；Unless 除非；Whereas 然而（表對比），皆不合邏輯。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["however", "therefore", "for instance", "in other words"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前文說成效驚人，此句卻說有些專家警告不可自滿，前後語意轉折，應用 however「然而」，置於主詞之後並以逗號隔開。therefore 因此；for instance 例如；in other words 換句話說，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C47", "title": "Paying for Rat Tails: When Rewards Backfire", "title_zh": "用老鼠尾巴換錢：當獎勵適得其反", "level": "C1", "topic": "incentives", "passage": "In 1902, the French colonial government in Hanoi faced an unexpected enemy. The modern sewer system it had recently built beneath the city's European quarter had become an ideal home for rats, and officials feared that the animals would spread plague. __1__ a growing sense of urgency, the authorities decided to turn the city's residents into rat hunters.\n\nThe plan seemed perfectly sensible. For every rat tail handed in, a hunter would receive a small reward of one cent. Officials asked only for the tail, __2__ they had no wish to deal with thousands of dead bodies. At first, the scheme appeared to be working remarkably well, as tails poured in by the thousands.\n\nBefore long, however, residents began to notice tailless rats running through the streets. Rather than killing the animals, some hunters had been cutting off their tails and releasing them, __3__ the rats could go on breeding and produce more tails in the future. Health officials later made an even more surprising discovery: some people on the edge of the city had begun breeding rats on purpose. The reward program was eventually __4__, but the city's rat problem was no closer to being solved.\n\nEconomists now use stories like this to illustrate Goodhart's law, named after the British economist Charles Goodhart. As it is often summarized, \"When a measure becomes a target, it ceases to be a good measure.\" The number of tails was meant to reflect how many rats had been killed, but once people were paid for tails, they found ways to increase the number without __5__ the underlying problem. The lesson applies far beyond rats: whenever we reward a number, from test scores to website clicks, we should ask whether we are rewarding the behavior we actually want.", "passage_zh": "1902 年，河內的法國殖民政府面對一個意想不到的敵人。他們不久前才在城中歐洲人居住區地下興建的現代化下水道，成了老鼠的理想住所，官員擔心這些動物會散播鼠疫。在越來越強烈的危機感驅使下，當局決定讓全城居民都變成捕鼠人。\n\n這個計畫看起來非常合理：每交出一條老鼠尾巴，捕鼠人就能得到一分錢的小額獎金。官員只要求交出尾巴，因為他們不想處理成千上萬具老鼠屍體。起初，這個計畫似乎成效奇佳，老鼠尾巴成千上萬地湧進來。\n\n然而沒過多久，居民開始注意到街上有沒有尾巴的老鼠到處亂竄。原來有些捕鼠人並沒有殺死老鼠，而是剪下尾巴後就把牠們放走，好讓這些老鼠繼續繁殖，將來生出更多尾巴。衛生官員後來還有更令人意外的發現：城市邊緣有些居民竟然開始刻意養殖老鼠。這項獎勵計畫最後被廢止了，但這座城市的鼠患問題並沒有因此更接近解決。\n\n經濟學家現在常用這類故事來說明「古德哈特定律」，這個定律是以英國經濟學家查爾斯‧古德哈特命名的。它常被概括為：「當一項指標變成目標，它就不再是好的指標。」尾巴的數量原本是用來反映有多少老鼠被殺死，但人們一旦能靠尾巴換錢，就會想辦法增加數量，卻沒有處理根本的問題。這個教訓的適用範圍遠遠不只老鼠：每當我們獎勵某個數字，從考試分數到網站點閱數，都應該問問自己，我們獎勵的是否真的是自己想要的行為。", "blanks": [{"options": ["Despite", "Instead of", "Driven by", "Regardless of"], "answer": 2, "explain": "當局是「在越來越強烈的急迫感驅使下」才決定動員居民捕鼠，Driven by…「受…驅使」為過去分詞片語，表示原因。Despite 儘管；Instead of 而不是；Regardless of 不管，皆與「因為情況急迫才採取行動」的因果關係不符。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["since", "unless", "although", "so that"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格後說明官員只要尾巴的原因：他們不想處理成千上萬具屍體，應用表示原因的連接詞 since「因為」。unless 除非；although 雖然；so that 以便，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["even if", "as though", "in case", "so that"], "answer": 3, "explain": "捕鼠人剪下尾巴後放走老鼠，「目的」是讓老鼠繼續繁殖、將來產生更多尾巴，應用 so that「以便；為了」引導目的子句。even if 即使；as though 彷彿；in case 以防萬一，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["extended", "abandoned", "rewarded", "celebrated"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前文說明計畫造成反效果，後句又以 but 指出鼠患並未因此改善，可知計畫最後「被廢止」。abandon「放棄；廢止」。extended 延長；rewarded 獎勵；celebrated 慶祝，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["exaggerating", "inheriting", "postponing", "addressing"], "answer": 3, "explain": "人們一旦能靠尾巴換錢，就想辦法增加尾巴的數量，卻沒有真正「處理」根本的問題（鼠患），address「處理；應對（問題）」；without 為介系詞，故四個選項都用動名詞。exaggerating 誇大；inheriting 繼承；postponing 延後，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C48", "title": "A Network Without a Brain: The Slime Mold That Copied Tokyo's Railways", "title_zh": "沒有大腦的路網設計師：模仿東京鐵路的黏菌", "level": "C1", "topic": "biology", "passage": "It has no brain, no nerves and not even separate cells, yet it can find the shortest route through a maze. Physarum polycephalum, a bright yellow slime mold that lives on rotting leaves and logs, is essentially one enormous cell containing millions of nuclei. __1__ its lack of a brain, it has proved remarkably good at solving problems that challenge human engineers.\n\nThe mold feeds by spreading out a network of tubes in search of food. When it finds something to eat, it strengthens the tubes that carry nutrients most efficiently and allows the rest to __2__ away. In 2000, a team led by the Japanese scientist Toshiyuki Nakagaki showed that this simple rule allowed the mold to connect two pieces of food through a maze by the shortest possible path.\n\nA decade later, the same group set an even bigger challenge. On a flat dish shaped like the area around Tokyo, they placed oat flakes in the positions of 36 cities and put the mold where Tokyo itself would be. Because the mold avoids light, the researchers shone light on areas __3__ mountains and lakes, where building tracks would be difficult. Within about a day, the mold had spread out and formed a network of tubes that looked strikingly similar to the region's real railway system.\n\nThe resemblance was not merely visual. When the team measured the mold's network for cost, efficiency and resistance to breakdowns, it performed roughly __4__ well as the system that human engineers had spent decades building. The study later won an Ig Nobel Prize, a humorous award for research that \"first makes people laugh, and then makes them think,\" and it has inspired computer programs based on the mold's rules. __5__ engineers may never hand the planning of a railway over to a slime mold, they have clearly learned something from it.", "passage_zh": "它沒有大腦、沒有神經，甚至沒有分開的細胞，卻能找出穿越迷宮的最短路線。多頭絨泡菌是一種鮮黃色的黏菌，生長在腐爛的落葉和木頭上，基本上是一個含有數百萬個細胞核的巨大細胞。儘管沒有大腦，它在解決連人類工程師都覺得棘手的問題上，卻表現得出奇地好。\n\n這種黏菌覓食時，會伸展出一張由管子組成的網路來尋找食物。找到食物後，它會強化那些運送養分最有效率的管子，並讓其餘的管子逐漸萎縮消失。2000 年，由日本科學家中垣俊之領導的團隊證明，靠著這條簡單的規則，黏菌就能以最短的路徑穿過迷宮，把兩塊食物連接起來。\n\n十年後，同一個團隊設下了更大的挑戰。他們在一個形狀像東京周邊地區的平坦培養皿上，把燕麥片放在 36 個城市的位置，並把黏菌放在東京所在的位置。由於黏菌會避開光線，研究人員便用光照射代表山脈和湖泊的區域，也就是難以鋪設鐵軌的地方。大約一天之內，黏菌就擴散開來，形成一張和該地區真實鐵路系統驚人相似的管狀網路。\n\n兩者的相似之處不只在外觀。研究團隊從成本、效率和抗故障能力來評估黏菌的網路，發現它的表現和人類工程師花了數十年建造的系統大致不相上下。這項研究後來獲得了搞笑諾貝爾獎，這個幽默的獎項專門頒給「先讓人發笑，再讓人思考」的研究；它也啟發了以黏菌規則為基礎的電腦程式。雖然工程師或許永遠不會把鐵路規劃交給黏菌，但他們顯然從中學到了一些東西。", "blanks": [{"options": ["Despite", "Because of", "Apart from", "Thanks to"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說黏菌沒有大腦，此句卻說它很擅長解決連工程師都覺得困難的問題，前後語意相反，應用表示讓步的 Despite「儘管」（介系詞，後接名詞 its lack of a brain）。Because of 因為；Apart from 除了…之外；Thanks to 多虧，皆無法表達讓步關係。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["give", "take", "wither", "put"], "answer": 2, "explain": "wither away 為片語動詞，意為「萎縮；逐漸消失」，與前面「強化最有效率的管子」形成對比：其餘的管子則任其萎縮。give away 贈送；take away 拿走；put away 收好，皆須接受詞，且語意不合。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["represented", "representing", "represent", "were represented"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格引導分詞片語修飾 areas（＝ areas that represent mountains and lakes），區域「代表」山脈和湖泊為主動，故用現在分詞 representing。represented 作過去分詞表示被動，語意不合；represent 為原形動詞，與前面的子句之間缺少連接詞；were represented 為被動的限定動詞，結構錯誤。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["so", "very", "much", "as"], "answer": 3, "explain": "as + adj. / adv. + as 為同等比較句型，roughly as well as 意為「和…表現得大致一樣好」；空格後已有第二個 as，故空格填 as。so…as 一般只用於否定句；very、much 無法與後面的 as 構成比較句型。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Unless", "Because", "While", "Once"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前半句說工程師也許永遠不會把鐵路規劃交給黏菌，後半句卻說他們顯然從黏菌身上學到東西，前後為讓步關係，應用 While「雖然」。Unless 除非；Because 因為；Once 一旦，皆不合邏輯。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C49", "title": "Going Over Backwards: The Fosbury Flop", "title_zh": "背向越竿：改寫跳高史的背越式", "level": "C1", "topic": "sports", "passage": "When Dick Fosbury arrived at the 1968 Olympic Games in Mexico City, few people expected him to win. The American high jumper used a technique that coaches considered not only odd but dangerous. __1__ facing the bar and swinging one leg over it, as almost every other jumper did, he ran toward it in a curve, turned his back to it and went over head first, landing on his shoulders.\n\nFosbury had not set out to start a revolution. As a teenager in Oregon, he had struggled with the standard technique and kept failing to clear heights that his teammates managed with ease. Frustrated, he went back to an older, simpler style and began to __2__ it, leaning a little further backward each time. The result looked so strange that a local reporter wrote that he looked like a fish flopping in a boat, and the name \"Fosbury Flop\" soon stuck.\n\nHis timing turned out to be perfect. For decades, high jumpers had landed in pits of sand or sawdust, __3__ made landing on one's back a painful, even risky, experience. By the mid-1960s, however, a growing number of schools had begun to replace them with thick foam mats. Without this change, the Flop would almost certainly have remained an unusual experiment rather than a winning technique.\n\nIn Mexico City, Fosbury cleared 2.24 meters, setting an Olympic record and winning the gold medal. Young athletes who watched him were quick to copy his style, and __4__ a decade, the Flop had become the dominant technique in the event. Today it is __5__ universal among elite high jumpers that the straddle, once the standard, is almost never seen at major competitions. Fosbury's story is a reminder that progress sometimes depends on someone who is willing to look foolish.", "passage_zh": "1968 年迪克‧福斯貝利來到墨西哥市參加奧運時，很少有人預料到他會奪冠。這位美國跳高選手所用的技巧，被教練們認為不但古怪，而且危險。他不像幾乎所有其他選手那樣面向橫竿、把一條腿甩過竿子，而是以弧線助跑，轉身背對橫竿，頭先過竿，再以肩膀著地。\n\n福斯貝利當初並不是想掀起一場革命。他在奧勒岡州念中學時，一直學不好當時的標準跳法，隊友們輕輕鬆鬆就能越過的高度，他卻屢屢失敗。沮喪之餘，他回頭採用一種更古老、更簡單的跳法，並開始加以修改，每次都讓身體再往後仰一點。結果看起來非常奇怪，當地一位記者寫道，他看起來就像一條在船上拍打翻跳的魚，「福斯貝利式跳高」（Fosbury Flop）這個名稱很快就傳開了。\n\n事實證明他的時機再好不過。數十年來，跳高選手都是落在沙坑或木屑坑裡，這使得背部著地成為一種疼痛、甚至危險的經驗。然而到了 1960 年代中期，越來越多學校開始改用厚厚的泡棉墊。要不是有這項改變，背越式幾乎一定只會停留在罕見的實驗階段，而不會成為奪冠的技巧。\n\n在墨西哥市，福斯貝利越過了 2.24 公尺，創下奧運紀錄並贏得金牌。看過他比賽的年輕選手很快就模仿他的跳法，不到十年，背越式就成了這個項目的主流技巧。如今，背越式在頂尖跳高選手之間已經如此普遍，以至於曾經是標準跳法的俯臥式，在大型比賽中幾乎再也看不到了。福斯貝利的故事提醒我們，進步有時要靠一個不怕看起來很傻的人。", "blanks": [{"options": ["As for", "Except for", "Due to", "Instead of"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說教練認為他的技巧古怪，後文描述他「不像其他選手那樣」面向橫竿，而是背對橫竿過竿，應用 Instead of「而不是」，後接動名詞 facing… and swinging…。As for 至於；Except for 除了；Due to 由於，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["abandon", "modify", "imitate", "forbid"], "answer": 1, "explain": "他回頭採用較舊的跳法，而且每次都讓身體再往後仰一點，可知他是在「修改」這種跳法。modify「修改；改良」。abandon 放棄，與他繼續使用此跳法矛盾；imitate 模仿，無法說明他逐步改變動作；forbid 禁止，不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["that", "what", "which", "it"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格前有逗號，須用非限定關係代名詞 which 引導形容詞子句，補充說明落在沙坑或木屑坑所造成的影響，並在子句中作主詞。that 不可用於非限定子句；what 本身含先行詞，不能接在名詞之後；it 無法連接兩個子句，會形成連寫句。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["within", "since", "during", "until"], "answer": 0, "explain": "within + 一段時間表示「在…之內」，本句意為「不到十年，背越式就成了主流」，與過去完成式 had become 搭配。since 自從，後須接時間點；during 表示在某段期間當中，無法表達「在某段時間之內就已完成」；until 直到，與文意不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["such", "so", "too", "very"], "answer": 1, "explain": "so + adj. + that… 表示「如此…以致於…」，空格後為形容詞 universal，並接 that 子句，故用 so。such 須接名詞（such + a/an + adj. + N）；too 用於 too…to 句型，表示「太…而不能」，不接 that 子句；very 無法與 that 子句構成此句型。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C50", "title": "Seeing the Whole Earth: The Overview Effect", "title_zh": "看見整顆地球：總觀效應", "level": "C1", "topic": "space", "passage": "Astronauts are selected for their calm, practical minds, so it is striking how often they describe their first view of Earth from space in almost poetic terms. Many report a sudden, overwhelming sense that the planet is a fragile, living whole, __1__ national borders are invisible and the atmosphere appears as no more than a thin blue line along the horizon. In 1987, the writer Frank White gave this experience a name: the \"overview effect.\"\n\nThe phrase has since become a subject of serious research. Psychologists who study awe note that astronauts' accounts share several features: a feeling of wonder, a sense of connection with all of humanity, and a new determination to protect the planet. Edgar Mitchell, who walked on the Moon in 1971, later said that he had developed an \"instant global consciousness.\" Experiences like these, researchers argue, can change __2__ people see their place in the world for the rest of their lives.\n\nEven people who have never left the ground have felt a version of the effect. On Christmas Eve 1968, the crew of Apollo 8 photographed Earth rising above the surface of the Moon. The image, later known as \"Earthrise,\" quickly __3__ the attention of millions of people around the world. It is often said to have helped __4__ the modern environmental movement; the first Earth Day was held less than two years later, in April 1970.\n\nNot everyone who reaches space reacts in the same way, __5__, and scientists still debate how much of the effect lasts once astronauts return to their busy lives on Earth. Some researchers are now testing whether virtual reality can give people a taste of the experience. If it can, a view that once belonged to only a few hundred astronauts might one day help millions of us see our crowded, quarrelsome planet as what it really is: a small, shared home.", "passage_zh": "太空人是因為頭腦冷靜務實才被選中的，因此令人驚訝的是，他們在描述第一次從太空看到地球的感受時，常常使用近乎詩意的語言。許多人說，他們突然強烈地感受到，地球是一個脆弱而有生命的整體，在它上面看不到國界，大氣層看起來也不過是地平線上一條細細的藍線。1987 年，作家法蘭克‧懷特為這種經驗取了一個名字：「總觀效應」。\n\n從那時起，這個詞成了嚴肅研究的主題。研究「敬畏感」的心理學家指出，太空人的描述有幾個共同點：驚奇的感受、與全人類相連的感覺，以及保護地球的新決心。1971 年曾在月球上漫步的艾德加‧米契爾後來說，他當時產生了一種「瞬間的全球意識」。研究人員認為，這類經驗可能會終生改變人們看待自己在世界上所處位置的方式。\n\n就連從未離開地面的人，也曾感受到某種程度的總觀效應。1968 年的平安夜，阿波羅 8 號的太空人拍下了地球從月球表面上方升起的畫面。這張後來被稱為「地出」的照片，很快就吸引了全世界數百萬人的目光。人們常說這張照片幫助引發了現代環保運動；不到兩年後，第一屆世界地球日就在 1970 年 4 月舉行。\n\n當然，並不是每個到過太空的人都有相同的反應，科學家也仍在爭論，太空人回到地球上忙碌的生活之後，這種效應能持續多少。有些研究人員目前正在測試，虛擬實境能否讓人們體驗到類似的感受。如果可以，這片曾經只屬於數百名太空人的景象，或許有一天能幫助我們數百萬人看清這顆擁擠又紛爭不斷的星球真正的模樣：一個小小的、大家共有的家。", "blanks": [{"options": ["which", "what", "on which", "whom"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格引導形容詞子句，修飾 a fragile, living whole（即地球），子句 national borders are invisible 結構完整，須用「介系詞 + which」表示「在這個整體（地球）上」，故用 on which。which 單獨使用時，子句中須缺主詞或受詞；what 本身含先行詞，不能接在名詞之後；whom 只能指人。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["that", "what", "which", "how"], "answer": 3, "explain": "change 的受詞為名詞子句，空格後 people see their place in the world 結構完整，語意為「人們看待自己在世界上位置的方式」，應用 how。that 子句只陳述事實，change that… 語意不通；what、which 引導的名詞子句中須缺主詞或受詞，但此處子句結構完整。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["captured", "deprived", "distracted", "dismissed"], "answer": 0, "explain": "capture the attention of…「吸引…的注意」為常見搭配，意為這張照片很快就吸引了全世界數百萬人的目光。deprived 剝奪，須用 deprive sb. of sth.；distracted 使分心，是把注意力「引開」，與照片吸引全世界目光的文意相反；dismissed 不予理會，與後文照片影響深遠矛盾。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["suppress", "spark", "postpone", "ignore"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由後半句「不到兩年後就舉行了第一屆世界地球日」可知，這張照片被認為幫助「引發」了現代環保運動。spark「引發；激起」。suppress 壓制；postpone 延後；ignore 忽視，皆與文意相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["for example", "as a result", "of course", "in addition"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前文描述總觀效應帶來的深刻感受，此句則承認並非每個人都有相同的反應，屬於讓步性的補充說明，應用 of course「當然」。for example 例如；as a result 結果；in addition 此外，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C51", "title": "The Disaster That Never Came: Y2K and the Preparedness Paradox", "title_zh": "沒有降臨的災難：千禧蟲與防備悖論", "level": "C2", "topic": "technology risk", "passage": "As the final hours of 1999 ticked away, engineers in control rooms around the world waited nervously for something to go wrong. For years, newspapers had warned that the so-called \"millennium bug\" could cripple banks, ground aircraft and shut down power grids. The cause of all this alarm was almost absurdly mundane. To save what was then costly computer memory, programmers in earlier decades had routinely recorded years with just two digits, so that 1999 was stored as \"99.\" When the calendar rolled over, many systems might read \"00\" not as 2000 but as 1900, throwing calculations of everything from interest payments to maintenance schedules into __1__.\n\nWhat followed was one of the largest coordinated engineering efforts ever undertaken. Governments set up special task forces, and companies hired armies of programmers, some of them coaxed out of retirement because they still understood long-neglected programming languages. Vast amounts of code were checked and rewritten line by line. Then midnight came and went, and the much-feared catastrophe failed to __2__. Apart from a scattering of minor glitches, planes stayed in the air, the lights stayed on and cash machines went on dispensing money.\n\nAlmost at once, a different story began to circulate. If nothing had happened, the argument ran, the danger must have been exaggerated all along, and the enormous sums spent on repairs had been wasted. Yet this reasoning contains a trap. __3__ the work had been done, nobody could prove what would have happened without it. Experts call this the preparedness paradox: the more successfully a disaster is averted, the more the effort to prevent it looks like an overreaction.\n\nNot everyone was persuaded, of course. Some critics pointed out that several countries which had spent comparatively little also came through the night largely unscathed, and they argued that the risks had been __4__ by consultants who stood to profit from them. Defenders replied that those countries relied heavily on software and equipment that suppliers elsewhere had already corrected, and that many small failures probably went unreported.\n\nThe debate has never been fully settled, but its lesson extends far beyond computing. Vaccination campaigns, flood defenses and safety inspections all suffer from the same thankless logic: when they work, their success is invisible, and the people responsible are left to defend themselves against the charge of having __5__ wolf. An absence of disaster, in other words, is not the same as an absence of danger.", "passage_zh": "隨著 1999 年的最後幾個小時一分一秒地過去，世界各地控制室裡的工程師都緊張地等待著，看會不會出什麼差錯。多年來，報紙不斷警告，所謂的「千禧蟲」可能會讓銀行癱瘓、飛機停飛、電網停擺。而引發這一切恐慌的原因，卻平凡得近乎荒謬。為了節省當時十分昂貴的電腦記憶體，早年的程式設計師習慣只用兩位數字記錄年份，因此 1999 年會被存成「99」。等到日曆跨入新年，許多系統可能會把「00」讀成 1900 年而不是 2000 年，使從利息支付到維修排程等各式各樣的計算陷入混亂。\n\n接下來展開的，是有史以來規模最大的協同工程行動之一。各國政府成立了專案小組，企業則聘請了大批程式設計師，其中有些是被說服重出江湖的退休人員，因為他們仍懂得那些早已乏人問津的程式語言。大量的程式碼被逐行檢查並改寫。然後，午夜來了又走，那場眾人擔心不已的災難卻始終沒有出現。除了零星幾個小故障之外，飛機照常飛行，電燈照常亮著，提款機也繼續吐出鈔票。\n\n幾乎就在同時，另一種說法開始流傳。這種論點認為，既然什麼事都沒發生，那麼危險想必從頭到尾都被誇大了，花在修復上的鉅額經費也都白白浪費了。然而，這種推論暗藏陷阱。正因為這些工作已經完成，所以沒有人能證明，如果沒做的話會發生什麼事。專家稱之為「防備悖論」：一場災難被避免得越成功，為了預防它所付出的努力，看起來就越像是反應過度。\n\n當然，並不是每個人都被說服了。一些批評者指出，有幾個投入相對較少的國家，同樣幾乎毫髮無傷地度過了那一夜；他們因此主張，風險是被那些能從中獲利的顧問誇大的。辯護者則回應說，那些國家大量使用的軟體與設備，早已由其他地方的供應商修正過了，而且許多小故障很可能根本沒有被通報。\n\n這場爭論從未真正塵埃落定，但它的教訓遠遠超出了電腦領域。疫苗接種計畫、防洪設施和安全檢查，都受困於同樣吃力不討好的邏輯：當它們發揮作用時，成功是看不見的，而負責的人還得替自己辯護，反駁別人指控他們「謊報狼來了」、危言聳聽。換句話說，沒有發生災難，並不等於沒有危險。", "blanks": [{"options": ["abeyance", "disarray", "remission", "dormancy"], "answer": 1, "explain": "throw sth into disarray 為固定搭配，意為「使陷入混亂」，符合年份被誤讀成 1900 年後各種計算全部出錯的情境。abeyance 擱置、暫停（常用 in abeyance）；remission（疾病的）緩解期；dormancy 休眠狀態，皆無法與 throw…into 搭配表達「混亂」。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["subside", "dissipate", "abate", "materialize"], "answer": 3, "explain": "fail to materialize 為常見搭配，指「（預期中的事）並未實現、沒有出現」；下一句說除了零星小故障外一切正常，可知災難根本沒有發生。subside 與 abate 指「（風雨、疼痛等）減弱、平息」，dissipate 指「消散」，三者都預設災難已經出現，只是後來減弱或消失，與文意不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Precisely because", "Even though", "Unless", "Much as"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格引導的子句說明「無人能證明不做會怎樣」的原因：正是因為修復工作已經完成，才無從得知不做的後果，應用 Precisely because「正因為」。Even though 與 Much as 都表「雖然」，為讓步語氣，與此處的因果關係不合；Unless「除非」語意不通。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["mitigated", "underestimated", "inflated", "dismissed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "批評者認為危險被誇大了，且顧問可從中獲利（stood to profit），可知他們指控顧問「誇大」風險，inflate 在此作「誇大、灌水」解。mitigate 減輕；underestimate 低估；dismiss 不予理會，都與「誇大風險以獲利」的批評方向相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["cried", "howled", "raised", "sounded"], "answer": 0, "explain": "cry wolf 為固定慣用語，源自《伊索寓言》中一再謊報狼來了的牧童，指「謊報危險、危言聳聽」；此處 having cried wolf 為完成式動名詞，作介系詞 of 的受詞。raise the alarm 與 sound the alarm 雖可表「發出警報」，但不能與 wolf 搭配；howl 指（狼）嚎叫，語意不合。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C52", "title": "A Clock to Cross the Ocean: John Harrison and the Longitude Problem", "title_zh": "橫渡大洋的時鐘：約翰．哈里森與經度難題", "level": "C2", "topic": "history of science", "passage": "For centuries, sailors could work out how far north or south they were with relative ease, simply by measuring the height of the sun or the Pole Star above the horizon. Determining how far east or west they had traveled was another matter entirely. Far from land, navigators were often reduced to educated guesswork, and the toll in wrecked ships, lost cargo and drowned crews was __1__.\n\nIn principle, the solution was simple. Because the earth turns fifteen degrees every hour, a navigator who knew the exact time at his home port could compare it with local noon at sea and calculate his longitude. In practice, there was no way of carrying home-port time across an ocean. The pendulum clocks of the day, __2__ reliable on land, were useless on a pitching deck, and changes in temperature made their metal parts expand and contract.\n\nIn 1714 the British Parliament offered a reward of up to £20,000, an immense fortune at the time, for a practical method of finding longitude at sea. Most leading scientists assumed that the answer lay in the heavens, in precise observation of the moon's position against the stars. John Harrison, a Yorkshire-born carpenter with no formal scientific education, __3__ instead to build a clock that could survive the sea.\n\nOver some forty years he produced a series of ingenious timekeepers, each an improvement on the last. His fourth, a large watch now known as H4, was tested on a voyage to Jamaica in the early 1760s and lost only a matter of seconds during the crossing. His success ought to have settled the matter. __4__, the Board of Longitude, some of whose members still favored astronomical methods, demanded further trials, questioned his results and insisted that he explain his designs in exhaustive detail.\n\nHarrison spent much of his old age fighting for recognition. He eventually received substantial payments, partly thanks to the support of King George III, but he was never formally awarded the prize itself. History has been kinder. Captain James Cook carried a copy of H4 on his second voyage to the Pacific and spoke of it with admiration, and today Harrison is remembered as the self-taught craftsman who solved a problem that had __5__ some of the finest minds in Europe.", "passage_zh": "幾百年來，水手只要測量太陽或北極星在地平線上的高度，就能相當輕鬆地算出自己位在多北或多南的地方。然而，要判斷自己往東或往西航行了多遠，卻完全是另一回事。在遠離陸地的海上，航海者往往只能憑經驗推測，因此造成的船隻失事、貨物損失與船員溺斃，代價之大令人震驚。\n\n原則上，解決方法很簡單。由於地球每小時自轉十五度，航海者只要知道母港當下的準確時間，再拿來和海上當地的正午相比，就能算出自己所在的經度。但實際上，當時根本沒有辦法把母港的時間帶著橫越大洋。那個時代的擺鐘在陸地上無論多麼準確，到了顛簸搖晃的甲板上都毫無用處，而且溫度的變化還會讓它們的金屬零件熱脹冷縮。\n\n1714 年，英國國會懸賞最高兩萬英鎊——在當時是一筆鉅款——徵求在海上測定經度的實用方法。當時大多數頂尖科學家都認為答案在天上，也就是精確觀測月亮相對於群星的位置。然而，出生於約克郡、沒有受過正式科學教育的木匠約翰．哈里森，卻另闢蹊徑，著手打造一座經得起海上考驗的時鐘。\n\n在大約四十年間，他製作出一系列精巧的計時器，每一座都比前一座更進步。他的第四座計時器是一只大型的錶，如今被稱為 H4；它在 1760 年代初期一趟前往牙買加的航程中接受測試，整趟航行下來只差了短短幾秒。他的成功理應讓這件事塵埃落定。然而事與願違，經度委員會（其中部分委員仍偏好天文觀測法）反而要求進行更多試驗、質疑他的結果，還堅持要他鉅細靡遺地說明自己的設計。\n\n哈里森晚年大多在為爭取肯定而奮鬥。部分多虧英王喬治三世的支持，他最終獲得了可觀的款項，卻從未正式獲頒那筆獎金本身。歷史對他則較為厚道。庫克船長在第二次前往太平洋的航行中帶了一只 H4 的複製品，並對它讚譽有加；如今，人們記得哈里森是一位自學出身的工匠，解決了一個曾讓歐洲許多頂尖人物束手無策的難題。", "blanks": [{"options": ["negligible", "unaccountable", "staggering", "illusory"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前文指出航海者在海上往往只能靠推測，可推知船隻失事、貨物損失與船員溺斃的代價「驚人」，staggering 意為「令人震驚的、極大的」。negligible 微不足道的，與文意相反；unaccountable 無法解釋的，但損失的原因前文已說明；illusory 虛幻不實的，亦不合理。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["however", "whatever", "whichever", "whenever"], "answer": 0, "explain": "however + 形容詞 表讓步「無論多麼……」，however reliable on land 是 however reliable they were on land 的省略，意為「在陸地上無論多麼準確」，與後文「在甲板上毫無用處」形成對比。whatever 與 whichever 須接名詞或作代名詞，不能直接修飾形容詞；whenever「每當」語意不通。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["resorted", "deigned", "objected", "set out"], "answer": 3, "explain": "set out to V 意為「著手、立志要（做某事）」，指哈里森不走天文觀測的路，而是著手打造時鐘。resort 須接 to + V-ing（resort to building），且帶有「不得已而為之」的意味；deign to V 指「屈尊去做」，語意不合；object 須接 to + V-ing，表「反對」，與文意相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Accordingly", "Instead", "Likewise", "Moreover"], "answer": 1, "explain": "前句說他的成功「理應」讓事情定案（ought to have settled 表示過去該發生卻沒有發生），後句說委員會反而要求更多試驗並質疑結果，結果與預期相反，應用 Instead「反而；結果卻」。Accordingly 因此、相應地；Likewise 同樣地；Moreover 此外，都無法表達與預期相反的轉折。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["vindicated", "exonerated", "confounded", "placated"], "answer": 2, "explain": "經度問題曾經難倒歐洲許多頂尖人物，confound 意為「使困惑、難倒」，a problem that had confounded… 即「曾讓……束手無策的難題」。vindicate 證明……是正確的；exonerate 免除（某人的）罪責；placate 安撫、平息怒氣，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C53", "title": "Plank by Plank: The Puzzle of Theseus's Ship", "title_zh": "一塊接一塊：忒修斯之船的謎題", "level": "C2", "topic": "philosophy and identity", "passage": "According to the Greek writer Plutarch, the Athenians preserved for many generations the ship in which their legendary hero Theseus had returned from Crete. As its old timbers decayed, they were taken out and replaced with new ones, and the vessel became a favorite example among philosophers, some of whom argued that it remained the same ship while others insisted that it did not. It has served ever since as an illustration of a puzzle that is easy to state but has proved notoriously __1__.\n\nCenturies later, the English philosopher Thomas Hobbes added an ingenious twist. Suppose, he proposed, that someone had collected every discarded plank and reassembled them into a second ship. Which of the two vessels would then have the better claim to be the original: the one that had been continuously repaired, or the one built entirely from the original materials? Each answer seems persuasive __2__ we consider the other.\n\nThe puzzle is more than an academic game. The question of what makes something the same over time __3__ in surprising places. Conservators restoring ancient buildings must decide how much original material can be replaced before a structure becomes a mere replica. Fans argue about whether a band that has gradually changed every one of its members is still the same band. Even our bodies raise the question: many of the cells that make them up are replaced over the course of a lifetime, though by no means all of them.\n\nPhilosophers have proposed several ways out. Some argue that identity depends on continuity: as long as changes happen gradually and the object goes on serving the same purpose, it remains the same thing. Others maintain that our everyday notion of \"sameness\" is simply too blunt a tool for such cases, and that asking which ship is the \"real\" one is __4__ asking exactly how many grains of sand it takes to make a heap.\n\nPerhaps the most valuable lesson of the ship, then, is not any particular answer but the realization that some of our most ordinary words rest on assumptions we rarely examine. We speak confidently of the same school, the same city and the same person we were as children, __5__ the fact that almost nothing about them has stayed unchanged.", "passage_zh": "根據希臘作家普魯塔克的記載，雅典人把傳說中的英雄忒修斯從克里特島返航時所搭乘的那艘船保存了好幾代。每當舊木材腐朽，就會被拆下來換上新的，這艘船也因此成了哲學家們最愛舉的例子：有些人主張它仍是同一艘船，另一些人則堅稱已經不是了。從那時起，它便一直被用來說明一個說起來容易、卻出了名地難以解決的謎題。\n\n好幾個世紀之後，英國哲學家湯瑪斯．霍布斯為這個問題加上了一個巧妙的轉折。他提出假設：如果有人把每一塊被拆下的木板都收集起來，重新組成第二艘船，那麼這兩艘船中，哪一艘更有資格被稱為原本的那艘？是不斷被修補的那一艘，還是完全用原始材料造成的那一艘？每一種答案看起來都很有說服力，直到我們想到另一種答案為止。\n\n這個謎題不只是學術上的遊戲。「是什麼讓一樣東西歷經時間後仍是同一樣東西」這個問題，常常在意想不到的地方冒出來。修復古建築的人員必須決定，最多能更換多少原始材料，才不至於讓建築物淪為一件複製品。樂迷們會爭論，一個陸續把所有成員都換過一輪的樂團，是否還是同一個樂團。就連我們的身體也引出這個問題：構成身體的許多細胞會在一生中陸續被汰換，儘管絕非全部如此。\n\n哲學家提出了幾種解套的方法。有些人主張，同一性取決於連續性：只要變化是逐步發生的，而且這樣東西持續發揮相同的功能，它就仍是同一樣東西。另一些人則認為，我們日常所說的「相同」這個概念，對這類情況來說根本是太粗糙的工具；問哪一艘才是「真正」的船，就好比問究竟要多少粒沙子才能堆成一座沙堆。\n\n如此說來，這艘船帶給我們最寶貴的啟示，或許不是任何一個特定的答案，而是讓我們體認到：我們一些最平常的用語，其實建立在我們很少檢視的假設之上。我們理直氣壯地談論同一所學校、同一座城市，以及和小時候同一個自己，儘管它們幾乎沒有任何一部分維持不變。", "blanks": [{"options": ["tractable", "self-evident", "superficial", "intractable"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前半句說這個謎題「說起來容易」（easy to state），but 之後語意轉折，應指它「出了名地難以解決」，intractable 意為「棘手的、難以處理的」。tractable 易處理的，與 but 的轉折相矛盾；self-evident 不證自明的；superficial 膚淺的，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["lest", "until", "whereby", "insofar as"], "answer": 1, "explain": "每個答案在我們想到另一個答案「之前」似乎都很有道理，一想到另一個答案就動搖了，應用表時間終點的 until「直到……為止」。lest 以免；whereby 藉此、憑此（關係副詞，前面須有先行詞）；insofar as 在……的範圍內，皆不合邏輯。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["wears off", "falls through", "crops up", "peters out"], "answer": 2, "explain": "後文列舉古建築修復、樂團成員、人體細胞等例子，說明這個問題會在意想不到的地方「出現」，crop up 意為「（問題等）突然出現、冒出來」。wear off（效果等）逐漸消退；fall through（計畫）落空；peter out 逐漸減少、消失，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["akin to", "averse to", "prone to", "privy to"], "answer": 0, "explain": "此句把「問哪艘船才是真的」比作「問多少粒沙才算一堆」這個同樣沒有明確界線的問題，be akin to 意為「類似於、相當於」，to 為介系詞，後接動名詞 asking。averse to 厭惡、反對；prone to 易於、傾向於（常指負面的事）；privy to 知悉（祕密），皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["owing to", "in view of", "by virtue of", "notwithstanding"], "answer": 3, "explain": "「我們理直氣壯地說同一所學校、同一個人」與「它們幾乎沒有一部分維持不變」語意相反，需要表讓步的 notwithstanding「儘管、雖然」（＝ despite）。owing to、in view of 與 by virtue of 都表「由於、因為」，代入後會造成因果矛盾。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C54", "title": "Dating the Earth, Finding the Poison: Clair Patterson's Unplanned Crusade", "title_zh": "測定地球年齡，卻發現了毒物：克萊爾．派特森意外的抗爭", "level": "C2", "topic": "environmental science", "passage": "In the late 1940s and early 1950s, the American geochemist Clair Patterson took on an extraordinarily ambitious task: to determine the age of the earth. His method relied on the slow, predictable decay of uranium into lead, which meant measuring minute quantities of lead with __1__ precision. Yet his samples kept producing absurd results. Lead, it turned out, was everywhere: in the air of his laboratory, in the dust on his equipment, even on the people handling the samples.\n\nTo get around the problem, Patterson developed painstaking methods for keeping contamination out of his laboratory, scrubbing every surface and filtering the air before he would trust a single measurement. The effort paid off. In 1956 he published an estimate of about 4.55 billion years, a figure that has __2__ remarkably well in the decades since.\n\nThe contamination that had nearly wrecked his work, however, raised a far more troubling question: where had all that lead come from? Patterson came to suspect that much of it originated in the exhaust of cars burning leaded gasoline, which had been sold widely since the 1920s. By analyzing seawater and layers of polar ice, he showed that lead levels in the environment had __3__ in the modern era, far beyond anything natural processes could explain.\n\nHis conclusions were deeply unwelcome to the powerful industry that manufactured the additive, and he faced years of fierce opposition from experts who defended it. Patterson refused to be silenced. He published his evidence, testified before lawmakers and kept pressing his case until the tide of scientific and public opinion slowly began to __4__.\n\nIn the 1970s the United States began to phase out leaded gasoline, and other countries gradually followed suit. Average levels of lead in people's blood fell sharply, a change of no small importance given that lead can impair the developing brains of children. In 2021 the United Nations announced that leaded gasoline was no longer sold for cars anywhere in the world. Patterson, who died in 1995, did not live to see that day, but millions of people owe their health in part to a scientist whose __5__ for an accurate number led him somewhere he had never intended to go.", "passage_zh": "1940 年代末到 1950 年代初，美國地球化學家克萊爾．派特森接下了一項極具野心的任務：測定地球的年齡。他的方法仰賴鈾緩慢而可預測地衰變成鉛的過程，這意味著他必須以極其嚴格的精確度測量微量的鉛。然而，他的樣本卻一再得出荒謬的結果。原來，鉛無所不在：在實驗室的空氣裡、在儀器的灰塵上，甚至在處理樣本的人身上。\n\n為了解決這個問題，派特森發展出一套極為嚴謹的方法，防止汙染進入實驗室：他把每一個表面刷洗乾淨、過濾空氣，然後才肯相信任何一筆測量數據。這番努力終於有了回報。1956 年，他發表了地球年齡約為 45.5 億年的估計值，這個數字在此後數十年間一直出奇地禁得起考驗。\n\n然而，那些差點毀了他研究的汙染，卻引出一個令人不安得多的問題：這些鉛到底是從哪裡來的？派特森逐漸懷疑，其中大部分來自汽車燃燒含鉛汽油所排出的廢氣——含鉛汽油自 1920 年代起便已廣泛販售。他藉由分析海水與極地冰層，證明環境中的鉛含量在現代急遽飆升，遠遠超出自然作用所能解釋的範圍。\n\n他的結論讓生產這種添加劑、勢力龐大的產業極為反感，他也因此遭到為其辯護的專家多年的猛烈反對。派特森拒絕噤聲。他發表證據、在國會作證，並持續力陳己見，直到科學界與大眾的輿論風向慢慢開始扭轉。\n\n1970 年代，美國開始逐步淘汰含鉛汽油，其他國家也陸續跟進。人們血液中的平均鉛濃度大幅下降；考慮到鉛會損害兒童發育中的大腦，這項改變意義非凡。2021 年，聯合國宣布全世界已不再有任何地方販售汽車用的含鉛汽油。派特森於 1995 年辭世，沒能親眼見到這一天，但數以百萬計的人能擁有健康，有一部分要歸功於這位科學家：他對一個精確數字的追求，把他帶到了一個他從未打算前往的地方。", "blanks": [{"options": ["exacting", "negligent", "haphazard", "cursory"], "answer": 0, "explain": "要測量「微量」的鉛，需要「極其嚴格」的精確度，exacting precision 為常見搭配，exacting 意為「要求嚴格的、一絲不苟的」。negligent 疏忽的；haphazard 隨意的、雜亂無章的；cursory 草率的、粗略的，都與精密測量的需求相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["broken down", "given out", "held up", "worn off"], "answer": 2, "explain": "hold up 在此意為「（論點、數據等）站得住腳、經得起檢驗」；45.5 億年的估計值數十年來仍被科學界接受，故用 has held up remarkably well。break down 故障、崩潰；give out 用完、（機器）停止運作；wear off（效果）逐漸消退，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["dwindled", "soared", "stagnated", "fluctuated"], "answer": 1, "explain": "由 far beyond anything natural processes could explain（遠超出自然作用所能解釋的範圍）可知，鉛含量在現代「急遽上升」，soar 意為「飆升」。dwindle 逐漸減少；stagnate 停滯不前；fluctuate 上下波動，皆無法表達大幅增加。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["recede", "swell", "linger", "turn"], "answer": 3, "explain": "the tide turns 為慣用說法，原指「潮水轉向」，引申為「情勢逆轉、風向改變」；派特森持續奮鬥，直到輿論開始轉向支持他的看法，故用 the tide…began to turn。recede（潮水）退去、減弱；swell 增強、膨脹；linger 逗留、徘徊不去，皆無法表達輿論「轉向」。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["aversion", "quest", "disdain", "pretext"], "answer": 1, "explain": "派特森原本只是想精確測定地球年齡，quest for… 意為「對……的追求、探尋」，正符合「他對一個精確數字的追求」。aversion 厭惡（常接 to）；disdain 鄙視；pretext 藉口，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C55", "title": "Work Expands to Fill the Time: Revisiting Parkinson's Law", "title_zh": "工作會膨脹到填滿所有時間：重探帕金森定律", "level": "C2", "topic": "work and productivity", "passage": "In 1955 the British historian C. Northcote Parkinson published a short, mischievous essay in The Economist whose opening sentence contained a line many office workers could now recite by heart: \"Work expands so as to fill the time available for its completion.\" To illustrate the point, he described an elderly lady with nothing else to do who spends an entire day sending a single postcard to her niece, devoting an hour to finding the card, another to hunting for her glasses, and so on, when a busy person might __1__ the same task in three minutes.\n\nParkinson's real target, however, was bureaucracy. Drawing on records from the British navy, he observed that the number of officials had kept rising even as the number of ships and sailors they were supposed to manage fell. Officials, he argued, prefer to multiply subordinates rather than rivals, and they make work for one another. An organization could therefore go on growing __2__ of how much there was actually to do.\n\nThe essay was written largely as satire, and Parkinson never pretended to have conducted a rigorous study. Even so, his \"law\" has __3__ a chord with generations of readers, perhaps because almost everyone has watched a simple job swell to fill an empty afternoon. A looming deadline tends to concentrate the mind; an open-ended one invites endless tinkering.\n\nSome managers have drawn a practical lesson from this: set tighter deadlines, and people will work faster. The advice is not without merit, but applied __4__, it can backfire. A deadline that is ambitious but realistic may sharpen concentration; one that is wildly unrealistic tends to produce hurried, careless work, or simply teaches staff that deadlines need not be taken seriously.\n\nPerhaps the most useful way to read Parkinson today, then, is not as a scientific law but as an invitation to notice how our time is actually spent. __5__ we stop to ask whether a task deserves the hours it is consuming, it will quietly take as many as we allow it.", "passage_zh": "1955 年，英國歷史學家 C．諾斯古德．帕金森在《經濟學人》發表了一篇簡短而略帶惡作劇意味的文章，文章的第一句話裡，有一句如今許多上班族都能倒背如流的名言：「工作會不斷膨脹，直到填滿可用來完成它的所有時間。」為了說明這一點，他描述一位無所事事的老太太花了一整天寄一張明信片給姪女：花一個小時找明信片，再花一個小時找眼鏡，諸如此類；而一個忙碌的人做同樣的事，可能三分鐘就搞定了。\n\n不過，帕金森真正針對的是官僚體系。他引用英國海軍的紀錄指出，儘管官員們應該管理的船艦與水兵數量減少了，官員的人數卻持續增加。他認為，官員寧可增加部屬，也不願增加對手，而且他們會彼此製造工作。因此，一個組織可能會不斷擴張，而與實際上有多少事情要做毫無關係。\n\n這篇文章大體上是以諷刺筆法寫成的，帕金森也從未假裝自己做過嚴謹的研究。即便如此，他的「定律」仍引起了一代又一代讀者的共鳴，或許是因為幾乎每個人都曾眼睜睜看著一件簡單的工作膨脹到填滿一整個空閒的下午。迫在眉睫的期限往往能讓人集中精神；沒有期限的工作則容易讓人無止境地東修西改。\n\n有些主管從中得出一個實用的教訓：把期限訂得緊一點，大家就會做得快一點。這個建議並非毫無道理，但若不分青紅皂白地套用，反而可能適得其反。有挑戰性但切合實際的期限或許能讓人更加專注；完全不切實際的期限則往往只會造成倉促草率的成果，或者乾脆讓員工學到：期限根本不必當真。\n\n如此看來，今天閱讀帕金森最有用的方式，或許不是把它當成一條科學定律，而是把它當成一種提醒，讓我們留意自己的時間實際上是怎麼花掉的。除非我們停下來問問某件工作是否值得耗費這麼多時間，否則它會悄悄占用我們容許它占用的所有時間。", "blanks": [{"options": ["put off", "fall behind", "polish off", "wind down"], "answer": 2, "explain": "此處與老太太花一整天寄一張明信片形成對比：忙碌的人三分鐘就能「迅速完成」同樣的事，polish off 意為「很快地做完、解決掉」。put off 拖延；fall behind 落後（如 fall behind schedule），與「迅速完成」語意相反；wind down 逐漸結束、放鬆下來，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["in light", "by dint", "on account", "irrespective"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前文說官員人數增加與要管理的船艦多寡無關，故組織擴張「不論」實際工作量多少，irrespective of 意為「不管、與……無關」。in light of 鑑於；by dint of 憑藉；on account of 由於，三者都表示依據或原因，與文意相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["struck", "played", "broken", "drawn"], "answer": 0, "explain": "strike a chord with sb 為固定搭配，意為「引起某人的共鳴」，has struck 為現在完成式；後文說幾乎每個人都有類似經驗，正說明這條「定律」引發廣泛共鳴。play a chord 只有字面上「彈奏和弦」的意思；break 與 draw 都不能和 chord 搭配表達共鳴。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["sparingly", "indiscriminately", "judiciously", "sporadically"], "answer": 1, "explain": "後文說明期限若訂得完全不切實際，反而會造成草率的成果，可知這個建議若「不加分辨地、一味地」套用就會適得其反，indiscriminately 意為「不加區別地、濫用地」。sparingly 節制地、少量地；judiciously 明智審慎地，兩者都不會導致反效果；sporadically 零星地、偶爾，與「反效果」沒有因果關係。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Provided", "Once", "Unless", "As long as"], "answer": 2, "explain": "句意為「除非我們停下來問一件工作是否值得花這麼多時間，否則它會悄悄占用我們容許的所有時間」，應用 Unless（＝ if…not）。Provided 與 As long as 都表「只要」，Once 表「一旦」，代入後會變成「只要／一旦我們去問，工作就會占用所有時間」，邏輯矛盾。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C56", "title": "Why Greenland Looks So Big: The Mercator Projection", "title_zh": "格陵蘭為何看起來這麼大：麥卡托投影法", "level": "C1", "topic": "cartography", "passage": "Look at the world map hanging on many classroom walls, and Greenland appears roughly as large as Africa. In reality, Africa is about fourteen times bigger. Such a map is not exactly lying; it is simply doing the job it was designed to do. That design dates back to 1569, when the Flemish cartographer Gerardus Mercator published a world map __1__ not for scholars or schoolchildren but for sailors crossing the open sea.\n\nFor a navigator, what matters most is not accurate area but accurate direction. On Mercator's map, a course that follows a constant compass bearing appears as a straight line, so a captain could draw a line between two ports, measure its angle and simply hold that heading. This convenience, however, came at a price. A globe cannot be flattened without being stretched or torn, so some degree of distortion is __2__ on any flat map. Mercator chose to space the lines of latitude further and further apart toward the poles, keeping shapes and angles correct within any small area. Only by enlarging the high latitudes in this way __3__ compass directions accurate across the whole map. The poles themselves, which would have to be stretched infinitely, cannot be shown at all.\n\nThe result is a map that is ideal for steering a ship but misleading as a picture of the world. Regions near the equator appear close to their true size, __4__ those far to the north or south, such as Greenland, Canada and Russia, look greatly enlarged. Because the projection became so familiar, some critics have argued that it quietly shapes how people judge the importance of different parts of the world.\n\nSince the twentieth century, Mercator's projection has __5__ considerable criticism as a way of showing the whole world, and many atlases now use projections that balance size and shape more evenly. Yet the old design has enjoyed an unexpected revival online. Many web maps use a version of it, because when users zoom in on a city, the streets need to meet at their true angles. The lesson is not that one projection is right and the others are wrong, but that every flat map involves choices, and a careful reader should always ask what a particular map was designed to show.", "passage_zh": "看看許多教室牆上掛的世界地圖，格陵蘭看起來都和非洲差不多大。事實上，非洲的面積大約是格陵蘭的十四倍。這樣的地圖並不完全是在說謊，它只是在做它原本被設計來做的事。這種設計可追溯到 1569 年，當時法蘭德斯製圖師麥卡托出版了一幅世界地圖；這幅地圖並不是為學者或學童而設計的，而是專為橫渡大海的水手所設計。\n\n對航海者來說，最重要的不是面積準確，而是方向準確。在麥卡托的地圖上，一條沿著固定羅盤方位前進的航線會呈現為一條直線，因此船長可以在兩個港口之間畫一條線，量出它的角度，然後只要一直維持那個航向即可。然而，這種便利是有代價的。地球儀不經拉伸或撕裂就無法攤平，因此任何平面地圖都不可避免地會有某種程度的變形。麥卡托選擇讓緯線越接近兩極、彼此的間距就越大，使任何小範圍內的形狀和角度都保持正確。唯有用這種方式放大高緯度地區，他才能讓整張地圖上的羅盤方向都保持準確。至於兩極本身，因為必須被無限拉長，根本無法呈現在地圖上。\n\n結果就是，這種地圖用來駕船航行十分理想，但作為世界的圖像卻容易誤導人。赤道附近的地區看起來接近實際大小，而遠在南北兩端的地區，例如格陵蘭、加拿大和俄羅斯，看起來則被大幅放大。由於這種投影法變得如此為人熟悉，一些批評者主張，它會在不知不覺中影響人們如何判斷世界各個地區的重要性。\n\n自二十世紀以來，麥卡托投影法作為呈現全世界的方式，受到了相當多的批評，許多地圖集如今改用能更平均兼顧大小與形狀的投影法。然而，這種古老的設計卻在網路上出乎意料地再度流行。許多網路地圖使用它的某種版本，因為使用者把畫面放大到某個城市時，街道必須以真實的角度相交。這給我們的啟示並不是某一種投影法正確、其他都錯誤，而是每一張平面地圖都涉及取捨；細心的讀者應該隨時問一問：某一張地圖原本是設計來呈現什麼的。", "blanks": [{"options": ["having intended", "intended", "intending", "to intend"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格前為名詞 a world map，空格引導修飾語，而地圖是「被設計、預定」給水手使用的，表被動，應用過去分詞 intended（＝ a world map that was intended not for… but for…）；be intended for「專為…而設計」。intending 為現在分詞，表主動，語意變成地圖自己「打算」；having intended 為完成式分詞，同樣表主動；to intend 為不定詞，亦表主動，語意不通。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["negligible", "optional", "reversible", "inevitable"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前半句說地球儀不經拉伸或撕裂就無法攤平，so 引導結果，可知任何平面地圖都「無法避免」某種程度的變形，inevitable「必然的；不可避免的」符合語意。negligible 微不足道的；optional 可選擇的；reversible 可逆轉的，皆與「無法避免」的因果關係不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["could he keep", "he could keep", "has he kept", "he has kept"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Only + 副詞片語（by enlarging the high latitudes in this way）置於句首時，主要子句須倒裝，即「助動詞＋主詞＋原形動詞」；本段敘述麥卡托在十六世紀的做法，應用過去式助動詞 could，故 could he keep 正確。he could keep 沒有倒裝；has he kept 與 he has kept 為現在完成式，與敘述過去事件的時態不符，且 he has kept 也沒有倒裝。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["unless", "so that", "whereas", "provided that"], "answer": 2, "explain": "空格前說赤道附近的地區看起來接近實際大小，空格後說南北兩端的地區被大幅放大，前後兩個子句形成對比，應用 whereas「而；然而」。unless 除非；so that 以便；provided that 只要、倘若，皆無法表達對比關係。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["come in for", "come up with", "come down to", "come across as"], "answer": 0, "explain": "come in for criticism 為常見搭配，意為「遭受批評」；後文說許多地圖集已改用能平均兼顧大小與形狀的投影法，可知麥卡托投影法作為呈現全世界的方式受到了相當多批評。come up with 想出（點子、辦法）；come down to 歸結為；come across as 給人…的印象，皆不能與 criticism 搭配表達「受到批評」。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C57", "title": "The Chemistry of a Golden Crust: The Maillard Reaction", "title_zh": "金黃外皮的化學：梅納反應", "level": "C1", "topic": "food chemistry", "passage": "Why does toast taste so different from plain bread, and a grilled steak so different from a boiled one? Much of the answer __1__ in a chemical process named after Louis-Camille Maillard, a French chemist who described it in 1912. When foods containing proteins and sugars are heated, amino acids react with certain sugars to yield hundreds of new compounds. Some of these produce deep, savory aromas, while others give the surface of the food its brown color.\n\nCooks relied on this reaction for centuries long before anyone could explain it. It __2__ for much of the flavor of bread crust, roasted coffee beans and fried onions. It is often confused with caramelization, but the two are chemically distinct: caramelization involves sugars alone, while the Maillard reaction requires amino acids as well.\n\nTemperature is crucial. The reaction takes place only slowly at room temperature and becomes rapid at temperatures well above the boiling point of water. As long as the surface of a food is wet, it can hardly get hotter than 100°C, so boiled meat stays pale and grey. It is for this reason __3__ experienced cooks pat meat dry before searing it and avoid crowding the pan, since too many pieces release steam that cools the surface.\n\nThe reaction is not without its __4__, however. In 2002, Swedish scientists reported that starchy foods such as potatoes and bread can form a chemical called acrylamide when they are fried, roasted or baked at high temperatures, and researchers soon traced it to the Maillard reaction. Acrylamide has been shown to cause cancer in laboratory animals. Although its effects on humans at the levels found in food are still debated, food safety agencies advise cooking fries and toast to a golden yellow rather than a dark brown.\n\nNone of this means that browned food should be avoided altogether. __5__, it suggests that the golden crust we prize is the product of a delicate balance: cooked too little, food tastes flat; cooked too much, it turns bitter and may be less healthy. Understanding the chemistry behind that balance can help any cook get it right.", "passage_zh": "為什麼吐司吃起來和一般麵包如此不同，烤牛排又和水煮的牛排如此不同？答案有很大一部分在於一種以法國化學家梅納（Louis-Camille Maillard）命名的化學過程，他在 1912 年描述了這種反應。含有蛋白質和糖的食物受熱時，胺基酸會與某些糖類發生反應，產生數百種新的化合物。其中有些會散發濃郁的鹹香氣味，有些則使食物表面呈現褐色。\n\n早在有人能解釋這種反應之前，廚師們就已經仰賴它好幾個世紀了。麵包外皮、烘焙咖啡豆和炒洋蔥的風味，大多來自這種反應。它常被誤認為焦糖化，但兩者在化學上截然不同：焦糖化只涉及糖類，而梅納反應還需要胺基酸。\n\n溫度是關鍵。這種反應在室溫下只會緩慢進行，要到遠高於水沸點的溫度才會變得迅速。只要食物表面是濕的，溫度就很難超過攝氏 100 度，因此水煮的肉始終呈現蒼白、灰暗的顏色。正是因為這個緣故，有經驗的廚師在煎肉之前會先把肉拍乾，並避免在鍋裡一次放太多塊，因為肉塊太多會釋出蒸氣，使食物表面降溫。\n\n然而，這種反應並非沒有缺點。2002 年，瑞典科學家發表報告指出，馬鈴薯和麵包等澱粉類食物在高溫下油炸、烘烤或焙烤時，可能產生一種名為丙烯醯胺的化學物質，研究人員很快便追查出它來自梅納反應。丙烯醯胺已被證實會使實驗動物罹癌。雖然食物中所含的劑量對人體有何影響仍有爭議，但食品安全機構建議，薯條和吐司應烹調至金黃色，而非深褐色。\n\n這一切並不表示應該完全避免煎烤至焦黃的食物。更確切地說，這顯示我們所珍視的金黃外皮，其實是微妙平衡下的產物：烹調不足，食物淡而無味；烹調過度，則會變苦，也可能比較不健康。了解這種平衡背後的化學原理，能幫助任何一位廚師拿捏得恰到好處。", "blanks": [{"options": ["falls", "stays", "lies", "holds"], "answer": 2, "explain": "the answer lies in…「答案在於…」為固定搭配，lie in 意為「在於；存在於」，此處指吐司與烤牛排風味不同的原因，大部分在於梅納反應這種化學過程。falls 落下（fall in 為倒塌、排隊）；stays 停留；holds 持有、維持，皆不能與 in 搭配表達「在於」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["accounts", "explains", "causes", "results"], "answer": 0, "explain": "account for 意為「是…的原因；說明；占（若干比例）」，此處指這種反應造就了麵包外皮、咖啡豆與炒洋蔥大部分的風味。explain 與 cause 皆為及物動詞，後面直接接受詞，不接 for；result 須用 result in（導致）或 result from（起因於），不與 for 搭配。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["which", "why", "what", "that"], "answer": 3, "explain": "本句為分裂句（強調句）It is … that …，強調副詞片語 for this reason，後面應用 that 連接其餘部分。which 與 what 不能用於分裂句中強調副詞片語；why 雖可接在 the reason 之後作關係副詞，但 for this reason 已表原因，分裂句的固定結構為 It is for this reason that…，不寫成 why。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["merits", "drawbacks", "admirers", "precedents"], "answer": 1, "explain": "be not without its + 名詞 意為「也有…；並非沒有…」。由 however 及後文提到此反應可能產生使實驗動物罹癌的丙烯醯胺可知，此處指它也有「缺點」，應用 drawbacks。not without its merits 意為「也有優點」，與後文的負面內容不符；not without its admirers（也有人欣賞）雖也是常見說法，但同樣無法與後文的健康疑慮呼應；precedents 先例，不合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Rather", "Moreover", "Similarly", "Otherwise"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句以否定說明「這並不表示應完全避免焦黃的食物」，本句接著提出更正確的理解，應用 Rather「更確切地說；反而」，常用於否定陳述之後。Moreover 此外，用於補充同方向的資訊；Similarly 同樣地；Otherwise 否則，皆不合前後的邏輯關係。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C58", "title": "Letters for the People: King Sejong and the Birth of Hangul", "title_zh": "為百姓而造的文字：世宗大王與韓文的誕生", "level": "C1", "topic": "writing systems", "passage": "For centuries, Koreans wrote mainly in Classical Chinese, sometimes using systems that borrowed Chinese characters to represent Korean words and grammar. All of these methods required years of study, since a reader needed to know thousands of characters. As a result, literacy remained largely the __1__ of a small aristocratic elite, while most farmers, merchants and women had no means of recording their thoughts or reading official notices.\n\nIn the fifteenth century, King Sejong, the fourth ruler of the Joseon dynasty, set __2__ to change this. He is credited with creating a new alphabet, which was completed in 1443 and officially introduced in 1446 in a document called Hunminjeongeum, meaning \"The Correct Sounds for the Instruction of the People.\" The name itself made the king's purpose clear: the script was meant for ordinary people, not just for scholars.\n\nThe design of the alphabet is remarkably systematic. The basic consonants were shaped to __3__ the position of the tongue, lips, teeth or throat when each sound is pronounced, while the vowels were built from three simple elements representing heaven, earth and humankind. Letters are combined into syllable blocks, which gives written Korean its distinctive square appearance. Because a small set of letters can represent every sound in the language, a quick learner can master the basics in a matter of days.\n\nNot everyone welcomed the invention. Some scholar-officials protested that turning away from Chinese characters would cut Korea off from the wider civilized world, and for centuries much of the elite looked down on the new script as fit only for women and commoners. __4__ they had their way, the alphabet might have disappeared altogether. Instead, it survived in private letters, popular novels and religious texts, spreading slowly among the very people it had been created to serve.\n\nNot until the late nineteenth century did the government begin using the script in official documents, and the name Hangul came into general use only in the early twentieth century. Today, South Korea celebrates Hangul Day every October 9, and a copy of the 1446 document is listed on UNESCO's Memory of the World Register. Nearly six centuries after its creation, the alphabet stands as a __5__ to the idea that the ability to read and write should belong to everyone, not just a privileged few.", "passage_zh": "好幾個世紀以來，韓國人主要以古典漢文書寫，有時也使用借用漢字來表示韓語詞彙和文法的書寫系統。這些方法都需要多年的學習，因為讀者必須認識數千個漢字。因此，識字能力大致上仍是少數貴族菁英的專利，而大多數農民、商人和婦女都無從記錄自己的想法，也無法閱讀官方公告。\n\n十五世紀時，朝鮮王朝第四代君主世宗大王著手改變這種情況。一般認為是他創造了一套新的字母；這套字母在 1443 年完成，並於 1446 年透過一份名為《訓民正音》的文件正式頒布，書名意為「教導百姓的正確字音」。這個名稱本身就清楚表明了國王的用意：這套文字是為一般百姓而設計的，而不只是為了學者。\n\n這套字母的設計極有系統。基本子音的字形，是依照發出各個音時舌頭、嘴唇、牙齒或喉嚨的位置描繪而成，而母音則由代表天、地、人的三個簡單元素組合而成。字母會組合成一個個音節方塊，使書寫的韓文呈現獨特的方正外觀。由於少數幾個字母就能表示這個語言中的每一個音，學得快的人只要幾天就能掌握基礎。\n\n並不是每個人都歡迎這項發明。一些士大夫抗議說，捨棄漢字會使朝鮮與更廣大的文明世界隔絕；而且好幾個世紀以來，許多菁英都看不起這套新文字，認為它只適合婦女和平民使用。假如這些人當初得償所願，這套字母可能早已完全消失。然而，它在私人書信、通俗小說和宗教典籍中存續了下來，並在它當初所要服務的百姓之間慢慢傳開。\n\n直到十九世紀末，政府才開始在官方文件中使用這套文字，而「韓文」（Hangul）這個名稱也直到二十世紀初才普遍使用。如今，南韓每年 10 月 9 日都會慶祝韓文日，而 1446 年那份文件的一部傳本也被列入聯合國教科文組織的「世界記憶名錄」。在誕生將近六個世紀之後，這套字母證明了一個理念：讀寫能力應該屬於每一個人，而不只是少數享有特權的人。", "blanks": [{"options": ["burden", "downfall", "rival", "preserve"], "answer": 3, "explain": "the preserve of + 某群體 意為「某群體專屬的領域或特權」，此處指讀寫能力大多仍只屬於少數貴族菁英，與後文一般農民、商人和婦女無法讀寫形成對照。burden 負擔，與「識字是少數人的特權」語意相反；downfall 垮台、衰敗；rival 對手，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["off", "up", "out", "about"], "answer": 2, "explain": "set out to + 原形動詞 意為「著手；立志要（做某事）」，符合世宗大王決心改變這種現況的語意。set about 雖然也有「著手」之意，但後面須接動名詞（set about changing this），不接不定詞；set off 為出發、引發；set up 為建立、設立，皆不接 to + 原形動詞表達此意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["dictate", "depict", "deplete", "deprive"], "answer": 1, "explain": "depict 意為「描繪；表現」，指基本子音的字形是依照發音時舌頭、嘴唇、牙齒或喉嚨的位置描繪而成。dictate 口述、命令；deplete 耗盡；deprive 剝奪（常用 deprive sb of sth），四個選項字形相近，但只有 depict 符合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Had", "If", "Should", "Were"], "answer": 0, "explain": "主要子句 might have disappeared 為「與過去事實相反」的假設語氣，條件子句原為 If they had had their way（假如他們當初得償所願），省略 If 後將助動詞 had 移到主詞前，成為 Had they had their way。If they had their way 為與現在事實相反的形式，與主要子句的時態不符；Should 倒裝用於未來可能發生的情況，須接原形動詞；Were 倒裝須接 to V 或補語，Should they had、Were they had 皆不合文法。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["threat", "barrier", "testament", "exception"], "answer": 2, "explain": "stand as a testament to sth 意為「是…的明證；證明了…」，指這套為一般百姓而創造的字母，證明了「讀寫能力應屬於每一個人」這個理念。threat 威脅；barrier 障礙，皆與韓文推廣識字的作用相反；exception 例外，也不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C59", "title": "Saving a Famous Flaw: The Rescue of the Leaning Tower of Pisa", "title_zh": "拯救一個著名的缺陷：比薩斜塔搶救記", "level": "C1", "topic": "engineering and heritage", "passage": "Few buildings owe their fame to a construction problem, but the bell tower of Pisa Cathedral in Italy is a striking exception. Work began in 1173, and by the time the builders had reached the third floor, the tower had already begun to sink into the soft clay and sand on its southern side. Construction was then halted for almost a century, largely __1__ wars between Pisa and its rival cities.\n\nIronically, this long interruption probably saved the tower, because it gave the soil beneath it time to compress and grow stronger under the weight already placed on it. If the builders had pressed on without a break, the tower almost certainly __2__ standing today. When work finally resumed, the builders tried to compensate for the tilt by making the upper floors slightly taller on the lower side, so the tower is in fact subtly curved.\n\nThe lean nevertheless kept increasing over the centuries. By 1990 it had become so severe that the authorities took the drastic __3__ of closing the tower to the public, fearing that it might topple. An international committee of experts was set up to find a way of stabilizing it. Straightening the tower completely was never the goal; after all, a perfectly vertical tower would no longer be the famous Leaning Tower. As a temporary measure, hundreds of tonnes of lead weights were placed on the northern side of its base.\n\nThe decisive solution, however, was surprisingly gentle. Between 1999 and 2001, engineers used angled drills to remove small amounts of soil from beneath the northern side, allowing the ground there to __4__ slightly and the tower to tilt gradually back toward the vertical. By the time the work was completed, the top had moved back by about 45 centimeters, returning the tower roughly to the position it had occupied in the 1830s. It reopened to visitors in December 2001, its lean reduced but by no means __5__, and tourists can still pose for photographs pretending to hold it up.", "passage_zh": "很少有建築物是因為施工上的問題而出名，但義大利比薩大教堂的鐘樓卻是一個顯著的例外。這座塔在 1173 年動工，等到工人蓋到第三層時，塔身已經開始陷入南側鬆軟的黏土和沙土之中。隨後工程停擺了將近一個世紀，主要是由於比薩與敵對城市之間的戰爭。\n\n諷刺的是，這次漫長的中斷很可能反而救了這座塔，因為它讓塔下的土壤有時間在已承受的重量下壓實、變得更加堅固。如果當年工人不停地繼續施工，這座塔今天幾乎肯定已不再屹立。等到工程終於恢復時，建造者把上方各層在較低的一側蓋得稍微高一些，試圖藉此彌補傾斜，因此這座塔實際上是微微彎曲的。\n\n儘管如此，幾個世紀以來，傾斜的程度仍持續增加。到了 1990 年，傾斜已嚴重到讓當局採取了關閉斜塔、不對外開放的斷然措施，因為擔心它可能會倒塌。當局成立了一個國際專家委員會，尋找穩定這座塔的方法。把塔完全扶正從來就不是目標；畢竟，一座完全垂直的塔就不再是那座著名的斜塔了。作為暫時性的措施，數百公噸的鉛塊被放置在塔基的北側。\n\n然而，真正具有決定性的解決辦法卻出奇地溫和。1999 年至 2001 年間，工程師利用斜向的鑽孔，從北側下方挖除少量土壤，讓那裡的地面略微下陷，使塔身逐漸往垂直的方向回傾。工程完成時，塔頂已往回移動了約 45 公分，使斜塔大致回到 1830 年代時的位置。斜塔在 2001 年 12 月重新開放參觀，傾斜程度雖已減少，卻絕對沒有消失；遊客至今仍能擺出假裝撐住斜塔的姿勢拍照。", "blanks": [{"options": ["owing to", "in spite of", "instead of", "regardless of"], "answer": 0, "explain": "空格後的「比薩與敵對城市之間的戰爭」是工程停擺將近一個世紀的原因，應用表示原因的 owing to「由於」（＝ because of）。in spite of 儘管；instead of 而不是；regardless of 不管、不顧，皆無法表示原因。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["will not be", "would not be", "had not been", "has not been"], "answer": 1, "explain": "本句為混合假設語氣：if 子句 had pressed on 表示與過去事實相反（當年工程其實中斷了），主要子句有 today，表示與現在事實相反，應用 would + 原形動詞，即 would not be standing today「今天就不會還屹立著」。will not be 為直述語氣的未來式，不能用於與事實相反的假設；had not been 與 has not been 都不能作為假設語氣的主要子句。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["pace", "stride", "step", "walk"], "answer": 2, "explain": "take the (drastic) step of + 動名詞 為固定搭配，意為「採取（斷然的）措施去做…」，此處指當局採取了關閉斜塔、不對外開放的斷然措施。pace 步調、速度；stride 大步（make strides 為「取得進展」）；walk 散步、步行，皆無 take the … of doing 的用法。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["subsidize", "subscribe", "substitute", "subside"], "answer": 3, "explain": "subside 可指「（地面、建築物）下陷、沉降」，工程師從北側下方挖除少量土壤，使那裡的地面略微下陷，塔身才能逐漸往回傾。subsidize 補助；subscribe 訂閱、認購；substitute 代替，三者字形相近，但語意皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["exaggerated", "eliminated", "accelerated", "exposed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "its lean reduced but by no means ___ 為獨立分詞構句，by no means 意為「絕不」，與 reduced「減少」形成對比；前文也提到工程的目標本來就不是把塔完全扶正，而遊客至今仍能假裝撐住斜塔拍照，可知傾斜「減少了，但絕沒有消除」，應用 eliminated。exaggerated 誇大；accelerated 加速；exposed 暴露，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C60", "title": "A Concert Without a Single Note", "title_zh": "一場沒有任何音符的演出", "level": "C1", "topic": "music and art", "passage": "On 29 August 1952, the pianist David Tudor walked onto the stage of a concert hall in Woodstock, New York, sat down at the piano and, instead of playing, closed the lid over the keyboard. Over the next four minutes and thirty-three seconds, he did not play a single note. He raised the lid at the end of each of the work's three movements and closed it again at the start of the next, but otherwise sat still, turning the pages of the score and watching a stopwatch. The audience had just heard the premiere of 4'33\", which would become the best-known and most __1__ work by the American composer John Cage.\n\nMany listeners were baffled, and some suspected that Cage was simply __2__. Yet the composer insisted that the piece was not silent at all. He later recalled that during the first movement the audience could hear the wind stirring outside, during the second rain began to fall on the roof, and during the third people themselves made all kinds of noises as they talked or walked out. What 4'33\" offered, in his view, __3__ a frame: a fixed stretch of time in which the ordinary sounds of the room became the music.\n\nCage's belief that true silence does not exist grew partly out of a visit in 1951 to an anechoic chamber at Harvard University, a room designed to absorb echoes and shut out noise from outside. Expecting to hear nothing, he heard two sounds, one high and one low. He was told that the high one was his nervous system at work and the low one his blood circulating. __4__ he was alive, Cage concluded, there would always be something to hear. He was also encouraged by the all-white paintings of his friend Robert Rauschenberg, whose blank surfaces changed with the light and shadows falling on them.\n\nMore than seventy years later, 4'33\" still divides opinion. Some regard it as a profound lesson in attention; others dismiss it as an empty gesture. __5__ one's view, it is hard to deny that the piece raises questions that are easier to ask than to answer. Where does music end and noise begin? And is a composer's task to create sounds, or to teach us how to listen?", "passage_zh": "1952 年 8 月 29 日，鋼琴家大衛・都鐸走上紐約州伍德斯托克一座音樂廳的舞台，在鋼琴前坐下，卻沒有彈奏，而是闔上了琴鍵蓋。在接下來的四分三十三秒裡，他一個音符也沒有彈。在這首作品三個樂章的每一個結尾，他都會掀起琴鍵蓋，到下一個樂章開始時再闔上；除此之外，他只是靜靜坐著，翻著樂譜、看著碼錶。觀眾剛剛聽到的，正是〈4'33\"〉的首演；這首作品後來成為美國作曲家約翰・凱吉最廣為人知、也最具爭議的作品。\n\n許多聽眾困惑不解，有些人甚至懷疑凱吉只是在捉弄他們。然而，這位作曲家堅稱這首作品根本不是無聲的。他後來回憶，第一樂章時，觀眾可以聽見外頭的風聲；第二樂章時，雨開始落在屋頂上；到了第三樂章，觀眾自己在交談或離席時，也發出了各式各樣的聲響。在他看來，〈4'33\"〉所提供的是一個框架：一段固定的時間，在這段時間裡，現場的日常聲響就成了音樂。\n\n凱吉認為真正的寂靜並不存在，這個信念有一部分源自他在 1951 年造訪哈佛大學的一間無響室；那是一個設計來吸收回音、隔絕外界噪音的房間。他原本預期什麼也聽不到，卻聽見了兩種聲音，一高一低。有人告訴他，高音是他的神經系統在運作，低音則是他的血液在循環。凱吉由此得出結論：只要他還活著，就永遠會有聲音可聽。他也受到好友羅伯特・勞森伯格那些全白畫作的鼓舞；那些空白的畫面會隨著投射在上面的光線與陰影而變化。\n\n七十多年後的今天，〈4'33\"〉仍然引發兩極的看法。有些人認為它是一堂關於專注的深刻課程；另一些人則認為它只是空洞的姿態，對它不屑一顧。無論一個人的看法如何，都很難否認這首作品提出了一些問起來容易、回答起來困難的問題：音樂在哪裡結束，噪音又從哪裡開始？作曲家的任務是創造聲音，還是教導我們如何聆聽？", "blanks": [{"options": ["anonymous", "unanimous", "notorious", "obscure"], "answer": 2, "explain": "notorious 意為「惡名昭彰的；因爭議而出名的」。由下一段聽眾困惑不解、甚至懷疑作曲家在捉弄他們可知，這首作品因極具爭議而聞名，與 best-known 並列也合理。anonymous 匿名的，但作曲者明確是凱吉；unanimous 意見一致的，用來形容人或決定，不能形容作品；obscure 默默無聞的，與 best-known 矛盾。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["pulling their weight", "pulling himself together", "pulling out all the stops", "pulling their leg"], "answer": 3, "explain": "pull sb's leg 為慣用語，意為「開某人玩笑；捉弄某人」，符合聽眾困惑不解、懷疑作曲家只是在耍他們的情境，下一句 Yet 也接著說作曲家堅稱這首作品是認真的。pull one's weight 盡自己的本分；pull oneself together 振作起來、恢復冷靜；pull out all the stops 竭盡全力，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["was", "were", "being", "it was"], "answer": 0, "explain": "本句為以 What 引導名詞子句作主詞的準分裂句：What 4'33\" offered（in his view）___ a frame。what 子句作主詞時視為單數，補語 a frame 也是單數，故動詞用 was。were 為複數動詞，與主詞不一致；being 為分詞，句子會缺少主要動詞；it was 多了一個主詞 it，與 what 子句重複。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["As far as", "As long as", "As soon as", "As well as"], "answer": 1, "explain": "as long as 可表「只要；在…的期間」，此處指只要他還活著，身體就會持續發出聲音，所以永遠會有聲音可聽，呼應前句他在無響室裡聽見自己神經系統和血液循環的聲音。as far as 就…而言、遠至；as soon as 一…就；as well as 以及，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["However", "Whichever", "Whoever", "Whatever"], "answer": 3, "explain": "Whatever one's view (may be) 意為「無論一個人的看法如何」，whatever 可直接接名詞片語並省略 may be，表示讓步。However 後面須接形容詞或副詞（如 however strongly one feels）；Whichever 作限定詞時直接修飾名詞（如 whichever view one takes），不能再接所有格 one's；Whoever 指人，不能修飾 one's view。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C61", "title": "Talking Across the Ravines: The Whistled Speech of La Gomera", "title_zh": "隔著峽谷交談：戈梅拉島的口哨語", "level": "C1", "topic": "language and culture", "passage": "On La Gomera, a small volcanic island in Spain's Canary Islands, the landscape seems almost designed to keep people apart. Deep ravines divide the island into steep, isolated valleys, and a distance that a bird could cross in minutes may take a person on foot several hours. Faced with this terrain, the islanders hit upon an ingenious solution: instead of climbing down one slope and up the next to deliver a message, they simply whistled it. The result, known as Silbo Gomero, is a whistled form of Spanish whose piercing notes can __1__ for several kilometers.\n\nSilbo is not a simple code made up of a few agreed signals; __2__, it is a genuine way of speaking. Whistlers keep the rhythm of ordinary Spanish but replace its vowels and consonants with whistled tones that rise and fall in pitch or are cut off abruptly. With enough practice, a skilled whistler can convey virtually any message, from an invitation to a wedding to a warning that a storm is on its way. The tradition is believed to date back to the island's original inhabitants, whose whistling was gradually adapted to Spanish after the island came under Spanish rule in the fifteenth century.\n\nFor centuries, Silbo was simply part of everyday life, used by shepherds calling to one another across the hills and by families passing on news from one valley to the next. In the second half of the twentieth century, however, it began to fall into __3__. New roads made it far easier to travel around the island, telephones made whistling across valleys unnecessary, and many young people left to seek work elsewhere. __4__ the determined efforts of local teachers and officials, Silbo might well have vanished along with its last elderly speakers.\n\nIn 1999, Silbo became a compulsory subject in the island's schools, so that every child there now learns at least the basics. Ten years later, UNESCO added it to its Representative List of the Intangible Cultural Heritage of Humanity, recognizing it as a living tradition __5__ protecting. Scientists have been fascinated too: brain-imaging research has found that, in experienced whistlers, Silbo activates the same regions of the brain that are involved in processing spoken language. Language, it seems, is not tied to the human voice alone.", "passage_zh": "戈梅拉島是西班牙加那利群島中的一座火山小島，島上的地形幾乎像是刻意要把人們隔開似的。深邃的峽谷把全島切割成一座座陡峭而孤立的山谷，鳥兒幾分鐘就能飛越的距離，人徒步走過去可能要花上好幾個小時。面對這樣的地形，島民想出了一個巧妙的辦法：他們不必為了傳個口信而爬下一面山坡、再爬上另一面山坡，只要用口哨把訊息吹過去就行了。這種做法就是所謂的「戈梅拉口哨語」，它是一種用口哨吹出來的西班牙語，尖銳響亮的哨音可以傳到好幾公里之外。\n\n口哨語並不是由少數幾個約定好的信號所組成的簡單暗號；相反地，它是一種真正的說話方式。吹哨者保留日常西班牙語的節奏，但把其中的母音和子音換成音高或升或降、或戛然而止的哨音。只要經過足夠的練習，技巧純熟的吹哨者幾乎可以傳達任何訊息，從婚禮的邀請到暴風雨即將來襲的警告都不成問題。一般認為，這項傳統可以追溯到島上的原住民；十五世紀這座島歸西班牙統治之後，他們的口哨便逐漸被改用來表達西班牙語。\n\n幾百年來，口哨語一直是日常生活的一部分：牧羊人隔著山丘用它互相呼喊，家家戶戶也用它把消息從一座山谷傳到另一座山谷。然而到了二十世紀下半葉，它開始逐漸被棄而不用。新的道路讓人們在島上往來方便許多，電話讓隔著山谷吹口哨變得沒有必要，許多年輕人也離鄉到外地謀生。要不是當地教師和官員的積極投入，口哨語很可能早已隨著最後一批年長的使用者一起消失了。\n\n1999 年，口哨語成為島上學校的必修科目，因此現在當地每個孩子至少都會學到基本的吹法。十年後，聯合國教科文組織將它列入「人類非物質文化遺產代表作名錄」，肯定它是值得保護的活傳統。科學家也對它深感興趣：腦部造影研究發現，在經驗豐富的吹哨者腦中，口哨語所活化的區域，正是處理口語時會用到的那些腦區。看來，語言並不只依附於人類的聲音。", "blanks": [{"options": ["linger", "carry", "fade", "dwell"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格須填入表示「（聲音）傳到遠處」的動詞。carry 作不及物動詞時可指聲音傳得遠，sound carries 為常見用法，此處指尖銳的哨音可傳到數公里之外。linger 逗留、久久不散，指時間上的延續；fade 逐漸消失，與哨音能跨越山谷的語意相反；dwell 居住、細想，不能用來形容聲音的傳播，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["otherwise", "likewise", "nonetheless", "rather"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句以 not 否定口哨語是「由少數信號組成的簡單暗號」，後句提出正確的說法「它是一種真正的說話方式」，應用 rather「相反地；更確切地說」來修正前述說法，形成 not A; rather, B 的結構。otherwise 否則；likewise 同樣地；nonetheless 儘管如此（表讓步），皆無法表達「否定前者、改提後者」的語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["disuse", "disrepair", "disarray", "dismay"], "answer": 0, "explain": "fall into disuse 為固定搭配，意為「逐漸被棄置不用」，符合下文新道路、電話普及與年輕人外移，使口哨語漸漸沒人使用的描述。fall into disrepair 指建築物或機器「年久失修」，用於有形的物體；fall into disarray 指「陷入混亂」；dismay 為「驚愕、沮喪」，是人的情緒，不能用來描述一種語言的處境。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["Thanks to", "Instead of", "But for", "Apart from"], "answer": 2, "explain": "主要子句 might well have vanished 為「might + have + 過去分詞」，表示與過去事實相反的假設結果，可知空格須表達假設條件。But for + 名詞 相當於 If it had not been for…「要不是…」，But for the determined efforts… 即「要不是當地教師和官員的積極投入」。Thanks to 意為「多虧」，表示原因，與「口哨語很可能早已消失」的結果互相矛盾；Instead of 意為「而不是」，Apart from 意為「除了…之外」，皆無假設條件的語意，也不合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["worthy", "worth", "deserving", "valuable"], "answer": 1, "explain": "worth + V-ing 表示「值得…」，動名詞以主動形式表達被動意義；a living tradition worth protecting 即「值得保護的活傳統」，為形容詞片語後位修飾名詞。worthy 須用 worthy of protection 或 worthy to be protected；deserving 須用 deserving of protection；valuable 不能直接接動名詞，三者後面都不能直接接 protecting。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C62", "title": "The Year Without a Summer: When a Volcano Changed the Weather", "title_zh": "沒有夏天的一年：一座火山如何改變天氣", "level": "C1", "topic": "volcanoes and climate", "passage": "In April 1815, Mount Tambora, a volcano on the Indonesian island of Sumbawa, produced the most powerful eruption in recorded history. The explosions were heard more than a thousand kilometers away, and clouds of ash turned day into night across the surrounding region. The death toll __1__ into the tens of thousands, with many victims dying not in the eruption itself but from the hunger and disease that followed as crops and livestock were destroyed.\n\nYet the disaster's effects reached far beyond Indonesia. The eruption hurled vast quantities of sulfur gas into the stratosphere, where it formed a fine haze of tiny droplets. Carried by high-altitude winds, this haze spread around the globe and __2__ part of the incoming sunlight back into space, cooling the planet below. Because the droplets stayed aloft for many months, the cooling persisted long after the eruption itself had ended.\n\nThe following year, 1816, came to be known in Europe and North America as \"the Year Without a Summer.\" In New England, snow fell in June and frosts struck again in July and August, killing crops in the fields. Across much of Europe, cold and relentless rain ruined harvests, food prices soared, and hunger drove desperate people to riot. __3__ indoors by the gloomy weather, a group of young writers staying near Lake Geneva that June passed the time by challenging one another to write ghost stories. One of them, eighteen-year-old Mary Godwin, later known as Mary Shelley, began the tale that would become Frankenstein.\n\nAt the time, few people suspected that a volcano thousands of kilometers away might be to blame. Only in the twentieth century __4__ scientists piece together the connection between Tambora and the strange weather of 1816. The episode remains a sobering reminder of how __5__ human societies can be to sudden shifts in climate, and of how events on one side of the planet can shape lives on the other.", "passage_zh": "1815 年 4 月，印尼松巴哇島上的坦博拉火山發生了有史以來威力最強的一次噴發。爆炸聲在一千多公里外都聽得到，火山灰形成的雲層更讓周遭地區白晝如夜。死亡人數高達數萬人，其中許多罹難者並非死於噴發本身，而是死於農作物與牲畜被摧毀之後隨之而來的飢荒與疾病。\n\n然而，這場災難的影響遠遠超出了印尼。噴發將大量含硫氣體拋入平流層，在那裡形成一層由微小液滴構成的薄霧。這層薄霧隨著高空的風擴散到全球，把部分射入的陽光反射回太空，使下方的地球變得寒冷。由於這些液滴在高空中停留了好幾個月，噴發結束許久之後，降溫的效應仍持續不退。\n\n隔年，也就是 1816 年，在歐洲和北美洲被稱為「沒有夏天的一年」。在美國新英格蘭地區，六月下雪，七月和八月又再度降霜，凍死了田裡的農作物。在歐洲大部分地區，寒冷且下個不停的雨毀了收成，糧價飛漲，飢餓更迫使走投無路的人們起而暴動。那年六月，一群住在日內瓦湖附近的年輕作家因天氣陰沉而被困在屋內，便以互相比賽寫鬼故事來打發時間。其中一位是十八歲的瑪麗．戈德溫，也就是後來的瑪麗．雪萊，她當時開始動筆的故事，日後成了《科學怪人》。\n\n當時很少有人想到，罪魁禍首可能是數千公里外的一座火山。直到二十世紀，科學家才拼湊出坦博拉火山與 1816 年異常天氣之間的關聯。這段往事至今仍發人深省，它提醒我們，人類社會面對氣候的驟變時可能有多麼脆弱，也提醒我們，地球一端發生的事，可能左右另一端人們的生活。", "blanks": [{"options": ["ran", "came", "fell", "broke"], "answer": 0, "explain": "run into + 數量 為常見搭配，意為「（數量、金額）高達…」，the death toll ran into the tens of thousands 即「死亡人數高達數萬人」。come into 進入、繼承；fall into 落入、陷入；break into 闖入、突然開始（如 break into tears），皆不能與數量搭配表示「高達」。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["emitted", "transmitted", "reflected", "generated"], "answer": 2, "explain": "微小液滴形成的薄霧把部分射入的陽光「反射」回太空，地球因此變冷，應用 reflected。emit 發出（自身產生的光、熱或氣體），但液滴本身並不會產生陽光；transmit 傳送、讓（光線）穿透，與 back into space「回到太空」的方向不合；generate 產生，同樣不合陽光來自太陽的事實。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["Confining", "To confine", "Having confined", "Confined"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格引導分詞構句，修飾主詞 a group of young writers。作家們是「被」陰沉的天氣困在屋內，主詞與動詞為被動關係，且句中有 by the gloomy weather 表示動作者，應用過去分詞 Confined（＝ Because they were confined indoors…）。Confining 與 Having confined 為主動形式，表示作家們去困住別人；To confine 表目的「為了困住」，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["have", "did", "had", "do"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Only + 副詞片語 置於句首時，主要子句須倒裝；此句敘述過去的事（二十世紀時），且其後動詞 piece 為原形，應用助動詞 did，即 Only in the twentieth century did scientists piece together…「直到二十世紀，科學家才拼湊出…」。have 與 had 後面須接過去分詞 pieced；do 為現在式，與過去的時間不合。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["vulnerable", "immune", "indifferent", "resistant"], "answer": 0, "explain": "be vulnerable to sth 意為「易受…傷害的、脆弱的」；由 sobering reminder「發人深省的提醒」及前文天候異常造成飢荒與暴動可知，此處強調人類社會面對氣候驟變時的脆弱。immune to 對…免疫；resistant to 能抵抗…的，二者與文意相反；indifferent to 對…漠不關心，亦不合語意。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C63", "title": "Clever Hans: The Horse That Taught Scientists a Lesson", "title_zh": "聰明的漢斯：給科學家上了一課的馬", "level": "C1", "topic": "scientific method", "passage": "In the early 1900s, a horse named Hans became one of the most famous animals in Germany. His owner, Wilhelm von Osten, a former mathematics teacher, claimed that he had taught Hans to add, subtract, multiply and even work with fractions. Asked a question, the horse would tap out the answer with his hoof, and he was right far more often than chance could explain. Huge crowds came to watch, and \"Clever Hans\" quickly became a national __1__.\n\nNot surprisingly, many people suspected a trick. In 1904, a commission that included a psychologist, a veterinarian and a circus manager was appointed to investigate. __2__ skeptical some of its members may have been at first, the commission found no evidence of deliberate deception. Von Osten, it seemed, was no fraud; he never even charged people to see his horse.\n\nThe mystery was finally solved by Oskar Pfungst, a young psychologist who examined the horse under carefully controlled conditions. He discovered that Hans answered correctly only when the questioner knew the answer and was visible to him. When Hans wore blinders, or when the person asking did not know the answer, his accuracy __3__. Pfungst concluded that the questioners were unconsciously signaling the answer. As the horse tapped, their posture and facial expressions changed in tiny ways, with tension building as the count approached the correct number and easing the moment it was reached. Hans had not learned arithmetic at all; __4__ he had learned was to read people with extraordinary precision.\n\nStrikingly, even Pfungst, who understood exactly how the cues worked, found it difficult to stop giving them himself. This is why the \"Clever Hans effect\" is still taught to researchers today: an experimenter who knows the expected result may __5__ influence the outcome. To prevent this, many studies are designed so that the people who interact with the subjects do not know what result is expected. The term is even used in artificial intelligence to describe computer systems that appear to solve a problem but are in fact relying on irrelevant clues hidden in their data.", "passage_zh": "二十世紀初，一匹名叫漢斯的馬成了德國最出名的動物之一。牠的主人威廉．馮．奧斯滕曾是一位數學教師，他聲稱自己教會了漢斯加法、減法、乘法，甚至還會計算分數。只要有人提問，這匹馬就會用蹄子敲出答案，而且答對的次數遠非碰運氣所能解釋。大批群眾前來觀看，「聰明的漢斯」很快就轟動全國。\n\n不出所料，許多人懷疑其中有詐。1904 年，一個成員包括一位心理學家、一位獸醫和一位馬戲團經理的委員會奉命展開調查。無論部分成員起初抱持多大的懷疑，委員會都找不到任何蓄意欺騙的證據。看來，馮．奧斯滕並不是騙子；他甚至從來不向觀眾收取觀賞費用。\n\n這個謎團最後由年輕的心理學家奧斯卡．芬斯特解開，他在嚴格控制的條件下對這匹馬進行了檢驗。他發現，只有在提問者知道答案、而且漢斯看得到提問者的時候，漢斯才會答對。當漢斯戴上眼罩，或提問的人自己也不知道答案時，牠的正確率便一落千丈。芬斯特的結論是：提問者在不知不覺中向馬透露了答案。在漢斯敲蹄子的過程中，他們的姿勢和臉部表情會產生細微的變化：隨著敲擊次數接近正確數字，緊張感逐漸升高，而一敲到正確數字，緊張感便立刻消退。漢斯根本沒有學會算術；牠學會的，是以驚人的精準度察言觀色。\n\n令人訝異的是，即使是完全明白這些暗示如何運作的芬斯特本人，也發現自己很難不發出這些暗示。這正是「聰明漢斯效應」至今仍是研究人員必學一課的原因：知道預期結果的實驗者，可能會在不知不覺中影響實驗的結果。為了避免這種情形，許多研究在設計上會讓與受試者互動的人員不知道預期的結果是什麼。這個詞甚至也被用在人工智慧領域，用來形容某些電腦系統看似解決了問題，實際上卻是依賴資料中隱藏的無關線索。", "blanks": [{"options": ["sentiment", "sensitivity", "sensation", "sensibility"], "answer": 2, "explain": "a sensation 指「轟動一時的人或事」，a national sensation 即「轟動全國的焦點」，符合大批群眾前來觀看的情境。sentiment 情感、看法；sensitivity 敏感度；sensibility 感受力、（藝術上的）鑑賞力，四個選項字形相近，但只有 sensation 能表示「轟動」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["However", "Although", "Whatever", "Despite"], "answer": 0, "explain": "However + 形容詞 + 主詞 + 動詞 為讓步句型，意為「無論多麼…」，However skeptical some of its members may have been 即「無論部分成員起初抱持多大的懷疑」。Although 引導子句時須用一般語序（Although some of its members may have been skeptical），不能把形容詞提前；Whatever 須修飾名詞或作代名詞，不能直接接形容詞；Despite 為介系詞，其後須接名詞或動名詞，不能接子句。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["soared", "plummeted", "stabilized", "flourished"], "answer": 1, "explain": "漢斯看不見提問者或提問者不知道答案時，牠就無法接收暗示，正確率自然「驟降」，應用 plummeted（plummet 暴跌、驟降）。soar 飆升；stabilize 穩定下來；flourish 蓬勃發展，皆與上文「只有在看得到知道答案的提問者時才答對」的發現相矛盾。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["that", "which", "where", "what"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格引導名詞子句作主詞，且子句 he had learned 缺少受詞，應用複合關係代名詞 what（＝ the thing that），形成 What he had learned was to… 的強調句型（wh- 分裂句），意為「牠學會的是…」。that 引導名詞子句時，子句本身須完整，不缺主詞或受詞；which 引導形容詞子句時前面須有先行詞；where 表地點，不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["deliberately", "unanimously", "unwittingly", "reluctantly"], "answer": 2, "explain": "本文重點在於提問者是「不自覺地」發出暗示，連明白原理的芬斯特本人都很難克制，因此知道預期結果的實驗者可能會「在不知不覺中」影響結果，應用 unwittingly「無意地、不知不覺地」。deliberately 故意地，與文意相反；unanimously 全體一致地；reluctantly 不情願地，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C64", "title": "The Psychologist Who Memorized Nonsense", "title_zh": "背誦無意義音節的心理學家", "level": "C1", "topic": "memory and learning", "passage": "In the late 1870s, the German psychologist Hermann Ebbinghaus set out to study something that many scholars of his day believed could not be measured at all: memory. Instead of recruiting volunteers, he used himself as his only subject, learning list after list of meaningless syllables such as \"WID\" and \"ZOF\" and recording exactly how long each list took to master. Real words, he reasoned, carry meanings and associations that make some far easier to remember than others. Nonsense syllables, __1__, offered something close to a blank slate.\n\nThe results, published in 1885, revealed a pattern now known as the forgetting curve. Memory did not fade at a steady rate. Instead, the steepest decline came within the first hours after learning, after which the curve gradually __2__ off. Ebbinghaus also made an encouraging discovery: even when he could no longer recall a list at all, relearning it took him less time than it had the first time, a sign that some trace of the original learning had survived.\n\nHis second major finding is perhaps even more useful to students. Ebbinghaus found that spreading his repetitions over several days was far more effective than packing them into a single session: fewer repetitions in total were needed to achieve the same result. This \"spacing effect\" has since been __3__ in hundreds of experiments with learners of all ages, and it is now regarded as one of the most reliable findings in the psychology of learning. Many flashcard apps are built around it, scheduling each card to reappear shortly before it is likely to be forgotten.\n\nNone of this means that cramming is useless. A night of intensive study can help you hold on to enough information to get through the next morning's test. Rarely, however, __4__ crammed material survive for long, which becomes a serious problem when each stage of a course builds on the last. Students preparing for a major exam would therefore be well __5__ to review their notes little and often, rather than leaving everything until the final week.", "passage_zh": "1870 年代末期，德國心理學家艾賓浩斯著手研究一樣當時許多學者認為根本無法測量的東西：記憶。他沒有招募志願者，而是以自己作為唯一的受試者，背誦一份又一份由無意義音節（例如「WID」和「ZOF」）組成的清單，並精確記錄每份清單要花多少時間才能背熟。他的理由是，真實的字詞帶有意義與聯想，會使某些字比其他字容易記得多；相較之下，無意義音節則提供了近乎一張白紙的條件。\n\n研究結果於 1885 年發表，揭示了一種如今被稱為「遺忘曲線」的模式。記憶並不是以穩定的速度消退；相反地，遺忘最劇烈的階段出現在學習後的最初幾個小時，之後曲線便逐漸趨於平緩。艾賓浩斯還有一項令人振奮的發現：即使他已經完全想不起某份清單，重新學習它所花的時間仍比第一次少，這表示最初學習的成果仍有某些痕跡留存了下來。\n\n他的第二項重要發現對學生而言或許更加實用。艾賓浩斯發現，把重複練習分散在好幾天進行，遠比全部擠在一次學習中有效：要達到同樣的效果，所需的總練習次數比較少。這種「間隔效應」此後已在數以百計、以各年齡層學習者為對象的實驗中一再獲得驗證，如今被視為學習心理學中最可靠的研究發現之一。許多單字卡應用程式就是根據這項原理設計的，會安排每張卡片在使用者可能快要忘記之前再次出現。\n\n這並不表示臨時抱佛腳毫無用處。熬夜苦讀一晚，或許能讓你記住足夠的內容來應付隔天早上的考試。然而，臨時硬塞進腦中的內容很少能保留太久；當課程的每個階段都建立在前一個階段之上時，這就成了一個嚴重的問題。因此，準備重大考試的學生最好少量多次地複習筆記，而不是把所有內容都留到最後一週才念。", "blanks": [{"options": ["as a result", "in other words", "for instance", "by contrast"], "answer": 3, "explain": "前句說真實的字詞帶有意義與聯想，記憶的難易程度不一；本句說無意義音節則近乎一張白紙，兩者形成對比，應用 by contrast「相較之下」。as a result 因此（表結果）；in other words 換句話說（表重述）；for instance 例如（表舉例），皆不合前後文的對比關係。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["called", "leveled", "set", "showed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "level off 為片語動詞，意為「（上升或下降的趨勢）趨於平緩、穩定下來」，符合遺忘曲線在學習後最初幾小時下降最劇烈、之後逐漸趨於平緩的描述。call off 取消；set off 出發、引發；show off 炫耀，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["refuted", "overlooked", "replicated", "fabricated"], "answer": 2, "explain": "replicate 為學術用語，指「（以相同方法）重做實驗並得到相同結果」；由下文說間隔效應如今被視為學習心理學中最可靠的發現之一，可知它已在數百個實驗中一再獲得驗證。refute 駁斥、證明…有誤；overlook 忽略；fabricate 捏造，皆與「最可靠的發現」互相矛盾。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["does", "do", "is", "has"], "answer": 0, "explain": "否定副詞 Rarely 置於句首時，主詞與助動詞須倒裝；本句原為 crammed material rarely survives for long，倒裝時須補上助動詞，成為 Rarely does crammed material survive。material 在此為不可數名詞，助動詞應用單數的 does，其後接原形動詞 survive。do 與單數主詞不一致；is 與 has 後面不能接原形動詞 survive。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["informed", "suggested", "aware", "advised"], "answer": 3, "explain": "be well advised to + 原形動詞 為固定用語，意為「最好…；…是明智的」，常用來提出建議，符合「準備大考的學生最好少量多次地複習」的語意。be well informed 意為「消息靈通的」，不接不定詞表示建議；suggest 不能用 sb be suggested to do sth 的結構；be well aware 須接 of 或 that 子句，皆不合文法與語意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C65", "title": "Preserved Before It Was Understood: The Birth of Canned Food", "title_zh": "先會保存，後懂原理：罐頭食品的誕生", "level": "C1", "topic": "food history", "passage": "For most of human history, keeping food from spoiling was a constant struggle. Salting, smoking and drying could make meat and fish last for months, but these methods changed the taste and texture of the food and offered little variety. The problem was especially __1__ for armies and navies, which had to feed huge numbers of men far from home. Sailors on long voyages, deprived of fresh fruit and vegetables, often fell victim to scurvy, a disease caused by a lack of vitamin C.\n\nThe breakthrough came from an unlikely figure: Nicolas Appert, a Parisian confectioner. After years of patient experiment, he found that food sealed tightly in glass bottles and then heated in boiling water would stay edible for months. In 1810, the French government, whose armies were then fighting across Europe, awarded him 12,000 francs __2__ he publish the details of his method. He duly did so, releasing a book that explained how to preserve everything from vegetables to meat.\n\nRemarkably, Appert had no idea why his method worked. He believed that the secret lay in keeping out air. It was not until the 1860s __3__ Louis Pasteur showed that food spoils because of microorganisms, which heating destroys and a tight seal keeps out. Appert's success, in other words, was a triumph of careful trial and error rather than of scientific theory.\n\nMeanwhile, in Britain, inventors adapted the idea to containers made of tin-plated iron, which were far sturdier than glass bottles. By the mid-1810s, canned food was being supplied to the Royal Navy. Curiously, early cans were so thick and heavy that they had to be opened with a hammer and chisel, and a __4__ can opener did not appear until the 1850s, nearly half a century after the can itself. Appert's story is a reminder that a practical solution can __5__ its scientific explanation by decades, and that inventors do not always need to understand a process fully in order to put it to good use.", "passage_zh": "在人類歷史的大部分時期，防止食物腐壞一直是一場永無止境的苦戰。鹽醃、煙燻和乾燥雖然能讓肉類和魚類保存好幾個月，但這些方法會改變食物的味道與口感，選擇也相當有限。這個問題對陸軍和海軍來說尤其嚴重，因為他們必須在遠離家鄉的地方供應大批人員的伙食。長途航行的水手吃不到新鮮蔬果，常常罹患壞血病，這是一種因缺乏維生素 C 而引起的疾病。\n\n突破來自一位出人意料的人物：巴黎的糕點師傅尼古拉．阿佩爾。經過多年耐心的實驗，他發現把食物緊密封在玻璃瓶中，再放進滾水裡加熱，食物就能保持可食用的狀態長達數月。1810 年，當時正派兵在歐洲各地征戰的法國政府頒給他一萬二千法郎，條件是他必須公開這項方法的細節。他也依約照辦，出版了一本書，說明從蔬菜到肉類等各種食物的保存方法。\n\n令人驚訝的是，阿佩爾完全不知道他的方法為什麼有效。他以為祕訣在於隔絕空氣。直到 1860 年代，巴斯德才證明食物腐壞是微生物造成的，而加熱能消滅微生物，密封則能防止它們進入。換句話說，阿佩爾的成功是細心反覆試驗的勝利，而不是科學理論的勝利。\n\n與此同時，英國的發明家把這個構想應用在鍍錫鐵製成的容器上，這種容器比玻璃瓶堅固得多。到了 1810 年代中期，罐頭食品已開始供應給英國皇家海軍。奇妙的是，早期的罐頭又厚又重，必須用鐵鎚和鑿子才能打開；而專用的開罐器直到 1850 年代才出現，比罐頭本身晚了將近半個世紀。阿佩爾的故事提醒我們，實用的解決辦法可能比它的科學解釋早出現數十年，而發明者也未必要完全理解一個過程，才能善加利用它。", "blanks": [{"options": ["acute", "marginal", "redundant", "dormant"], "answer": 0, "explain": "an acute problem 為常見搭配，意為「嚴重而急迫的問題」；由 especially 及下文軍隊必須在遠方供應大批人員伙食、水手常罹患壞血病可知，此問題對軍隊而言尤其嚴重。marginal 次要的、不重要的；redundant 多餘的；dormant 潛伏的、暫時不活動的，皆與「尤其嚴重」的語意不合。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["in case", "for fear that", "on condition that", "even though"], "answer": 2, "explain": "on condition that 意為「以…為條件」：政府頒發獎金給阿佩爾，條件是他必須公開方法的細節，下一句說他依約照辦（duly did so），正可印證此義。其後子句用原形動詞 publish，為表示要求或條件的假設語氣（省略 should）。in case 以防萬一；for fear that 唯恐，表示政府不希望他公開，與文意相反；even though 雖然，表讓步，皆不合語意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["when", "which", "since", "that"], "answer": 3, "explain": "It was not until + 時間 + that + 子句 為固定的強調句型，意為「直到…才…」，被強調的 not until the 1860s 置於 It was 與 that 之間，此句型須以 that 連接。when 雖可表時間，但不用於此強調句型；which 引導形容詞子句，前面須有名詞作先行詞；since 意為「自從」，語意與句型皆不合。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["devoted", "dedicated", "committed", "addicted"], "answer": 1, "explain": "dedicated 用來形容器材、設備時，意為「專用的、專供某種用途的」，a dedicated can opener 即「專用的開罐器」，與前文只能用鐵鎚和鑿子開罐形成對比。devoted 與 committed 指人「忠誠的、投入的」；addicted 指人「上癮的」，三者皆用於形容人，不能用來修飾開罐器這類工具。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["succeed", "follow", "precede", "replace"], "answer": 2, "explain": "precede 意為「發生在…之前」，precede A by decades 即「比 A 早數十年出現」；阿佩爾的保存方法在 1810 年就已公開，巴斯德的科學解釋卻要到 1860 年代才出現，可知實用的方法「先於」科學解釋。succeed 與 follow 皆為「接在…之後」，先後順序顛倒；replace 取代，不合語意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C66", "title": "Unwelcome Evidence: Ignaz Semmelweis and the Mystery of Childbed Fever", "title_zh": "不受歡迎的證據：塞麥爾維斯與產褥熱之謎", "level": "C2", "topic": "history of medicine", "passage": "In the 1840s, the maternity wards of Vienna General Hospital posed a grim puzzle. The hospital ran two clinics side by side: in the first, women were attended by doctors and medical students; in the second, by midwives and their pupils. Yet deaths from childbed fever, an infection that could kill a new mother within days of giving birth, were consistently far higher in the first clinic than in the second. The difference was no secret; some women pleaded to be sent to the second clinic. A young Hungarian assistant named Ignaz Semmelweis noticed something stranger still: women who had given birth on their way to the hospital fared better than those delivered in the first clinic.\n\nThe decisive clue came in 1847, through a tragedy. A senior colleague was accidentally cut by a student's scalpel during an autopsy and died of an illness whose symptoms closely resembled those of the dying mothers. Semmelweis drew a bold conclusion. Doctors and students routinely went straight from dissecting corpses to examining women in labor, and he reasoned that they were carrying some deadly \"cadaverous\" material on their hands; the midwives, who performed no autopsies, were not. He ordered everyone in the first clinic to wash their hands in a solution of chlorinated lime before examining patients, and within weeks the death rate __1__ to a fraction of its former level.\n\n__2__ compelling the figures were, they carried an implication that many physicians found intolerable: that they themselves had been carrying death from bed to bed. His explanation also sat uneasily with the prevailing view that disease arose from bad air or from imbalances within the body. Nor did Semmelweis help his own cause. For years he published little, and after finally setting out his findings in a book in 1861, he followed it with furious open letters denouncing some of his critics as murderers. His warnings largely fell on __3__ ears. Having returned to his native Hungary, he grew increasingly erratic, and in 1865 he was committed to an asylum, where he died within weeks, aged just 47.\n\nOnly in the following decades, as the work of Louis Pasteur and Joseph Lister established that microorganisms cause infection, were his ideas fully __4__. Today the tendency to reject new evidence because it contradicts established beliefs is sometimes called the \"Semmelweis reflex,\" a dubious honor for a man who, __5__ he been a more patient advocate, might have persuaded far more of his colleagues in his own lifetime.", "passage_zh": "1840 年代，維也納總醫院的產科病房出現了一個令人憂心的謎團。這家醫院同時設有兩間產科診所：第一診所由醫師和醫學生照顧產婦，第二診所則由助產士及其學徒負責。然而，產褥熱（一種可能在產婦分娩後數日內便奪走其性命的感染）在第一診所造成的死亡人數，始終遠高於第二診所。這種差異並非祕密，有些婦女甚至苦苦哀求改送到第二診所。一位名叫伊格納茲．塞麥爾維斯的年輕匈牙利籍助理醫師還注意到一件更奇怪的事：在前往醫院途中就已生產的婦女，情況反而比在第一診所分娩的婦女來得好。\n\n關鍵的線索在 1847 年隨著一場悲劇出現。一位資深同事在進行屍體解剖時，不慎被學生的手術刀割傷，隨後死於一種病症，其症狀與那些垂死產婦的症狀極為相似。塞麥爾維斯由此得出一個大膽的結論。醫師和醫學生經常剛解剖完屍體，就直接去檢查待產的婦女；他推斷，他們手上帶著某種致命的「屍體物質」，而不做屍體解剖的助產士則沒有。他下令第一診所的所有人在檢查病患之前，都必須用含氯石灰（漂白粉）溶液洗手；短短幾週之內，死亡率便驟降到原本的一小部分。\n\n無論這些數據多麼有說服力，它們都隱含著一個許多醫師無法忍受的意涵：正是他們自己把死亡從一張病床帶到另一張病床。他的解釋也和當時盛行的觀點格格不入；當時的人普遍認為，疾病源自汙濁的空氣，或是體內的失調。塞麥爾維斯本身也無助於自己的主張被接受。多年來，他幾乎沒有發表什麼論著；而在 1861 年終於出書闡述自己的發現之後，他又接連發表措辭激烈的公開信，痛斥某些批評者是殺人兇手。他的警告大多被當成耳邊風。返回故鄉匈牙利之後，他的言行日益反常；1865 年，他被送進精神病院，幾週之內便死在那裡，得年僅四十七歲。\n\n直到之後的數十年間，隨著路易．巴斯德和約瑟夫．李斯特的研究證實微生物會引發感染，他的觀點才完全獲得平反。如今，人們只因新證據與既有信念相牴觸就加以排斥的傾向，有時被稱為「塞麥爾維斯反射」。對他而言，這個「榮譽」實在頗為諷刺：倘若他當年是個更有耐心的倡導者，或許在有生之年就能說服更多的同事。", "blanks": [{"options": ["abated", "plummeted", "subsided", "receded"], "answer": 1, "explain": "空格描述洗手措施實施後死亡率的變化，句中有 within weeks「短短幾週之內」與 to a fraction of its former level「降到原本的一小部分」兩個線索，可知是急遽而大幅的下降，應用 plummet「驟降、暴跌」。abate 與 subside 多指（風暴、洪水、疼痛、怒氣等）「減弱、平息」，recede 指（水、記憶等）「退去、逐漸遠離」，三者都不用來描述比率在短時間內「急降到某個程度」。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["Although", "Whatever", "Despite", "However"], "answer": 3, "explain": "空格後為「形容詞 compelling＋主詞＋動詞」的語序，應用 However＋形容詞＋主詞＋動詞，表「無論多麼…」的讓步，意即數據再怎麼有說服力，醫師們仍難以接受其中的意涵。Although 為連接詞，後接正常語序的子句（Although the figures were compelling），不能把形容詞移到句首；Whatever 須修飾名詞或作代名詞，不能直接修飾形容詞；Despite 為介系詞，後接名詞或動名詞，不能接子句。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["deaf", "blind", "closed", "dull"], "answer": 0, "explain": "fall on deaf ears 為固定慣用語，意為「（勸告、警告）不被理會、被當成耳邊風」，符合前文醫師們難以接受其主張、他本人又四處樹敵的情境。blind 常見於 turn a blind eye「視而不見」，不與 ears 搭配；closed 與 dull 也都無法構成此慣用語。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["exonerated", "acquitted", "vindicated", "absolved"], "answer": 2, "explain": "vindicate 指「證明（某人的主張、看法）是正確的、使獲得平反」，受詞可以是想法或主張；此處 his ideas 在巴斯德與李斯特證實微生物致病之後，終於被證明是對的。exonerate、acquit、absolve 雖然都有「洗刷、開脫」之意，但對象是被指控或被責怪的「人」，分別指「免除罪責」、「（法院）宣判無罪」、「赦免、解除責任」，不用來表示「想法被證明正確」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["had", "were", "should", "if"], "answer": 0, "explain": "此處為省略 if 的「與過去事實相反」假設句：had he been… ＝ if he had been…，主要子句相應地使用 might have persuaded，表示「倘若他當年更有耐心，或許就能說服更多同事」。were he 用於與現在事實相反的假設，後面也不能再接 been；should he 後須接原形動詞 be；if he been 缺少助動詞 had，文法錯誤。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C67", "title": "No Tragedy Required: Elinor Ostrom and the Commons", "title_zh": "悲劇並非必然：歐斯壯與公共資源", "level": "C2", "topic": "economics and shared resources", "passage": "In 1968 the ecologist Garrett Hardin published a short essay that would shape thinking about the environment for decades. He asked his readers to imagine a pasture open to all. Each herder gains the full benefit of adding one more animal to the herd, while the cost of overgrazing is shared by everyone. Acting rationally, each herder keeps adding animals until the pasture is ruined. Hardin called this \"the tragedy of the commons,\" and to many readers his argument seemed __1__. For years it was routinely invoked to support one of two remedies: dividing shared resources into private property, or placing them under the control of the state.\n\nThe political scientist Elinor Ostrom was not convinced. Instead of reasoning from an imaginary pasture, she and her colleagues studied how real communities had managed fisheries, forests, irrigation systems and grazing land, sometimes for centuries. What they found was __2__ Hardin's bleak forecast. In the Swiss village of Törbel, for instance, farmers had grazed their cattle on shared alpine meadows for hundreds of years under a rule that no family could send more cows to the summer pastures than it could feed through the winter. In eastern Spain, farmers had shared scarce irrigation water through local institutions, including courts of their own, that had endured for centuries.\n\nA crucial difference, Ostrom argued, lay in communication. Hardin's herders never speak to one another; real users of a commons almost always __3__. Communities that succeeded tended to share certain features: clear boundaries defining who might use a resource, rules suited to local conditions, monitoring by people answerable to the users themselves, and penalties that began mildly and grew heavier for repeat offenders. __4__ she believed every commons could be saved. She documented many that had collapsed, and she warned repeatedly against the search for panaceas, insisting that no single set of rules would work everywhere.\n\nIn 2009 Ostrom became the first woman to be __5__ the Nobel Memorial Prize in Economic Sciences. By then, Hardin himself had acknowledged that he should have written of an \"unmanaged\" commons. The lesson is not that shared resources are safe, but that their fate may depend less on who owns them than on whether the people who use them can make, monitor and enforce rules of their own.", "passage_zh": "1968 年，生態學家蓋瑞特．哈汀發表了一篇短文，在此後數十年間深深影響了人們對環境問題的思考。他請讀者想像一片對所有人開放的牧場。每個牧人多放一頭牲畜，就能獨享這頭牲畜帶來的全部好處，而過度放牧的代價卻由所有人共同承擔。於是，每個牧人在理性考量之下都會不斷增加牲畜，直到牧場被毀為止。哈汀把這種情況稱為「公地悲劇」，而對許多讀者來說，他的論點似乎無懈可擊。多年來，這個論點經常被援引來支持兩種解決方法之一：把共有資源劃分為私有財產，或是交由國家控管。\n\n政治學家伊莉諾．歐斯壯卻不以為然。她和同事們並不從一片假想的牧場出發進行推論，而是去研究真實的社群如何管理漁場、森林、灌溉系統和放牧地，有些社群甚至已經管理了好幾個世紀。他們的發現與哈汀悲觀的預測大相逕庭。舉例來說，在瑞士的特貝爾村，農民數百年來一直在共有的高山草地上放牧牛群，並遵守一項規定：任何家庭送上夏季牧場的牛隻，不得多於自家在冬天養得起的數量。在西班牙東部，農民則透過在地的組織（包括他們自己的法庭）分配稀少的灌溉用水，而這些組織已延續了好幾個世紀。\n\n歐斯壯認為，關鍵差異之一在於溝通。哈汀筆下的牧人彼此從不交談，但現實中公共資源的使用者幾乎總是會互相溝通。成功的社群往往具備某些共同特徵：有明確的界線規定誰可以使用某項資源、有符合當地條件的規則、由對使用者本身負責的人進行監督，以及一開始從輕處罰、對屢犯者則逐步加重的罰則。這並不是說她認為每一處公共資源都能被拯救。她記錄了許多崩潰的案例，並一再提醒人們不要尋找萬靈丹，堅稱沒有任何一套規則能適用於所有地方。\n\n2009 年，歐斯壯成為第一位獲頒諾貝爾經濟學獎的女性。在那之前，哈汀本人也已承認，他當初應該把所談的對象寫成「無人管理的」公共資源。由此得到的啟示並不是共有資源就安全無虞，而是它們的命運與歸誰所有的關係，或許不如與使用者能否自行制定、監督並執行規則的關係來得大。", "blanks": [{"options": ["unaccountable", "unavailing", "unassailable", "unabashed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "由下一句可知，哈汀的論點多年來常被援引以支持特定政策，顯示許多讀者認為它「無懈可擊、難以反駁」，應用 unassailable「（論點、地位）無法攻破的」。unaccountable 指「無法解釋的」或「不必負責的」；unavailing 指「徒勞無功的」；unabashed 指「毫不害臊的、不加掩飾的」，皆不能用來表示論點令人信服。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["a long shot at", "a far cry from", "a close shave with", "a wide berth to"], "answer": 1, "explain": "a far cry from 為慣用語，意為「與…相去甚遠、截然不同」；後文舉出瑞士與西班牙的社群數百年來成功共管資源的例子，正好與哈汀悲觀的預測相反。a long shot 指「希望渺茫的嘗試」；a close shave 指「千鈞一髮、險些出事」；wide berth 須用於 give…a wide berth「避開、敬而遠之」，三者皆不合句意與搭配。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["are", "have", "did", "do"], "answer": 3, "explain": "此處為以助動詞代替前文動詞片語的省略用法：almost always do ＝ almost always speak to one another。前半句 never speak 為現在簡單式，後半句須以 do 承接。are 與 have 無法代替一般動詞 speak；did 為過去式，與前句的現在時態不一致，而本句陳述的是一般情況。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["Not that", "Not until", "Nor that", "Only that"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Not that＋子句意為「這並不是說…」，用來預先排除讀者可能產生的誤解：前文說成功的社群具有某些共同特徵，此處補充「並不是說她認為每一處公共資源都能被拯救」，接著舉出她記錄了許多失敗的案例。Not until 意為「直到…才」，後須接主要子句並倒裝；Nor that 與 Only that 都不是正確的句首結構，語意也不通。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["rewarded", "conferred", "awarded", "bestowed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "award 可接雙受詞（award sb a prize），改為被動語態即為 be awarded the prize「獲頒獎項」。reward 的用法是 reward sb with/for sth，指「獎賞、酬謝」，不直接接獎項名稱作受詞；confer 與 bestow 須以獎項或榮譽為受詞，再接 on/upon sb（confer/bestow a prize on sb），不能以人為主詞寫成 be conferred/bestowed the prize。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C68", "title": "Reading Between the Lines: A Reference That Said Too Little", "title_zh": "字裡行間：一封說得太少的推薦信", "level": "C2", "topic": "workplace communication", "passage": "Ruth had been hiring editors for a small publishing house for nearly twenty years, so when her new colleague Marcus described a reference letter as glowing, she asked to see it. The candidate was applying for a senior editing post, and the letter, from his former manager, was brief. It praised his punctuality, his tidy desk and his unfailingly cheerful manner with the reception staff. About his editing it said nothing at all.\n\n\"Glowing?\" said Ruth, handing it back. \"I'd hardly call it that. When someone is asked about an editor and talks only about his timekeeping, the silence speaks volumes.\"\n\n\"Aren't you reading rather a lot into it?\" Marcus asked. \"Nothing in it is actually negative.\"\n\n\"That's rather the point. Referees seldom criticize a candidate outright. Some are wary of being unfair; others worry about the consequences. So they __1__ the candidate with faint praise instead and leave the reader to do the rest. The philosopher Paul Grice built a famous example around exactly this. He imagined a professor whose student was applying for a post in philosophy, and who wrote only that the student's command of English was excellent and that he had attended tutorials regularly. Not a word of it is false, yet any reader will __2__ that the student is no philosopher. Grice called this kind of unstated meaning an implicature.\"\n\n\"So what should I do? Call the man and ask him whether the candidate can actually edit?\"\n\n\"Not in __3__ words,\" said Ruth. \"Ask him what he thinks the candidate still needs to work on, or whether he would hire him again. If he hesitates, or says the young man is still finding his feet, you'll have your answer. And if he says he'd rehire him tomorrow, I'll __4__ corrected.\"\n\nTwo days later Marcus poked his head around her door. \"I spoke to the referee,\" he said. \"I asked about the candidate's editing three times, and each time he told me how much everyone had enjoyed having him in the office.\" Then he added dryly, \"I take it that counts as an answer.\"\n\n\"It does,\" said Ruth. \"In that case, I'd rather we __5__ him a polite rejection now. His manager has told us everything we need to know. He simply hasn't said it.\"", "passage_zh": "露絲負責為一家小型出版社招募編輯已將近二十年，所以當她的新同事馬可斯形容一封推薦信「讚不絕口」時，她便要求看看那封信。應徵者申請的是資深編輯的職位，而這封由他前任主管所寫的信相當簡短。信中稱讚他守時、辦公桌整潔，而且對櫃檯人員總是笑臉迎人。至於他的編輯能力，信中則隻字未提。\n\n「讚不絕口？」露絲一邊把信遞回去一邊說，「我可不會這麼說。當有人被問到一位編輯的情況，卻只談他守不守時，這種沉默本身就說明了一切。」\n\n「妳是不是想太多了？」馬可斯問，「信裡沒有任何一句話是負面的。」\n\n「問題就在這裡。推薦人很少會直截了當地批評應徵者。有些人擔心有失公允，有些人則顧慮可能帶來的後果。所以他們改用不痛不癢的稱讚來暗中貶低應徵者，剩下的就交給讀信的人自己去體會。哲學家保羅．格萊斯正是以這種情況為核心，舉了一個著名的例子。他設想一位教授，其學生正在應徵一份哲學方面的職位，而教授在推薦信裡只寫了這名學生的英文能力極佳，而且一向按時出席個別指導課。信裡沒有一個字是假的，但任何讀者都會推斷出這名學生不是當哲學家的料。格萊斯把這種沒有明說的意思稱為『會話含意』。」\n\n「那我該怎麼做？打電話給那位主管，直接問他這名應徵者到底會不會編輯嗎？」\n\n「別問得那麼直白，」露絲說，「問他覺得這名應徵者還有哪些地方需要加強，或是問他願不願意再僱用他。如果他猶豫了，或是說這個年輕人還在摸索階段，你就知道答案了。要是他說明天就願意再僱用他，那我就承認自己錯了。」\n\n兩天後，馬可斯從她辦公室門口探頭進來。「我跟那位推薦人談過了，」他說，「我問了三次這名應徵者的編輯能力，他每次都告訴我，大家有多喜歡跟他一起在辦公室共事。」接著他又不動聲色地揶揄了一句：「我想這就算是答案了吧。」\n\n「沒錯，」露絲說，「既然如此，我寧願我們現在就客氣地回絕他。他的前主管已經把我們需要知道的一切都告訴我們了，只是沒有說出口而已。」", "blanks": [{"options": ["condemn", "curse", "blame", "damn"], "answer": 3, "explain": "damn sb with faint praise 為固定慣用語（源自英國詩人波普的詩句），指「以不痛不癢的稱讚來暗中貶低某人」，正符合推薦人不直接批評、只稱讚無關緊要之處的做法。condemn 雖有「譴責」之意，但不構成此慣用語；curse 指「咒罵」；blame 指「責怪」，皆無法與 with faint praise 搭配表達此意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["infer", "imply", "insinuate", "intimate"], "answer": 0, "explain": "infer 指「（聽者、讀者）從已知訊息推斷出」，主詞是接收訊息的一方，此處主詞為 any reader，表示讀者會「推斷出」該學生不擅長哲學。imply、insinuate、intimate 的主詞都是說話或寫作的一方，分別指「暗示」、「（不懷好意地）影射」、「委婉地透露」，不能用於讀者。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["as many", "too many", "so many", "so much"], "answer": 2, "explain": "not in so many words 為固定說法，意為「不明說、不直接說出來」；露絲的意思是不要直接問對方「他會不會編輯」，而要用間接的問題探出答案，與後文她建議的問法一致。as many、too many、so much 都無法構成此慣用說法。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["sit", "stand", "stay", "keep"], "answer": 1, "explain": "stand corrected 為固定說法，意為「承認自己說錯了、接受指正」，語氣較為正式；露絲表示，若推薦人說明天就願意再僱用他，她就承認自己判斷錯誤。sit、stay、keep 都不與 corrected 構成此用法。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["will send", "sending", "to send", "sent"], "answer": 3, "explain": "would rather＋主詞＋過去式動詞，表示希望某人（現在或未來）做某事，屬於假設語氣的用法，故用 sent，表示「我寧願我們現在就回絕他」。will send 不能用於 would rather 後的子句；sending 與 to send 都不是限定動詞，無法作子句的動詞。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C69", "title": "Accidents and Sagacity: The Story of Serendipity", "title_zh": "意外與睿智：「serendipity」一字的故事", "level": "C2", "topic": "language and discovery", "passage": "In January 1754 the English writer Horace Walpole, writing to a friend, invented a word. He had just made a lucky discovery connected with a portrait his friend had sent him, and it reminded him of a \"silly fairy tale\" called The Three Princes of Serendip. Its heroes, he explained, \"were always making discoveries, by accidents and sagacity, of things which they were not in quest of.\" Serendip was an old name for Sri Lanka, and from it Walpole made \"serendipity.\"\n\nFor almost two centuries the word __1__ in obscurity, known mainly to collectors and to readers of Walpole's letters. Only in the twentieth century did it begin to spread more widely, taken up notably by scientists, who found in it a name for a familiar experience, and later by the wider public. Today it turns up on everything from café signs to film titles.\n\nAs the word has grown more popular, however, its meaning has been steadily __2__. In everyday use it often means little more than a happy coincidence, such as bumping into an old friend in a foreign city. It would be a mistake, though, to think of Walpole's princes as merely lucky. __3__, their luck mattered less than their wits: they noticed what others overlooked and grasped its significance. Accident supplied the opportunity; sagacity made something of it.\n\nScientists have long appreciated the distinction. \"In the fields of observation,\" Louis Pasteur told an audience in 1854, \"chance favors only the prepared mind.\" A celebrated example is Wilhelm Röntgen's discovery of X-rays in 1895. While experimenting with a cathode-ray tube covered in black cardboard, he noticed that a fluorescent screen some distance away had begun to glow. Others may well have seen similar effects and __4__ them as a nuisance. Röntgen instead spent weeks investigating, and before the year was out he had produced an image of the bones in his wife's hand.\n\nSerendipity in Walpole's original sense, then, is less a matter of luck than of attention. It cannot be planned, but it can be encouraged: by staying curious, by taking anomalies seriously and by resisting the temptation to explain them __5__.", "passage_zh": "1754 年 1 月，英國作家霍勒斯．沃波爾在寫給朋友的一封信中創造了一個字。他剛在一件與朋友寄給他的肖像畫有關的事情上意外有所發現，這讓他想起一則名為《塞倫迪普三王子》的「無聊童話」。他解釋說，故事中的主角們「總是憑著意外與睿智，發現他們原本並未尋找的事物」。塞倫迪普（Serendip）是斯里蘭卡的舊稱，沃波爾便由此造出了「serendipity」一字。\n\n在將近兩個世紀的時間裡，這個字一直默默無聞，主要只有收藏家和沃波爾書信的讀者知道。直到二十世紀，它才開始更廣泛地流傳，尤其受到科學家青睞，因為他們發現這個字正好能為一種熟悉的經驗命名；之後，一般大眾也開始使用它。如今，從咖啡館的招牌到電影片名，到處都看得到它的蹤影。\n\n然而，隨著這個字越來越流行，它的意義也不斷被沖淡。在日常用法中，它往往只是指一場令人開心的巧合，例如在異國城市巧遇老朋友。不過，若把沃波爾筆下的王子們看成只是運氣好，那就錯了。甚至可以說，運氣對他們而言還不如機智重要：他們注意到別人忽略的事物，並領悟其中的意義。意外提供了機會，而睿智則讓機會有所成果。\n\n科學家早就體認到這個區別。路易．巴斯德在 1854 年對聽眾說：「在觀察的領域中，機會只青睞有準備的心靈。」一個著名的例子是威廉．倫琴在 1895 年發現 X 光。他在用一根包覆著黑色厚紙板的陰極射線管做實驗時，注意到一段距離外的一塊螢光屏開始發光。其他人或許也曾看過類似的現象，卻把它當成惱人的干擾而不予理會；倫琴則花了好幾週深入研究，並在那一年結束之前，拍下了他妻子手骨的影像。\n\n由此可見，按照沃波爾原本的意思，serendipity 與其說是運氣問題，不如說是專注力的問題。它無法事先計畫，卻可以加以促成：保持好奇心、認真看待反常現象，並抗拒用各種說法把它們搪塞過去的誘惑。", "blanks": [{"options": ["lapsed", "languished", "loitered", "lounged"], "answer": 1, "explain": "languish in obscurity 為常見搭配，指「長期默默無聞、被埋沒」，且可與 for almost two centuries 這種表示持續的時間連用，符合這個字長期只有少數人知道的情況。lapse 須用 lapse into obscurity「逐漸被遺忘」，表示狀態的轉變，不與 in 及一段持續時間連用；loiter 指「閒晃、逗留」；lounge 指「懶洋洋地倚靠或休息」，皆不合語意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["distilled", "dispersed", "diluted", "dissolved"], "answer": 2, "explain": "下一句說這個字在日常使用中往往只剩「巧合」之意，可知其原本的意涵仍在，只是被「沖淡、削弱」了，應用 dilute「稀釋；使（意義、效果）減弱」。distill 指「蒸餾、提煉精華」，意思正好相反；disperse 指「（人群、光線等）分散、驅散」；dissolve 指「溶解；（組織、婚姻）解散」，用於比喻時表示「消失、瓦解」，與「意義變淡但仍存在」不符。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["If anything", "Even so", "All the same", "By the same token"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前句說不應把王子們看成只是運氣好，空格後進一步提出更強烈的說法：運氣對他們而言反而不如機智重要。If anything 正是用來引出這種「甚至可以說恰恰相反」的修正說法。Even so 與 All the same 都表「儘管如此」，用於讓步，與前句的邏輯不合；By the same token 表「同理、基於同樣的理由」，前後並無類比關係。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["dispelled", "dissuaded", "disowned", "dismissed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "dismiss sth as… 為常見搭配，指「把…當作…而不予理會」；此處說其他人可能看過類似現象，卻把它當成惱人的干擾而不予理會，與倫琴深入研究形成對比。dispel 指「驅散（疑慮、恐懼、迷思）」；dissuade 用於 dissuade sb from doing sth「勸阻某人做某事」；disown 指「與…斷絕關係、不承認是自己的」，皆不能與 as a nuisance 搭配表達此意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["off", "away", "out", "over"], "answer": 1, "explain": "explain sth away 為片語動詞，指「為（反常或不利的事）找理由搪塞過去、自圓其說而不予重視」，與前面 taking anomalies seriously「認真看待反常現象」形成對照。explain 不與 off、out、over 構成具有此意的片語動詞。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C70", "title": "Golden Seams: The Art of Kintsugi", "title_zh": "金色的接縫：金繼的藝術", "level": "C2", "topic": "art and craft", "passage": "When a treasured bowl breaks, most of us either throw it away or glue it back together and hope the cracks will not show. The Japanese craft of kintsugi, or \"golden joinery,\" takes the opposite approach. __1__ hiding the damage, the repairer joins the pieces with lacquer and then dusts the seams with powdered gold, so that the cracks are not concealed but __2__. The mended bowl carries a visible record of its own history, and admirers often consider it more beautiful, not less, than it was before.\n\nA story often told about the craft's origins concerns Ashikaga Yoshimasa, a fifteenth-century shogun, who is said to have sent a damaged Chinese tea bowl back to China for repair. It returned held together with clumsy metal staples, and Japanese craftsmen, so the story goes, set out to find a more elegant solution. Historians treat the tale with caution, regarding it as quite possibly __3__, but it captures something real about the tea culture in which kintsugi flourished, with its fondness for objects that are rustic, irregular and marked by use. This taste is often linked to wabi-sabi, an aesthetic that finds beauty in imperfection and impermanence.\n\nTraditional kintsugi is anything but quick. The lacquer, made from the sap of the lacquer tree, can cause severe skin rashes before it hardens, and each layer must cure slowly in warm, humid conditions before the next can be applied. A single repair may take weeks or even months. In recent years, however, the craft has found an eager audience abroad, where it is often held up as a metaphor for resilience: the idea that people, too, can emerge from hardship stronger and more interesting than before. Many of the kits sold to meet this demand promise results in a single afternoon, replacing lacquer and real gold with quick-setting glue and gold-colored powder. __4__, then, that some traditional craftspeople regard such kits as a different thing altogether.\n\nThere is an irony here worth savoring. A craft that __5__ patience, and an acceptance that some things cannot be hurried, is now sold as a quick fix. Yet perhaps that is not wholly a bad thing. Anyone who tries even a simplified version learns at least part of the lesson: that a break need not be the end of an object's story, and that repair, done with care, can be a form of respect.", "passage_zh": "心愛的碗破了，我們大多數人不是把它丟掉，就是用膠水黏回去，並希望看不出裂痕。日本的金繼工藝（意為「金色的接合」）卻採取完全相反的做法。修復者不但不掩飾損傷，反而用漆把碎片接合起來，再在接縫上撒上金粉，讓裂痕非但沒有被隱藏，反而更加醒目。修補好的碗留下了記錄自身歷史的可見痕跡，而欣賞者往往認為它比破損前更美，而非更遜色。\n\n關於這項工藝的起源，有一個經常被傳述的故事與十五世紀的幕府將軍足利義政有關。據說他曾把一只破損的中國茶碗送回中國修理，結果送回來的茶碗卻是用粗糙的金屬鋦釘固定起來的；據傳，日本工匠因此開始尋找更優雅的修補方法。歷史學家對這個故事持保留態度，認為它很可能是杜撰的；但它確實反映了金繼得以興盛的茶道文化中某些真實的特質，也就是對質樸、不規則且帶有使用痕跡之器物的喜愛。這種品味常被連結到「侘寂」，一種在不完美與無常之中發現美的美學觀。\n\n傳統的金繼絕不是速成的工藝。用漆樹樹液製成的漆在乾硬之前可能會引起嚴重的皮膚紅疹，而且每一層漆都必須在溫暖潮濕的環境中慢慢固化，才能塗上下一層。單是一件修復作品，就可能要花上好幾週，甚至好幾個月。然而近年來，這項工藝在海外找到了熱情的愛好者；在那裡，它常被視為韌性的隱喻，象徵人也能在歷經磨難之後，變得比以前更堅強、更有內涵。許多為迎合這股需求而推出的套組，都標榜一個下午就能完成，並改用快乾膠和金色粉末來取代漆和真金。難怪有些傳統工匠認為，這類套組根本是另一回事。\n\n這其中的諷刺值得細細玩味。一項體現耐心，以及「有些事急不得」這種體認的工藝，如今竟被當作速成的解方來販售。不過，這或許也不完全是壞事。任何人即使只嘗試過簡化版，也至少能領會其中的部分道理：破損不一定是一件器物故事的終點，而用心的修補，也可以是一種尊重。", "blanks": [{"options": ["Rather than", "Other than", "Let alone", "Short of"], "answer": 0, "explain": "前文說金繼採取與一般人「相反的做法」，空格後說修復者不掩飾損傷，反而用金粉凸顯裂痕，應用 Rather than「不…而是…」，引出被捨棄的做法。Other than 意為「除了…之外」；Let alone 意為「更不用說」，須置於否定敘述之後；Short of 意為「除了（採取某種極端手段）之外、不至於」，皆不合語意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["attenuated", "extenuated", "adulterated", "accentuated"], "answer": 3, "explain": "not A but B 表示「不是 A 而是 B」，與 concealed「被隱藏」相對的應是「被凸顯、被強調」，且撒上金粉的接縫正是為了引人注目，故用 accentuate。attenuate 指「減弱、削弱」；extenuate 指「減輕（罪行的嚴重性）」，常見於 extenuating circumstances「可減輕罪責的情況」；adulterate 指「（摻入劣質成分）使不純」，皆不合語意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["apoplectic", "apologetic", "apocryphal", "anonymous"], "answer": 2, "explain": "前文說這是一個「經常被傳述」的故事，且歷史學家對它持保留態度，可知它「流傳甚廣但真實性可疑」，應用 apocryphal「（廣為流傳但）未必屬實的、杜撰的」。apoplectic 指「暴怒的；中風的」；apologetic 指「道歉的、愧疚的」；anonymous 指「匿名的、作者不詳的」，皆與「真偽存疑」無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["Little doubt", "Small wonder", "No matter", "Not least"], "answer": 1, "explain": "Small wonder (that)… 為固定說法，意為「難怪…、…不足為奇」；前文說速成套組以快乾膠和金色粉末取代漆與真金，因此傳統工匠認為兩者根本是不同的東西，也就不令人意外。Little doubt 須說成 There is little doubt that…，不能單獨置於句首；No matter that… 意為「…也無所謂」，與文意不合；Not least 意為「尤其是」，不能引導此結構。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["embezzles", "embroils", "embodies", "embellishes"], "answer": 2, "explain": "embody 指「體現、具體表現（某種特質或理念）」，此處說這項體現耐心、體認「有些事急不得」的工藝，如今卻被當成速成的解方來販售，正是作者所說的諷刺之處。embezzle 指「侵占、挪用（公款）」；embroil 指「使捲入（紛爭）」；embellish 指「裝飾；渲染、誇大」，皆無法與 patience 搭配表達此意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C71", "title": "A Flaw in the Sky: The Quiet Rescue of the Citicorp Center", "title_zh": "高空中的瑕疵：花旗中心的祕密搶救", "level": "C2", "topic": "engineering ethics", "passage": "When the Citicorp Center opened in Manhattan in 1977, it was hailed as an engineering triumph. Because a church occupied one corner of the site, the 59-story tower could not rest on corner columns in the usual way. Instead, the engineer William LeMessurier set it on four massive columns at the middle of each side, so that it seemed to hover above the street. An ingenious system of diagonal braces carried the load, and a huge sliding weight near the top, designed to __1__ the tower's sway in high winds, completed what many considered a masterpiece.\n\nIn 1978, prompted in part by questions from an engineering student, LeMessurier took a second look at his calculations. His design comfortably met the building code, which considered only winds striking a building head-on. He had not, however, checked what would happen if the wind struck at an angle, hitting two faces at once. When he did, he found the forces in some braces far higher. Worse, the braces' joints had been bolted rather than welded, a change his own office had approved to save money. In a severe enough storm, he realized, the tower might __2__ give way.\n\nLeMessurier now faced a choice few professionals ever have to make. He could keep quiet and hope the storm never came, or reveal a flaw that might ruin his reputation and invite enormous lawsuits. He chose to tell the building's owners, who, to his relief, responded not with recriminations but with a plan. Night after night, welders reinforced the joints with heavy steel plates, while the office workers arriving each morning were none the wiser. Officials quietly drew up plans to evacuate the surrounding blocks should a hurricane __3__.\n\nOne did. In early September, Hurricane Ella was churning northward off the Atlantic coast, and the repairs were only partly complete. Then, __4__, the storm turned away and headed out to sea. By October the work was done, and the tower was judged safe. Remarkably, the public learned almost nothing at the time; a newspaper strike then under way helped keep the story out of print.\n\nThe full story emerged only in 1995, when a magazine published a detailed account. Far from being __5__, LeMessurier saw his standing enhanced, and the episode is now a staple of engineering ethics courses. Its lesson is simple, if uncomfortable: what marks a true professional is not never making mistakes, but having the courage to own up to them before they cost lives.", "passage_zh": "1977 年，花旗中心在曼哈頓落成時，被譽為工程上的一大成就。由於基地的一角已有一座教堂，這棟 59 層的大樓無法像一般建築那樣立在四個角落的柱子上。於是，工程師威廉．勒梅蘇里爾改將大樓架在四根分別位於各邊中央的巨大柱子上，讓它彷彿懸浮在街道上方。一套巧妙的斜向支撐系統負責承載重量，而大樓頂部附近一個可以滑動的巨大重物，則用來抵銷強風造成的搖晃；這一切讓許多人將這棟大樓視為傑作。\n\n1978 年，勒梅蘇里爾部分是受到一位工程系學生提問的啟發，重新檢查了自己的計算。他的設計綽綽有餘地符合建築法規的要求，而法規只考量從正面吹向建築物的風。然而，他並沒有核算過，如果風從斜角吹來、同時吹襲大樓的兩個面，會發生什麼情況。等到他算了之後，發現部分支撐所承受的力要高出許多。更糟的是，這些支撐的接頭是用螺栓固定而非焊接，而這項為了節省成本的變更，正是經由他自己的事務所核准的。他意識到，遇上夠強烈的暴風時，這棟大樓很可能會倒塌。\n\n勒梅蘇里爾此時面臨一個很少有專業人士必須面對的抉擇。他可以保持沉默，祈禱暴風永遠不會來；也可以公開這個瑕疵，即使這可能毀掉他的名聲，並招來鉅額的訴訟。他選擇告知大樓的業主，而令他鬆一口氣的是，業主的回應並非指責，而是一套計畫。焊接工人夜復一夜地用厚重的鋼板補強接頭，而每天早上進來上班的員工們卻渾然不覺。官員們也悄悄擬定計畫，萬一颶風來襲，就疏散周邊的街區。\n\n果然有颶風逼近。九月初，艾拉颶風正在大西洋沿岸外海翻騰北上，而補強工程只完成了一部分。接著，幸好這個颶風轉了向，朝外海而去。到了十月，工程全部完成，大樓經評估已屬安全。值得注意的是，大眾當時幾乎一無所知；一場正在進行的報業罷工，使這件事沒有見報。\n\n直到 1995 年，一本雜誌刊出詳盡的報導，整件事的來龍去脈才為人所知。勒梅蘇里爾非但沒有因此名譽掃地，聲望反而更高，這段經過如今也成了工程倫理課程的必備教材。它的教訓雖然令人不太自在，卻很簡單：真正專業的人，不在於從不犯錯，而在於有勇氣在錯誤奪走人命之前坦白承認。", "blanks": [{"options": ["counterfeit", "countermand", "counteract", "countersign"], "answer": 2, "explain": "counteract 意為「抵銷、對抗（某種作用）」；大樓頂部那個可滑動的巨大重物，是用來抵銷強風造成的搖晃，故選 counteract。counterfeit 意為「偽造（貨幣、文件）」；countermand 意為「撤銷（命令）」；countersign 意為「會簽、副署」，三者雖然都以 counter- 開頭，卻都不能接 sway 表示「減輕搖晃」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["well", "as well", "rather", "sooner"], "answer": 0, "explain": "might well + 原形動詞 意為「很可能會…」，是語氣委婉但相當肯定的推測，符合勒梅蘇里爾發現支撐受力大增、接頭又改用螺栓後，判斷大樓在強烈暴風中很可能倒塌的情境。might as well 意為「不妨、倒不如」，表示建議或無可奈何的選擇，用在此處語意荒謬；rather 與 sooner 表「寧願」時須用 would rather、would sooner，might rather 與 might sooner 都不是正確的搭配。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["threatened", "threatens", "to threaten", "threaten"], "answer": 3, "explain": "此句為省略 if 的條件句倒裝：should a hurricane threaten 等於 if a hurricane should threaten「萬一颶風來襲」，用於對未來可能性較低的情況所做的假設；倒裝後 should 之後須接原形動詞，故選 threaten。threatened 為過去式或過去分詞，threatens 為第三人稱單數現在式，to threaten 為不定詞，皆不能接在 should 之後。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["mercilessly", "mercifully", "regrettably", "unwittingly"], "answer": 1, "explain": "mercifully 在此為修飾整句的副詞，意為「幸好、謝天謝地」；颶風在補強工程只完成一部分時轉向出海，對所有人而言都是幸事，下一句也說工程隨後順利完成。mercilessly「無情地」語意不合；regrettably「遺憾地」與颶風轉向的好消息相反；unwittingly「不知不覺地、無意中」用於有意識的行為者，無法表達此處慶幸的語氣。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["disgraced", "exonerated", "lionized", "commended"], "answer": 0, "explain": "Far from + V-ing 意為「非但沒有…，反而…」，空格的內容必須與後文 saw his standing enhanced「聲望反而提高」形成對比，應選 disgraced「名譽掃地的」。exonerated「被證明無罪的」、lionized「被捧為名人的」與 commended「受到表揚的」都是正面的結果，與「聲望提高」方向一致，無法形成 Far from 所要求的對比。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "C72", "title": "More Than a Lamp: Florence Nightingale and the Power of Numbers", "title_zh": "不只是一盞燈：南丁格爾與數字的力量", "level": "C2", "topic": "history of statistics", "passage": "For most people, the name Florence Nightingale __1__ a gentle figure moving through a darkened hospital ward, lamp in hand, comforting wounded soldiers. The image, popularized by newspapers and poets in the 1850s, is not false. But it captures only part of her achievement, and arguably not the most important part. Nightingale was also a gifted statistician who used numbers as a weapon in a long campaign for reform.\n\nWhen she arrived at the British army hospital at Scutari, near Constantinople, in November 1854, during the Crimean War, she found conditions that defied belief. The wards were overcrowded, the drains were blocked, and supplies of everything from soap to bandages were __2__ inadequate. Soldiers were dying in alarming numbers, yet far more of them were killed by diseases such as cholera, typhus and dysentery than by wounds received in battle. Only after a government commission arrived in the spring of 1855 to flush out the sewers and improve ventilation did the death rate begin to fall sharply.\n\nThe experience changed her. After the war, working closely with the statistician William Farr, she analyzed army records in painstaking detail. The figures pointed to a painful conclusion: most of the deaths had been preventable, and the hospitals themselves, perhaps even her own, had been part of the problem. Nightingale was determined that this lesson should not be __3__ the officials responsible, many of whom were in no hurry to admit failure.\n\nKnowing that busy ministers would never wade through tables of figures, she presented her findings in striking circular diagrams. Each was divided into twelve wedges, one for each month, with colored areas showing how many soldiers had died from preventable disease, from wounds and from other causes. At a glance, the reader could see vast blue wedges of avoidable death __4__ the slivers of red that represented battle wounds. The charts were as much a means of persuasion as of analysis.\n\nThey also worked. Her arguments helped bring about sweeping reforms in army medicine and hospital design, and in 1858 she became the first woman elected to the Statistical Society of London. Yet popular memory of her has remained stubbornly sentimental. It is perhaps the final irony of her life that a woman who trusted evidence over feeling should be remembered __5__ for a lamp.", "passage_zh": "對大多數人來說，佛羅倫斯．南丁格爾這個名字讓人聯想到的，是一個手提油燈、穿梭在昏暗病房中安慰傷兵的溫柔身影。這個形象在 1850 年代經由報紙與詩人而廣為流傳，並非虛構。但它只呈現了她成就的一部分，而且可以說不是最重要的部分。南丁格爾同時也是一位才華洋溢的統計學家，她把數字當作武器，投入一場長期的改革運動。\n\n1854 年 11 月，正值克里米亞戰爭期間，她抵達君士坦丁堡附近斯庫塔里的英軍醫院時，眼前的景況令人難以置信。病房過度擁擠，排水道堵塞，從肥皂到繃帶，各種物資都嚴重不足。士兵死亡的人數多得驚人，然而死於霍亂、斑疹傷寒、痢疾等疾病的人，遠比死於戰傷的人多。直到 1855 年春天，政府派出的委員會抵達，清理了下水道並改善通風之後，死亡率才開始大幅下降。\n\n這段經歷改變了她。戰後，她與統計學家威廉．法爾密切合作，鉅細靡遺地分析軍方的紀錄。這些數據指向一個令人痛心的結論：大部分的死亡原本都可以避免，而醫院本身——或許甚至包括她自己的醫院——正是問題的一部分。南丁格爾下定決心，絕不讓相關官員忽略這個教訓，而這些官員當中，有許多人並不急著承認失敗。\n\n她知道忙碌的大臣們絕不會耐著性子細讀一張張數字表格，於是用醒目的圓形圖表來呈現她的發現。每張圖分成十二個扇形，每個扇形代表一個月，並以不同顏色的面積，顯示有多少士兵死於可預防的疾病、死於戰傷，以及死於其他原因。讀者一眼就能看出，代表可避免死亡的巨大藍色扇形，讓代表戰傷的細長紅色區塊相形見絀。這些圖表既是分析的工具，同樣也是說服的手段。\n\n而且這些圖表確實奏效了。她的論證促成了軍隊醫療與醫院設計的全面改革；1858 年，她更成為第一位獲選加入倫敦統計學會的女性。然而，大眾對她的記憶始終停留在溫情的形象。一位重視證據勝過感情的女性，最後卻主要因為一盞燈而被人記住，這或許是她一生中最後的一個諷刺。", "blanks": [{"options": ["drums up", "conjures up", "rakes up", "plays up"], "answer": 1, "explain": "conjure up 意為「使人聯想起、喚起（畫面或回憶）」，the name conjures up a figure 即「這個名字讓人想起一個身影」。drum up 意為「招攬（生意、支持）」；rake up 意為「重提（不愉快的往事）」；play up 意為「誇大、強調」，皆無法表達「讓人聯想起某個形象」。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["willfully", "wistfully", "warily", "woefully"], "answer": 3, "explain": "woefully inadequate 為常見搭配，意為「嚴重不足、少得可憐」，符合病房擁擠、排水道堵塞，從肥皂到繃帶各種物資都缺乏的情況。willfully「故意地」不能形容物資的多寡；wistfully「惆悵地、渴望地」用於人的神情；warily「小心提防地」也只能形容人的態度，皆不合文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["lost on", "lost to", "lost in", "lost for"], "answer": 0, "explain": "be lost on sb 為慣用語，意為「（道理、笑話等）未被某人理解或重視」，this lesson should not be lost on the officials 即「這個教訓不應被官員們忽略」，與後文官員們不急於承認失敗相呼應。be lost to sb 意為「（某人）已失去…」；be lost in 意為「沉浸於…；迷失在…」；be lost for words 意為「（一時）說不出話」，皆不合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["dwindling", "dwelling", "dwarfing", "dawdling"], "answer": 2, "explain": "dwarf 作動詞時意為「使相形見絀、使顯得矮小」；此處為 see + 受詞 + V-ing 的句型，表示讀者一眼就能看到代表可避免死亡的巨大藍色扇形，讓代表戰傷的細長紅色區塊相形見絀。dwindle「逐漸減少」為不及物動詞，不能接受詞；dwell「居住；（on）老是想著」不合文意；dawdle「閒晃、磨蹭」語意不通。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["preemptively", "presumptuously", "precariously", "predominantly"], "answer": 3, "explain": "predominantly 意為「主要地、大多」，remembered predominantly for a lamp 即「人們主要因為一盞燈而記得她」，呼應前句大眾對她的記憶始終停留在溫情形象，也點出「重視證據的人卻因一盞燈而被記住」的諷刺。preemptively「先發制人地」、presumptuously「自以為是地、冒昧地」、precariously「搖搖欲墜地、不穩地」皆不合文意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "C73", "title": "An Egg in Disguise: The Cuckoo's Evolutionary Arms Race", "title_zh": "偽裝的蛋：杜鵑的演化軍備競賽", "level": "C2", "topic": "animal behavior and evolution", "passage": "Of all the strategies that animals use to survive, few are as audacious as that of the common cuckoo. Found across much of Europe and Asia, it never builds a nest or feeds its own young. Instead, the female watches for a chance to slip into the nest of another species, such as a reed warbler, while the owners are away. In a matter of seconds, she removes one of their eggs and lays one of her own in its place, leaving the __1__ hosts to raise a chick that is not theirs.\n\nWhat follows is ruthless. The cuckoo chick usually hatches before the host's own eggs, and within a day or so, though still blind and featherless, it heaves them one by one over the rim of the nest, balancing each in a small hollow in its back. From then on it has its foster parents' undivided attention. Its rapid begging call sounds __2__ like a whole brood of hungry chicks, spurring the hosts to bring food at a rate no single chick of their own could command. By the time it leaves the nest, it may be several times their size.\n\nNot surprisingly, many hosts have evolved defenses. Reed warblers mob adult cuckoos that come too close, and many birds can recognize eggs that differ from their own and either throw them out or desert the nest. The cuckoos, in turn, have evolved eggs that closely __3__ those of their hosts. Remarkably, different female cuckoos specialize in different host species, each typically laying eggs that match the colors and markings of its chosen victims. Biologists call this escalating contest of trick and counter-trick an evolutionary arms race.\n\nNot every host has joined the fight, however. Dunnocks, small brown birds common in British gardens, will __4__ accept cuckoo eggs that look nothing like their own, perhaps because they have only recently become targets and have yet to evolve defenses. More puzzling is why even the most discriminating hosts almost never reject the cuckoo chick itself. One theory holds that learning what one's chicks look like is risky: a bird whose first brood happened to be parasitized might learn the wrong image and reject its own offspring ever after.\n\nThe cuckoo has long been a byword for trickery, and in English its name has even come to mean \"crazy.\" Yet there is nothing mad about its strategy, which is a finely __5__ product of evolution, shaped at every step by the hosts it exploits.", "passage_zh": "在動物用來求生的各種策略中，很少有像大杜鵑那樣大膽的。大杜鵑分布於歐洲與亞洲的大部分地區，牠從不築巢，也不餵養自己的幼雛。相反地，雌鳥會伺機趁其他鳥類（例如葦鶯）不在時，溜進牠們的巢。短短幾秒鐘內，牠就移走巢主的一顆蛋，並在原處產下一顆自己的蛋，留下毫不知情的宿主，去撫養一隻不屬於自己的雛鳥。\n\n接下來的過程相當冷酷。杜鵑雛鳥通常比宿主自己的蛋更早孵化，而且大約一天之內，儘管眼睛還沒睜開、身上也還沒長羽毛，牠就會把那些蛋一顆一顆頂出巢緣，每次都把蛋擱在背上一處小凹陷裡保持平衡。從此以後，養父母便全心全意只照顧牠一個。牠急促的乞食叫聲，聽起來出奇地像一整窩飢餓的雛鳥，促使宿主以自己任何一隻雛鳥都不可能換來的頻率送來食物。等到牠離巢時，體型可能已經是養父母的好幾倍。\n\n不難想見，許多宿主已經演化出防禦的方法。葦鶯會群起圍攻靠得太近的成年杜鵑，而許多鳥類也能辨認出與自己的蛋不同的蛋，然後把這些蛋丟出巢外，或是棄巢而去。杜鵑則演化出與宿主的蛋極為相似的蛋作為回應。值得注意的是，不同的雌杜鵑各自專門寄生在不同種類的宿主身上，每一隻所產的蛋，通常都符合其目標宿主蛋的顏色和斑紋。生物學家把這種你出一招、我拆一招，不斷升級的較量，稱為演化上的軍備競賽。\n\n不過，並不是每一種宿主都加入了這場戰鬥。林岩鷚是英國庭園中常見的褐色小鳥，牠們會毫不猶豫地接受外觀和自己的蛋完全不同的杜鵑蛋，這或許是因為牠們最近才成為杜鵑的目標，還沒演化出防禦方法。更令人費解的是，即使是最善於辨識的宿主，也幾乎從不排斥杜鵑雛鳥本身。有一種理論認為，學習記住自己雛鳥的長相是有風險的：如果一隻鳥的第一窩雛鳥碰巧就被寄生，牠可能會記錯樣子，從此以後反而排斥自己真正的後代。\n\n長久以來，杜鵑一直是欺騙的代名詞；在英文裡，cuckoo 這個字甚至有「瘋狂」的意思。然而，牠的策略一點也不瘋狂，而是演化精心磨練出來的成果，每一步都受到牠所利用的宿主所形塑。", "blanks": [{"options": ["unwieldy", "unwonted", "unwarranted", "unwitting"], "answer": 3, "explain": "unwitting 意為「不知情的、被蒙在鼓裡的」；宿主並不知道巢裡的蛋已被調包，仍替杜鵑撫養雛鳥，故選 unwitting。unwieldy 意為「笨重而不便操作的」；unwonted 意為「不尋常的」；unwarranted 意為「沒有正當理由的、不必要的」，皆無法說明宿主受騙的處境。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["unceremoniously", "uncannily", "uncharitably", "unconscionably"], "answer": 1, "explain": "uncannily 意為「出奇地、不可思議地」，sound uncannily like 即「聽起來出奇地像…」，說明一隻杜鵑雛鳥的乞食叫聲，竟像一整窩飢餓雛鳥的叫聲。unceremoniously 意為「毫不客氣地、粗魯地」，可以形容把蛋推出巢外的動作，卻不能修飾 sounds like；uncharitably 意為「刻薄地」；unconscionably 意為「過分地、違背良心地」，皆不合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["mock", "mime", "mimic", "mince"], "answer": 2, "explain": "mimic 意為「模仿、酷似」，eggs that closely mimic those of their hosts 指杜鵑演化出與宿主十分相像的蛋，以騙過會辨識異蛋的宿主；下一句也說各種杜鵑所產的蛋，通常都符合其宿主蛋的顏色和斑紋。mock 意為「嘲弄」，即使作「模仿」解，也是帶有取笑意味的模仿，不能用來描述蛋的外觀相似；mime 意為「以默劇動作表演」；mince 意為「切碎；（說話）拐彎抹角」，皆不合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["readily", "rarely", "vehemently", "ostensibly"], "answer": 0, "explain": "readily 意為「欣然地、毫不猶豫地」；本段以 Not every host has joined the fight 開頭，說明林岩鷚並未發展出防禦，連外觀完全不同的杜鵑蛋都照單全收。rarely「很少」與本段文意相反；vehemently「激烈地」通常修飾 deny、oppose 等動詞，不與 accept 搭配；ostensibly「表面上」暗示並非真心接受，與「還沒演化出防禦」的說明矛盾。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["hoarded", "hemmed", "honed", "heeded"], "answer": 2, "explain": "finely honed 為常見搭配，意為「經過精心磨練的、精益求精的」，hone 原指「磨（刀）」；此處說杜鵑的策略是演化長期磨練出來的精巧成果，並非瘋狂之舉。hoard 意為「囤積」；hem 意為「給（衣服）縫邊」，hem in 指「包圍、限制」；heed 意為「留意、聽從（勸告）」，皆不能與 finely 搭配，形容演化的成果。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "C74", "title": "Why the Ones Come First: Benford's Law and the Hunt for Fake Figures", "title_zh": "為什麼 1 總是打頭陣：班佛定律與揪出假數字", "level": "C2", "topic": "mathematics and fraud detection", "passage": "In 1881, the astronomer Simon Newcomb noticed something odd about the books of logarithm tables that scientists then relied on for calculation. The early pages, covering numbers that begin with 1, were far grubbier and more worn than the later ones. He concluded that numbers starting with 1 must turn up more often in real-world calculations, and he even proposed a formula to describe the pattern. His observation, however, went largely __1__.\n\nMore than half a century later, in 1938, the physicist Frank Benford independently rediscovered the same pattern and tested it on some 20,000 numbers drawn from sources as varied as the areas of rivers, city populations and physical constants. Time and again, the digit 1 led the way. In data of this kind, roughly 30 percent of numbers begin with 1 and about 18 percent with 2, while fewer than 5 percent begin with 9. The phenomenon has borne Benford's name __2__, although Newcomb had described it first.\n\nWhy should this be so? The key is that many quantities grow by multiplication rather than addition. Consider a town of 1,000 people whose population rises by a steady percentage each year. It must double before its first digit changes from 1 to 2, yet it needs to grow by only about 11 percent to get from 9,000 to 10,000, at which point it begins with 1 once more. Quantities that __3__ in this way spend far longer beginning with small digits than with large ones. The pattern holds whatever the units, too: a company's figures obey it equally well whether they are recorded in dollars or in yen.\n\nBenford's law is more than a mathematical curiosity. Because people who make up numbers tend to spread the first digits far too evenly, a set of accounts that departs sharply from the expected pattern can be a sign that something is __4__. Auditors and tax investigators now use the law as a screening tool, flagging suspicious records for closer examination.\n\nStill, the law must be applied with care. It works only for data that span several orders of magnitude and arise naturally; it has nothing useful to say about adult heights, telephone numbers or prices deliberately set at $9.99. Nor does a mismatch prove wrongdoing: an honest company whose business is unusual may fail the test through no __5__ of its own. Like many statistical tools, Benford's law is best treated as a prompt for questions rather than a source of answers.", "passage_zh": "1881 年，天文學家西蒙．紐康注意到，科學家當時用來計算的對數表書籍有個奇怪的現象：前面幾頁，也就是以 1 開頭的數字所在的頁面，比後面的頁面髒汙、磨損得多。他由此推斷，在現實世界的計算中，以 1 開頭的數字一定出現得比較頻繁，甚至還提出了一個公式來描述這個規律。然而，他的觀察大致上並未受到重視。\n\n半個多世紀之後，物理學家法蘭克．班佛在 1938 年獨立地重新發現了相同的規律，並用約兩萬個數字加以檢驗，這些數字的來源五花八門，從河流的面積、城市的人口到物理常數都有。結果一次又一次，都是數字 1 遙遙領先。在這類數據中，大約 30% 的數字以 1 開頭，約 18% 以 2 開頭，而以 9 開頭的則不到 5%。從那時起，這個現象就一直冠上班佛的名字，儘管最先描述它的其實是紐康。\n\n為什麼會這樣呢？關鍵在於，許多數量是以乘法而非加法的方式成長。假設有一個 1,000 人的小鎮，人口每年以固定的百分比增加。它的人口必須加倍，首位數字才會從 1 變成 2；然而從 9,000 人增加到 10,000 人，只需要成長約 11%，而到了那時，首位數字又再度變回 1。以這種方式累積成長的數量，首位是小數字的時間，遠比首位是大數字的時間長。而且不論使用什麼單位，這個規律都成立：一家公司的數字無論以美元還是日圓記錄，都同樣符合這個規律。\n\n班佛定律不只是數學上的奇特現象。由於捏造數字的人往往會讓首位數字分布得過於平均，因此一套帳目如果大幅偏離預期的分布，就可能代表其中有些不對勁。如今，審計人員與稅務調查人員都把這個定律當作篩檢工具，標出可疑的紀錄，以便進一步檢查。\n\n不過，這個定律必須審慎使用。它只適用於跨越好幾個數量級、而且是自然產生的數據；對於成年人的身高、電話號碼，或是刻意訂為 9.99 美元的價格，它提供不了任何有用的資訊。分布不符也不能證明有不法行為：一家業務性質特殊的誠實公司，可能並非自己的過錯，卻無法通過這項檢驗。就像許多統計工具一樣，班佛定律最好被視為引發提問的線索，而不是答案的來源。", "blanks": [{"options": ["unheeded", "unbidden", "unabated", "unrequited"], "answer": 0, "explain": "go unheeded 為常見搭配，意為「未受重視、被置之不理」，與下一段所說直到半個多世紀後，班佛才重新發現這個現象相呼應。unbidden 意為「不請自來的、自發的」，多用於 come unbidden；unabated 意為「（暴風、爭議等）有增無減的、未見緩和的」，多用於 continue unabated；unrequited 多指「（愛情）得不到回報的」，皆不合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["henceforth", "thereupon", "ever since", "forthwith"], "answer": 2, "explain": "ever since 意為「從那時起一直」，常與現在完成式連用；has borne Benford's name ever since 即「此後一直冠上班佛的名字」。henceforth 意為「從今以後」，指未來，不能與現在完成式連用；thereupon「隨即、於是」與 forthwith「立刻」都表示某個時間點之後馬上發生的事，無法表達「持續至今」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["confound", "compound", "compute", "comply"], "answer": 1, "explain": "compound 作不及物動詞時意為「以複利方式增長、層層累積」，呼應前文所說許多數量是以乘法而非加法成長，例如人口每年按固定比例增加。confound 意為「使困惑；使驚訝」；compute 意為「計算」，主詞通常是人或電腦；comply 意為「遵守（with）」，皆不合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["abreast", "aloof", "akin", "amiss"], "answer": 3, "explain": "something is amiss 為常見說法，意為「有些不對勁、出了問題」；帳目首位數字的分布若大幅偏離預期，可能代表數字是捏造的，故選 amiss。abreast 意為「並排地；（of）跟上最新情況」；aloof 意為「冷漠的、疏離的」；akin 意為「類似的」，須接 to，皆不合文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["blame", "fault", "guilt", "liability"], "answer": 1, "explain": "through no fault of one's own 為固定慣用語，意為「並非自己的過錯」；一家誠實但業務性質特殊的公司，即使沒有做假帳，也可能無法通過這項檢驗。blame「責怪」、guilt「罪責、內疚」與 liability「（法律上的）責任」雖然語意相關，卻都不能用於這個固定說法。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "C75", "title": "Paying to Be Seen: Thorstein Veblen and Conspicuous Consumption", "title_zh": "花錢給人看：韋伯倫與炫耀性消費", "level": "C2", "topic": "economics and society", "passage": "In 1899, an American economist named Thorstein Veblen published a book with the sober title The Theory of the Leisure Class. Its subject was the behavior of the newly rich in an age of mansions, private railroad cars and lavish balls, and its tone was one of cool, almost anthropological detachment, as though the author were describing the rituals of a remote tribe. Beneath the dry prose lay a __1__ wit that readers still relish today.\n\nVeblen's central claim was that much spending has little to do with usefulness. The wealthy, he argued, buy expensive things not merely to enjoy them but to display their ability to pay, and thereby to __2__ their standing in the eyes of others. He called this \"conspicuous consumption,\" a phrase that has since passed into everyday English. By the same logic, idleness itself could serve as a badge of rank: a life visibly free from productive work proved that one had no need to earn a living.\n\nThe idea has an intriguing implication. Normally, demand for a product falls as its price rises. For goods whose chief appeal is their exclusivity, the reverse may __3__ true, since the cost is precisely what is being shown off. Economists later named such products \"Veblen goods\" in his honor. Luxury handbags and watches are often cited as examples, and their makers tend to guard their prices jealously. In 2018, one British fashion house disclosed that it had destroyed unsold goods worth millions of pounds, reportedly to keep them from being sold cheaply, and abandoned the practice only after a public outcry.\n\nVeblen's theory is not without its __4__. Critics point out that people buy expensive things for many reasons, including quality and durability, and that showing off is only one motive among several. The theory is also harder to apply in an age when the very rich often dress down, and some scholars argue that status is now signaled less by diamonds than by things that are costly yet discreet, such as an elite education or a carefully chosen diet.\n\nYet the core insight has aged remarkably well. In a world of social media, where holidays, meals and purchases are photographed and shared for an audience, Veblen's observations seem, if anything, more __5__ now than when he made them. He would hardly have been surprised, though he might have permitted himself a wry smile.", "passage_zh": "1899 年，一位名叫托斯丹．韋伯倫的美國經濟學家，出版了一本書名平實的著作——《有閒階級論》。這本書的主題是新富階級的行為，當時正是豪宅、私人鐵路車廂與奢華舞會盛行的年代；而書中的語調冷靜疏離，幾乎像人類學研究一般，彷彿作者是在描述某個遙遠部落的儀式。在平淡的文字底下，蘊藏著帶有嘲諷意味的機智，至今仍令讀者津津樂道。\n\n韋伯倫的核心論點是：許多消費與實用性幾乎毫無關係。他主張，富人購買昂貴的東西，不只是為了享受，更是為了展示自己的支付能力，藉此提升自己在他人眼中的地位。他把這種行為稱為「炫耀性消費」，這個詞後來也成了英文的日常用語。依照同樣的邏輯，閒散本身也可以成為身分地位的象徵：一種顯然不必從事生產性工作的生活，就證明了這個人不需要謀生。\n\n這個想法帶出一個耐人尋味的推論。一般而言，產品的價格上漲時，需求就會下降。但對於主要吸引力在於稀有獨特的商品來說，情況可能正好相反，因為被拿來炫耀的，正是它的價格。經濟學家後來為了紀念他，把這類商品命名為「韋伯倫財」。名牌包和名錶經常被舉為例子，而製造這些商品的廠商往往極力維護它們的價格。2018 年，一家英國時尚品牌揭露，該公司銷毀了價值數百萬英鎊的滯銷商品，據報導是為了避免這些商品被低價出售；直到引發輿論強烈抗議，才停止這種做法。\n\n韋伯倫的理論並非沒有批評者。批評者指出，人們購買昂貴的東西有許多原因，包括品質與耐用性，而炫耀只是眾多動機之一。在一個巨富往往刻意穿著樸素的時代，這個理論也比較難以套用；有些學者主張，如今彰顯地位的，與其說是鑽石，不如說是那些昂貴卻低調的事物，例如菁英教育，或是精心挑選的飲食。\n\n然而，這個理論的核心洞見歷久彌新。在社群媒體的世界裡，旅遊、餐點與購物都被拍下來分享給眾人觀看，韋伯倫的觀察如今看來，甚至比他當年提出時更切合現實。他大概不會感到意外，不過或許會容許自己露出一絲苦笑。", "blanks": [{"options": ["sardonic", "saccharine", "sanguine", "sanctimonious"], "answer": 0, "explain": "sardonic 意為「帶著嘲諷的、挖苦的」，a sardonic wit 即「帶有嘲諷意味的機智」，與前文作者以冷靜疏離、彷彿描述遙遠部落儀式的口吻來寫富人相呼應。saccharine 意為「甜得膩人的、過分感傷的」；sanguine 意為「樂觀的」；sanctimonious 意為「假裝虔誠的、自以為道德高尚的」，皆無法說明這種冷嘲式的幽默。故選 (A)。"}, {"options": ["bluster", "blister", "bolster", "bloat"], "answer": 2, "explain": "bolster 意為「鞏固、提升」，bolster one's standing 即「提高自己的地位」，符合富人藉由展示支付能力，來提升自己在他人眼中地位的說法。bluster 意為「大聲威嚇、咆哮」，多作不及物動詞；blister 意為「使起水泡」；bloat 意為「使膨脹、使臃腫」，皆不能與 standing 搭配表示「提升地位」。故選 (C)。"}, {"options": ["ring", "hold", "come", "stand"], "answer": 1, "explain": "hold true 為常見搭配，意為「（規律、說法）適用、成立」；the reverse may hold true 指「相反的情況可能成立」，也就是價格越高，需求反而越大。ring true 意為「聽起來可信」，是對說法真假的感受，而非規律是否適用；come true 意為「（願望、預言）實現」；stand 不與 true 搭配表示「成立」。故選 (B)。"}, {"options": ["devotees", "adherents", "proponents", "detractors"], "answer": 3, "explain": "be not without one's detractors 意為「並非沒有批評者、仍有人批評」；下一句以 Critics point out 開頭，列出批評者的看法，故選 detractors「貶低者、批評者」。devotees「熱衷者、信徒」、adherents「擁護者、追隨者」與 proponents「支持者、提倡者」都是支持這個理論的人，與下文的批評無法銜接。故選 (D)。"}, {"options": ["pertinacious", "perfunctory", "pertinent", "impertinent"], "answer": 2, "explain": "pertinent 意為「切題的、相關的、適用的」；在社群媒體時代，人們把旅遊、餐點與購物拍照分享給別人看，韋伯倫的觀察顯得比當年更切合現況。pertinacious 意為「固執的、堅持不懈的」；perfunctory 意為「敷衍了事的」；impertinent 現多指「無禮的、冒失的」，其古義「不相干的」又與文意相反；三者字形雖然相近，皆不合文意。故選 (C)。"}]}];
